const storyData = {
  start: {
    text: "The sky is wrong.\n\nThat's the first thing you notice when you wake up in the attic of the Mystery Shack \u2014 the sky outside the triangular window isn't blue anymore. It's a sickly, pulsing red, shot through with cracks of blinding yellow light like the whole atmosphere is a shattered windshield. Through those cracks, you can see things that shouldn't exist: geometric shapes that move like living creatures, swirling vortexes of color that hurt your eyes, and everywhere \u2014 EVERYWHERE \u2014 enormous floating eyeballs with bat wings, circling like vultures over what used to be the peaceful town of Gravity Falls, Oregon.\n\nWeirdmageddon. Bill Cipher won.\n\nDipper Pines sits up in bed, his heart hammering so hard he can feel it in his teeth. Beside him, Mabel is already awake, clutching Waddles to her chest. The pig is trembling. Mabel's sweater \u2014 today's features a shooting star made of actual glitter \u2014 is rumpled from sleeping in it. Her eyes are wide, but there's something fierce in them too. Something that says she's scared but she's not giving up. Not yet. Not ever, if Mabel has anything to say about it.\n\n\"It's been three days,\" Dipper says quietly, pulling Journal 3 from under his pillow where he's kept it every night since the world ended. \"Three days since Bill broke through the rift and started all... this.\"\n\nHe gestures at the window. Outside, the Fearamid \u2014 Bill's massive floating pyramid of nightmare brick and compressed screaming \u2014 hovers above the town like a mountain that forgot which direction was up. Lightning arcs between its surface and the shattered sky. Even from here, half a mile away, they can hear Bill's laughter echoing across the landscape. It's the kind of laugh that makes you feel small and cold and very, very mortal.\n\nThe Mystery Shack shudders. It's been doing that a lot lately \u2014 Weirdmageddon sends tremors through the ground that have nothing to do with earthquakes and everything to do with reality itself coming apart at the seams. The walls groan. A crack runs up the ceiling that wasn't there yesterday. Downstairs, something heavy crashes and Grunkle Stan's voice rises in a string of words that Dipper is pretty sure he's not supposed to know yet.\n\n\"We can't just stay here,\" Mabel says. It's not a question. They've had this conversation every morning for three days, and every morning the answer gets more urgent. The Shack's defenses won't hold forever. Ford's emergency barrier is flickering. Supplies are running low. And out there, in the chaos of Weirdmageddon, people are suffering. Their friends. Their family. The whole town.\n\nDipper opens Journal 3 to a page he's been studying all night, the pages illuminated by the hellish red light from outside. Ford's handwriting fills the margins with notes about Bill Cipher, about the Fearamid, about something called the Cipher Wheel, and \u2014 in the deepest, most hidden layer of invisible ink \u2014 about a being called the Axolotl. A cosmic entity. Something even Bill is afraid of.\n\n\"I've been thinking about our options,\" Dipper says, spreading the journal across his knees. \"There are four things we can do. Four paths. And I think... I think whichever one we pick is going to determine whether we save Gravity Falls or lose it forever.\"\n\nMabel sets Waddles down gently and scoots closer to look at the journal. \"Okay, bro-bro. Hit me. What are our options?\"\n\nDipper takes a deep breath. \"Option one: we run. Ford built an emergency bunker in the forest \u2014 his old research station. It's underground, it's fortified, and it might be the safest place left in Gravity Falls. We could hide there, regroup, figure out our next move.\"\n\n\"Option two: we fight. The Mystery Shack still has people in it \u2014 Stan, Soos, Wendy, some of the townsfolk. If we can rally everyone together, turn this place into a real resistance headquarters, we might be able to push back against Bill's forces.\"\n\n\"Option three: we go straight to the source. The Fearamid. Bill's fortress. It's insanely dangerous, but if there's a weakness \u2014 if the Cipher Wheel prophecy is real \u2014 we might be able to end this by confronting Bill directly.\"\n\n\"And option four...\" Dipper's finger traces the hidden ink on the journal page. \"The Axolotl. Ford wrote about it in secret, like he was afraid even writing its name where Bill might see. It's some kind of cosmic being \u2014 ancient, powerful, existing outside of normal reality. If we can find it, it might be the only thing in the universe that can actually undo what Bill has done.\"\n\nMabel is quiet for a moment, staring at the journal. Outside, an eye-bat shrieks past the window, its single enormous pupil swiveling toward them before it moves on. The Fearamid pulses with sickly light. The ground trembles.\n\n\"Four paths,\" Mabel says finally. \"And we pick one. Right now.\"\n\n\"Right now,\" Dipper confirms.\n\nMabel pulls her sweater sleeves over her hands \u2014 her comfort gesture, the thing she does when she's steeling herself for something big. She looks at her brother. He looks at her. They've faced gnomes, lake monsters, time travelers, dream demons, and the literal end of the world. Whatever they choose next, they'll face it together.\n\n\"So what's it gonna be, Dipper? What's our move?\"",
    choices: [
      { label: "Flee to the Author's bunker in the forest", target: "bunker_entrance" },
      { label: "Rally the resistance at the Mystery Shack", target: "resistance_rally" },
      { label: "Confront Bill at the Fearamid", target: "fearamid_approach" },
      { label: "Search for the Axolotl", target: "axolotl_search" }
    ]
  },

  // ===== BUNKER BRANCH =====
  bunker_entrance: {
    text: "The forest is on fire. Not regular fire, either -- this is Weirdmageddon fire, flames that burn in colors that shouldn't exist. Purple. Screaming yellow. A shade of green that makes your teeth hurt just looking at it. The trees aren't just burning, they're laughing, their bark splitting into horrible grinning faces as Bill Cipher's chaos magic twists everything it touches.\n\n\"DIPPER, MOVE!\" Mabel screams, yanking her brother sideways as an eye-bat swoops down from the fractured sky. The creature's massive single eye locks onto them, its leathery wings sending waves of hot, rotten-smelling air across their faces. Dipper stumbles over a root -- or what used to be a root. It's squirming now, trying to grab his ankle.\n\nThey've been running for twenty minutes straight, ever since the Mystery Shack's protective barrier flickered and died. The sky above Gravity Falls is torn wide open like a wound, and through the gap, you can see dimensions that human eyes were never meant to process. Geometric shapes that are also alive. Colors that whisper. Bill Cipher's Fearamid looms over everything, a nightmare made of brick and screaming.\n\n\"There!\" Dipper points through the smoke. The old pine tree -- the one with the metal hatch hidden beneath its roots. The entrance to the Author's bunker. Ford's secret underground laboratory, built decades ago to prepare for exactly this kind of apocalypse. Dipper grabs the hidden lever disguised as a branch and pulls. Nothing happens. He pulls harder, his sneakers skidding on the dirt. \"Come on, come on, COME ON!\"\n\nMabel fires her grappling hook at a swooping eye-bat, catching it right in its enormous pupil. The creature shrieks and spirals away, trailing a stream of purple goo. \"Dipper, whatever you're doing, do it FASTER!\"\n\nThe hatch groans open with a rusty shriek. They tumble inside, Dipper slamming the hatch shut just as three more eye-bats crash into the metal surface from above. BANG. BANG. BANG. The sound echoes down the shaft like war drums, but the hatch holds. Ford built things to last.\n\nThey slide down the ladder into darkness, their flashlight beams cutting through dust that hasn't been disturbed in months. The bunker's main level stretches out before them -- and it's a mess. Someone, or something, has been through here. Filing cabinets are overturned, papers scattered across the floor like fallen leaves. One of Ford's old experiment tables is smashed in half. Glass crunches under their shoes.\n\n\"This doesn't look good,\" Dipper whispers, sweeping his flashlight across the wreckage. Some of the damage looks recent -- claw marks gouged into the walls, deep and savage, still fresh enough that metal shavings glitter on the floor beneath them.\n\nBut beyond the ransacked entry area, the bunker goes deeper. Much deeper. Ford designed this place with multiple levels, each one more heavily reinforced than the last. The emergency lighting still works down here, casting everything in a dim red glow that makes their shadows look ten feet tall. The air smells like old metal and something chemical -- some of Ford's preserved experiments, maybe.\n\nMabel picks up a torn page from the floor. It's covered in Ford's handwriting, cramped and urgent: \"IF BILL SUCCEEDS -- the lower levels contain everything needed for long-term survival. The cryogenic section has independent power. DO NOT trust anything that speaks with a familiar voice.\"\n\n\"Comforting,\" Mabel says, her voice cracking just slightly.\n\nThen they hear it. From below. From the deeper levels of the bunker. A sound like something heavy dragging itself across a metal floor. Scraaaape. Pause. Scraaaape. Pause. And then, faintly, what might be a voice. Or might be a growl pretending to be a voice.\n\nDipper and Mabel look at each other. Above them, the hatch shudders as the eye-bats continue their assault. There's no going back up. The only way forward is down -- or they could search this upper level first, ransacked as it is, and hope to find something useful before facing whatever is making that sound.",
    choices: [
      { label: "Descend to the lower levels", target: "bunker_descent" },
      { label: "Search the upper level for supplies first", target: "bunker_supplies" }
    ]
  },

  bunker_descent: {
    text: "They take the stairs down. Each step rings hollow under their feet, the metallic clang echoing through the bunker like a countdown. The emergency lights flicker with every distant tremor from the surface -- Weirdmageddon is shaking the very earth, and even fifty feet underground, they can feel it. Dust sifts down from the ceiling in thin streams. The walls groan.\n\n\"Ford really went all out with this place,\" Dipper mutters, running his hand along the reinforced steel walls. Rivets the size of quarters. Blast doors every thirty feet. This wasn't just a laboratory -- it was a fortress, built by a man who knew the end of the world was coming and wanted to be ready.\n\nThe stairwell opens into a wide corridor lined with heavy doors, each one labeled in Ford's precise handwriting. SPECIMEN STORAGE. DIMENSIONAL ANALYSIS LAB. WEAPONS TESTING -- DO NOT ENTER WITHOUT PROTECTIVE GOGGLES (THIS MEANS YOU, FIDDLEFORD). Mabel pauses at that last one. \"McGucket worked down here?\"\n\n\"Before he lost his mind,\" Dipper says quietly. \"Before he erased his own memories because what they were doing was too scary to remember.\"\n\nThe corridor ends at a massive reinforced door, thicker than any of the others. Dipper recognizes it immediately, and his stomach drops straight through the floor. The cryogenic chamber. The room where Ford kept his most dangerous captured specimen locked in frozen suspension. The room where, last summer, Dipper and Mabel and Wendy and Soos had the worst day of their lives.\n\nThe Shapeshifter's containment unit.\n\nThe door is slightly ajar.\n\n\"Nope,\" Mabel says immediately. \"Nope nope nope. I vote we go literally anywhere else. I'll take the eye-bats. I'll take Bill Cipher's personal karaoke night. Anything but--\"\n\n\"Mabel, look.\" Dipper pushes the door open with the tip of his flashlight, keeping his body well back.\n\nThe cryogenic chamber is bathed in stuttering blue light, the emergency power struggling to keep the systems running. The massive glass containment tube dominates the center of the room, frost still clinging to its surface in thick crystals. But the glass is cracked. Not shattered -- not yet -- but a web of fractures spreads across the tube like a frozen lightning strike. The Weirdmageddon tremors have been slowly shaking it apart.\n\nAnd something is oozing through the cracks.\n\nA thick, grayish-white substance, like living putty, is seeping through the fractures and pooling on the floor. It moves with horrible purpose, flowing uphill when it should flow down, gathering into a mass about the size of a basketball. As they watch, frozen with terror, the mass twitches. A tiny mouth forms in its surface, opens, and whispers something too quiet to hear.\n\n\"It's waking up,\" Dipper breathes. The Shapeshifter. Experiment 210. The creature that could take the form of anything it saw, that had mimicked their friends and tried to kill them, that had frozen mid-scream as a warning to anyone who might set it free. The cryo tube had held it for thirty years. But Weirdmageddon was undoing everything.\n\nThe oozing mass twitches again, larger now. A single eye opens in its surface, milky and unfocused, swiveling wildly before fixing on Dipper and Mabel. The tiny mouth moves again, and this time, they can hear it.\n\n\"Pine... Tree...\"\n\nThe cryo tube shudders. Another crack snaps across the glass with a sound like breaking bones. More of the substance begins to pour through, the mass on the floor growing, starting to take a vague shape -- something with too many limbs and not enough face.\n\nDipper's mind races. The containment machinery is right there -- banks of controls, gauges, emergency protocols labeled in Ford's handwriting. If they could reseal the tube, reinforce the glass, maybe boost the cryo power, they could put the Shapeshifter back to sleep before it fully forms. But the damage might be too severe. The machine might not have enough power.\n\nOr they could keep going deeper. Ford's bunker extends further down -- unexplored sections that even Dipper hasn't seen. There might be something useful down there. Something that could help them survive. And the further they go from the Shapeshifter, the better.\n\nThe mass on the floor sprouts three more eyes and what might be the beginning of a hand.",
    choices: [
      { label: "Try to reseal the containment unit", target: "bunker_shapeshifter" },
      { label: "Go deeper into the bunker's unexplored sections", target: "bunker_deep" }
    ]
  },

  bunker_shapeshifter: {
    text: "\"We can't leave that thing to get loose,\" Dipper says, his voice steadier than he feels. \"Last time it almost killed us. If it gets to full strength and gets out of the bunker, during Weirdmageddon? It'll be unstoppable.\"\n\nMabel swallows hard. \"Okay. Okay, fine. But if I die in a bunker fighting a goo monster, I'm haunting you FOREVER, Dipper. I will be the most annoying ghost. I will rattle chains at three AM every single night.\"\n\n\"Deal.\"\n\nDipper rushes to the control panel while Mabel keeps her grappling hook trained on the growing mass. The controls are complex -- Ford designed them for a six-fingered genius, not a twelve-year-old in a panic -- but Dipper's spent enough time studying the journals to recognize the basics. Cryo power is at eighteen percent. The backup generators are offline. The glass integrity is at critical levels. But there's an emergency reseal protocol, labeled in red marker: LAST RESORT.\n\nHe's reaching for the switch when the mass on the floor lurches upward. It grows with sickening speed, stretching and molding itself like clay on a potter's wheel being spun by a maniac. Limbs form, retract, reform. A torso assembles itself. And then a face emerges from the chaos -- freckled, friendly, topped with red hair and a green flannel cap.\n\nWendy Corduroy smiles at them.\n\n\"Hey, dudes,\" the thing wearing Wendy's face says. The voice is perfect. Every inflection, every casual syllable, exactly right. \"Man, am I glad to see you guys. I've been stuck in that tube for, like, forever. Bill's chaos must've cracked the seal.\"\n\nMabel's grappling hook wavers. \"Wendy...?\"\n\n\"DON'T,\" Dipper snaps. His heart is hammering, because part of him wants it to be Wendy so badly it physically hurts. But he remembers. He remembers last summer, in this very room, how the Shapeshifter had worn Wendy's face then too. How it had almost fooled them. Almost.\n\nDipper steps forward, putting himself between Mabel and the creature. \"If you're really Wendy,\" he says, keeping his voice level, \"then tell me something. What song was playing on the radio during our first mission together? The convenience store haunting. You changed the station and said it was the worst song ever written.\"\n\nThe Wendy-thing's smile doesn't flicker. \"Oh man, that was so long ago. It was... uh...\" The eyes -- Wendy's green eyes -- dart left. Just for a second. The tiniest hesitation. \"It was that one pop song, you know the one--\"\n\n\"It was 'Disco Girl' by BABBA,\" Dipper says flatly. \"And Wendy didn't change the station. I did. Because I was embarrassed she caught me singing along.\"\n\nThe Wendy-face freezes. For one horrible second, it's perfectly still, like a photograph. Then the smile stretches. Wider. WIDER. Far wider than any human mouth should go, the corners reaching past where the ears should be, revealing rows and rows of needle-thin teeth that spiral back into a throat that goes on forever.\n\n\"Clever boy,\" the Shapeshifter hisses, and Wendy's voice is gone, replaced by something wet and ancient and furious. \"You caught me last time too. But last time, you had an ice gun and a team. What do you have now?\"\n\nThe creature begins to grow, abandoning the Wendy form entirely. It cycles through shapes with nauseating speed -- a bear, a giant insect, something with tentacles that doesn't have a name, Ford's face twisted in a scream, Mabel's face laughing, Dipper's own face crying. It's showing off. It's trying to terrify them. And honestly? It's working.\n\n\"We have something better than a team,\" Mabel says, stepping up beside her brother and leveling her grappling hook. \"We have matching sweaters.\" She's wearing her shooting star sweater, the one she made for Weirdmageddon. Her hands are shaking. But she's standing.\n\nThe Shapeshifter roars -- a sound that comes from every form at once, layered and horrible -- and lunges.",
    choices: [
      { label: "Fight the Shapeshifter", target: "bunker_fight" },
      { label: "Try to reason with it - offer it freedom in exchange for help against Bill", target: "bunker_trust" }
    ]
  },

  bunker_fight: {
    text: "There's no time to think. The Shapeshifter launches itself at them like a freight train made of nightmares, shifting forms mid-lunge -- it starts as a massive spider, becomes a charging bull, and by the time it reaches them it's something that doesn't exist in any nature textbook, all whipping tendrils and snapping jaws.\n\n\"SCATTER!\" Dipper screams, and the twins dive in opposite directions as the creature slams into the control panel behind them. Sparks fly. Warning alarms blare. The already-damaged cryo tube groans and another crack spirals across its surface.\n\nMabel rolls behind an overturned equipment rack, her grappling hook clutched to her chest. Dipper scrambles behind a support column, his mind racing through everything he knows about Experiment 210. It can take any form it's seen. It's incredibly strong. It's fast. It's smart -- terrifyingly smart. But it has weaknesses. It takes a moment to fully stabilize a new form. And it hates the cold. That's why cryo containment worked in the first place.\n\nThe Shapeshifter rises to the ceiling, taking the form of something like a massive bat, its wingspan filling the entire chamber. It screams down at them with a sound like metal tearing. \"THIRTY YEARS IN THAT TUBE! THIRTY YEARS FROZEN AND DREAMING! DO YOU KNOW WHAT I DREAMED ABOUT, PINE TREE?\"\n\nIt drops from the ceiling, shifting into a hulking gorilla-like form, and swipes at Dipper's column. The reinforced steel BENDS. Dipper barely dodges, feeling the wind of the near miss ruffle his hair.\n\n\"I DREAMED ABOUT YOUR FACE! THE FACE OF THE BOY WHO LOCKED ME AWAY!\"\n\n\"Technically that was my Great Uncle Ford!\" Dipper shouts back, diving behind another column. \"I just helped! I was more of a supporting character in that particular--\"\n\nThe Shapeshifter smashes through the column.\n\n\"MABEL! THE COOLANT PIPES!\" Dipper points at the ceiling, where thick pipes run from the cryo unit to the cooling system. They're frosted over, still carrying cryogenic fluid. If they can break one near the Shapeshifter--\n\nMabel understands instantly. She fires her grappling hook straight up, catching one of the overhead pipes. She swings -- actually swings, like the world's bravest, most sparkly Tarzan -- across the chamber, the Shapeshifter's tentacle-arm missing her by inches. At the peak of her swing, she lets go and lands on top of the cryo machinery.\n\n\"HEY, UGLY!\" she shouts. \"BET YOU CAN'T DO THIS FORM!\" She strikes a pose -- jazz hands, one leg up, tongue out. It's the stupidest, most Mabel pose imaginable.\n\nThe Shapeshifter, driven by instinct to mimic, actually hesitates. It starts to copy her pose, its massive body contorting in ways that would be hilarious if they weren't also horrifying. And in that moment of distraction, Mabel fires her grappling hook into the main coolant pipe directly above the creature.\n\nThe pipe BURSTS. Cryogenic fluid pours down like the world's worst waterfall, engulfing the Shapeshifter in a cascade of freezing mist. The creature SCREAMS, its forms flickering wildly -- a dozen shapes a second, each one more desperate than the last. It lurches toward the twins, half-frozen, ice crystals forming across its body.\n\n\"The chamber! We need to get it back in the chamber!\" Dipper grabs a length of broken pipe and uses it to herd the stumbling, freezing creature toward the cracked containment tube. Mabel fires her grappling hook at the Shapeshifter's leg, yanking it off balance.\n\nThe creature topples backward, crashing through the broken glass of the containment tube and landing inside with a wet, heavy THUD. It's half-frozen, its movements sluggish and desperate, but the cryo machinery is damaged. The tube can't fully seal. Ice is forming across the Shapeshifter's body, but it's melting as fast as it freezes.\n\n\"The emergency freeze protocol!\" Dipper scrambles to the control panel, which is sparking and cracked from the Shapeshifter's earlier impact. Half the buttons are destroyed. But there -- a red lever behind a glass panel, labeled EMERGENCY CRYOGENIC PURGE. One pull and the entire chamber floods with cryo gas.\n\nOr they could collapse the tunnel. The support columns are already damaged. A few more hits and the whole ceiling comes down, burying the Shapeshifter under tons of rock and steel. It's cruder, but it's certain.\n\nThe Shapeshifter's eye, the one part still unfrozen, fixes on Dipper. \"You can't... keep me... forever,\" it rasps. \"When the chaos above... reaches down here... everything... thaws.\"",
    choices: [
      { label: "Use the emergency freeze protocol", target: "bunker_victory" },
      { label: "Collapse the tunnel to trap it", target: "ending_bunker_trapped" }
    ]
  },

  bunker_victory: {
    text: "Dipper slams his palm against the glass panel, shattering it, and grabs the red lever with both hands. It's stiff -- rusted, maybe, or just built to resist accidental activation. He pulls with everything he has, his sneakers skidding on the frost-slicked floor.\n\n\"MABEL! HELP!\"\n\nMabel sprints over and grabs the lever too, her small hands wrapped around it just above Dipper's. Together, the Pines twins pull. The lever groans. The Shapeshifter, still half-trapped in the broken containment tube, begins to thrash with renewed fury, sensing what's coming. It extends a tentacle toward them, stretching across the chamber, the tip morphing into a hand -- into Dipper's hand, small and desperate, reaching out as if asking for help.\n\nDipper doesn't look at it. He pulls.\n\nThe lever clicks home.\n\nEvery vent in the cryogenic chamber blasts open simultaneously, and a hurricane of cryo gas floods the room. The temperature drops so fast that frost forms on Dipper's eyelashes between one blink and the next. The air turns white. The Shapeshifter screams -- a long, descending wail that shifts through a hundred voices before finally, mercifully, going silent.\n\nWhen the fog clears, the Shapeshifter is frozen solid. Not in the tube this time -- the tube is too damaged for that -- but on the floor in front of it, caught mid-reach, its tentacle still extended toward where Dipper and Mabel were standing. It's frozen in a form that's half Dipper, half something alien, with too many eyes and a mouth that's open in a silent scream. Frost covers every surface of the creature like a crystal cocoon.\n\n\"Is it... done?\" Mabel whispers. Her breath comes out in thick clouds.\n\nDipper checks the control panel. The cryo purge drained almost everything the emergency generators had left. Power is at four percent and falling. The containment chamber's temperature will hold for maybe twelve hours before the ice starts to melt. After that, the Shapeshifter wakes up again, and this time, there won't be enough power to stop it.\n\n\"It's done for now,\" Dipper says carefully. \"But we can't stay here. The bunker's power is failing. When it goes, the Shapeshifter thaws and we're back to square one.\"\n\nMabel slumps against the wall, pulling her sweater tighter. She's shivering, and not just from the cold. \"So what do we do? We can't go back to the surface. Bill's nightmare circus is up there. We can't stay here. Everything down here is either broken or trying to eat us. What's left?\"\n\nDipper is already moving, pulling open drawers in Ford's workstation, rifling through frozen papers and scattered equipment. He finds what he's looking for wedged behind a bank of monitors -- a leather-bound notebook, smaller than the journals, filled with Ford's cramped handwriting. It's labeled BUNKER SYSTEMS - EMERGENCY PROTOCOLS.\n\nHe flips through the pages, his flashlight beam dancing across diagrams and notes. \"Mabel, look at this. Ford built more down here than we knew about. There's a whole section below us -- Level Four. He calls it the 'Last Resort Wing.' And there's...\" He stops, his eyes widening. \"There's a communication device. A dimensional radio. Ford built it to send signals across realities. If it still works, we could call for help. We could reach someone -- Ford, maybe, or--\"\n\n\"Or anyone who's listening,\" Mabel finishes. She looks at the frozen Shapeshifter, then at the failing power readings, then at her brother. \"A radio that talks to other dimensions. In a bunker under a town where reality is broken. What could possibly go wrong?\"\n\n\"Everything,\" Dipper says honestly. \"But we're out of safe options.\"\n\nHe also finds another note, this one in Ford's most urgent handwriting, the kind he used when he was scared: If all else fails, fortify Level Three. Supplies for six months. Seal the blast doors. Wait. Sometimes survival is its own victory.\n\nSometimes survival is its own victory. It's not the most inspiring battle cry. But with the world ending above them, it's something.",
    choices: [
      { label: "Use Ford's communication device to call for help", target: "bunker_radio" },
      { label: "Fortify the bunker and wait out Weirdmageddon", target: "bunker_fortify" }
    ]
  },

  bunker_trust: {
    text: "\"WAIT!\" Dipper throws his hands up, stepping between Mabel and the raging Shapeshifter. It's a move that's either incredibly brave or incredibly stupid, and Dipper is fully aware it might be both. The creature freezes mid-lunge, a hundred forms flickering across its surface like a television caught between channels. One of its many eyes focuses on Dipper with something that might be curiosity. Or hunger. Hard to tell with a being made of living nightmares.\n\n\"We don't have to fight,\" Dipper says, his voice shaking only a little. \"Think about it. You've been locked in that tube for thirty years. Ford put you there because you were dangerous. But right now, the most dangerous thing in Gravity Falls isn't you -- it's Bill Cipher. He's ripping reality apart. And when reality falls apart completely, what happens to YOU?\"\n\nThe Shapeshifter pauses. Its forms slow their cycling, settling into something vaguely humanoid but deliberately neutral -- no specific face, just a smooth, pale figure with too-bright eyes. When it speaks, the voice is its own, not borrowed from anyone else. It's surprisingly soft, like wet silk dragged across stone.\n\n\"You make an interesting point, Pine Tree.\" The creature tilts its featureless head. \"Bill Cipher's chaos is... indiscriminate. It unmakes everything. Even me. I can feel it from down here -- reality is thinning. The walls between dimensions are dissolving. For a creature like me, that is not freedom. That is dissolution. I would lose my ability to hold any form at all. I would become... nothing.\"\n\n\"So help us stop him,\" Mabel says, stepping up beside Dipper. Her grappling hook is still in her hand, but she lowers it slightly. \"We need allies. You're super strong and you can turn into literally anything. That's, like, the best superpower for fighting a chaos god.\"\n\nThe Shapeshifter regards them both. Something shifts in its expression -- if you can call the subtle rearrangement of a blank face an expression. It almost looks... thoughtful. \"An alliance. Between the children who imprisoned me and the monster they imprisoned. There is a certain poetry to it, I suppose.\"\n\n\"Is that a yes?\" Dipper asks hopefully.\n\n\"It is a... consideration.\" The Shapeshifter shrinks slightly, becoming less threatening, more contained. It takes the form of a large white cat -- neutral, non-threatening, a peace offering in physical form. \"I despise Bill Cipher. His kind treats creatures like me as toys. Playthings to be summoned, used, and discarded. I have existed for millennia, children. I will not be unmade by a triangle with delusions of grandeur.\"\n\nMabel actually smiles. \"See, Dipper? This is what I've been saying. Everyone has good in them. Even shapeshifting nightmare monsters.\"\n\n\"Mabel, I really don't think--\"\n\n\"We should go deeper into the bunker,\" the Shapeshifter-cat says smoothly. \"Ford had resources. Weapons. Things that could hurt even Bill. I saw them, in the years before I was frozen. Follow me. I know the way.\"\n\nIt turns and pads down the corridor on silent paws, its white tail swishing gently. Mabel follows immediately, her natural trust overriding every survival instinct Dipper wishes she had. Dipper hesitates. Something feels wrong. The Shapeshifter gave in too easily. It agreed too quickly. And its eyes, even in cat form, have a brightness that isn't warmth -- it's calculation.\n\nBut what choice do they have? The surface is death. The bunker is failing. And they need help from somewhere.\n\nDipper follows.\n\nThe Shapeshifter leads them down a narrow corridor, through a door Dipper hasn't seen before. It's dark. The emergency lights don't reach here. \"Just a little further,\" the cat purrs. \"The good stuff is in the back.\"\n\nMabel is three steps ahead of Dipper when it happens.\n\nThe white cat EXPLODES outward, growing in every direction at once. Tentacles wrap around Mabel's waist, lifting her off her feet. A massive arm -- gorilla-thick and covered in scales -- slams Dipper against the wall so hard he sees stars. His flashlight clatters to the floor, spinning, casting wild shadows.\n\nThe Shapeshifter rises above them, filling the corridor, a form of pure malice with dozens of arms and a grinning mouth that stretches from wall to wall. \"Did you really think,\" it says, in a voice that's all of their voices at once -- Dipper's, Mabel's, Wendy's, Ford's, Stan's, everyone they've ever loved -- \"that I would HELP the creatures who kept me in a CAGE for THIRTY YEARS?\"\n\nIt drags them, struggling and screaming, back toward the cryogenic chamber. The cracked containment tube still stands, just barely functional. The Shapeshifter shoves Dipper and Mabel inside and slams the broken glass door shut, jamming it closed with a tentacle-turned-steel-bar. Through the frosted glass, they can see it changing, shifting, settling on a form.\n\nDipper's form. His hat. His vest. His face. Except the smile is wrong -- too wide, too sharp, with teeth that catch the emergency light and gleam like needles.\n\n\"Don't worry,\" the Shapeshifter says in Dipper's voice, adjusting the pine tree hat on its stolen head. \"I'll take good care of your friends. They'll never know the difference.\"\n\nThe last thing Dipper saw was his own face smiling back at him with too many teeth. Then the chamber door sealed, the cryo systems kicked in on their last dregs of power, and the cold took everything.",
    ending: true
  },

  bunker_deep: {
    text: "They leave the Shapeshifter's chamber behind, sealing the blast door with every lock they can find. It won't hold forever, but it buys them time. The deeper they go, the older the bunker feels. The walls shift from reinforced steel to bare rock lined with copper wiring and strange symbols that Dipper recognizes from the journals -- protective wards, dimensional anchors, things Ford carved to keep reality stable in his most paranoid years.\n\nLevel Three. Level Four. A stairway Dipper didn't know existed, spiraling down into rock that's warm to the touch. The air smells different here -- electric, like the moment before a lightning strike, mixed with something sweeter. Ozone and honey.\n\n\"Ford must have built this section in secret,\" Dipper whispers, running his fingers along the walls. \"Even the blueprints I found in the journal only showed three levels. This is... this is something else.\"\n\nThe stairway opens into a chamber that takes their breath away.\n\nIt's enormous. Carved from living rock, the walls studded with crystals that pulse with a faint inner light -- not the emergency red of the upper levels, but a soft, shifting blue-white that makes the whole room feel like the inside of a star. Equipment lines the walls: machines Dipper has never seen before, instruments that look half-scientific and half-magical, screens displaying data in languages that aren't entirely human. Ford wasn't just studying the paranormal down here. He was building with it.\n\n\"Dipper, look at this!\" Mabel is standing before a massive corkboard mounted on the far wall -- Ford's characteristic method of organizing his thoughts. But this board is different from the conspiracy walls Dipper has seen in the journals. This one is focused, precise, obsessive. Every note, every diagram, every photograph is connected by red string that converges on a single image pinned at the center.\n\nAn axolotl.\n\nNot a regular axolotl -- this one is drawn in Ford's hand with reverent detail, surrounded by equations and dimensional coordinates and notes in a language Dipper doesn't recognize. The word AXOLOTL is written above it in letters three inches tall, and beneath it, in smaller, shakier handwriting: The one being Bill fears. The only entity with the power to undo what he has done. If Cipher succeeds, this is the last hope.\n\n\"The Axolotl,\" Dipper breathes, pulling notes from the board with trembling hands. \"Ford knew about it. He KNEW.\" The notes are extensive but incomplete, as if Ford ran out of time -- or got too scared to continue. There are references to a being that exists outside normal dimensions, a cosmic entity older than Bill Cipher himself, one that governs the deepest rules of reality. Ford describes it as \"neither good nor evil -- simply fundamental. Like gravity. Like time. Like the rules that Bill Cipher is breaking.\"\n\nOne note, scrawled on a napkin in what looks like four AM handwriting, reads: The Axolotl can reset the damage. But reaching it requires a journey no human has survived. The coordinates point to a space between dimensions -- the In-Between. A place where thought becomes geography and fear becomes weather.\n\nAnother note, calmer and more analytical: I've identified three possible methods of contact. The dimensional radio on Level 4 (unreliable). The small portal prototype (EXTREMELY DANGEROUS -- DO NOT ACTIVATE WITHOUT FULL CALIBRATION). And the journals themselves -- I've encoded a cipher in the pages that, when read in the correct order, opens a channel to the Axolotl's realm. But I never finished the cipher. I never finished because I was afraid of what would answer.\n\nMabel is examining something else -- a device in the corner of the room, draped in a tarp. She pulls the tarp away and gasps. It's a portal. Much smaller than the universe portal under the Mystery Shack -- this one is only about six feet tall, a ring of dark metal covered in the same symbols as the walls, with a control panel that's relatively simple compared to the massive machine they destroyed last summer.\n\n\"Ford built a backup,\" Mabel says. \"A baby portal.\"\n\n\"An uncalibrated baby portal,\" Dipper corrects, reading the warning labels plastered across the control panel. PROTOTYPE. UNSTABLE. DO NOT ACTIVATE WITHOUT DIMENSIONAL ANCHOR COORDINATES. SERIOUSLY, STANFORD, DON'T DO IT. -- That last one is in different handwriting. McGucket's, maybe.\n\nDipper looks between Ford's notes about the Axolotl and the small portal humming quietly in its corner. Two paths. The notes could tell them how to reach the Axolotl, the being that might be able to undo all of this. Or the portal could take them somewhere -- anywhere -- but without proper calibration, \"anywhere\" is a terrifyingly literal word.",
    choices: [
      { label: "Study Ford's notes about the Axolotl", target: "axolotl_journal" },
      { label: "Try to activate the small portal", target: "ending_bunker_portal" }
    ]
  },

  bunker_supplies: {
    text: "\"Supplies first,\" Dipper decides, pulling open the nearest overturned cabinet. \"We don't even have food. Or weapons. Or -- actually, we don't have ANYTHING except your grappling hook and my anxiety.\"\n\n\"And my winning personality,\" Mabel adds, already rummaging through a pile of debris with the enthusiasm of someone shopping at a garage sale during the apocalypse. \"Ooh, flashlight! Working flashlight! Two flashlights! Dipper, we're RICH in flashlights!\"\n\nThe upper level of the bunker is a mess, but it's the kind of mess that rewards thorough searching. Ford was a hoarder of useful things -- every drawer, every cabinet, every shelf was packed with equipment, provisions, and paranormal countermeasures. Most of it has been scattered by whatever ransacked the place, but the goods are still here, just disorganized.\n\nThey find canned food first. Lots of it. Ford apparently believed in being prepared for a thirty-year siege. Canned beans, canned peaches, canned something-that-might-be-meat-but-the-label-fell-off. Mabel stacks them into a pyramid out of habit. \"Twenty-six cans! That's like... a lot of days of eating. Especially if we skip lunch. Which I am NOT willing to do, for the record.\"\n\nDipper finds a crate of Ford's old weapons -- the kind of bizarre hybrid devices that only a man with twelve PhDs and a grudge against the supernatural could create. There's a modified crossbow that shoots bolts of concentrated UV light. A glove that generates small electromagnetic pulses. Something that looks like a snow globe but is labeled EMERGENCY REALITY STABILIZER -- SINGLE USE. And, at the bottom of the crate, a device Dipper recognizes from the journals: a Magnet Gun, capable of pulling any metallic object toward its user from up to fifty feet away.\n\n\"This stuff is incredible,\" Dipper mutters, strapping the Magnet Gun to his wrist. \"Ford was ready for war.\"\n\n\"Dipper.\" Mabel's voice has gone quiet. The kind of quiet that means she's found something she wasn't expecting.\n\nShe's crouching near the bunker's small kitchenette area -- a hot plate, a mini fridge, a tiny sink, all Ford's concessions to basic human needs during his years of underground research. The mini fridge is open. And on the counter beside it sits a can of beans. Open. Still warm. A spoon rests against its lip, as if someone set it down just moments ago.\n\n\"Someone's been eating Ford's beans,\" Mabel whispers.\n\nDipper sweeps his flashlight across the kitchenette. Now that he's looking for it, the signs are everywhere. A sleeping bag, rolled up hastily and shoved behind the fridge. Boot prints in the dust -- fresh ones, with a distinctive tread pattern. A pair of dirty work gloves draped over the sink. And on the wall, scratched into the metal with what looks like a screwdriver, a tally of days. Seven marks. Someone has been living here for a week.\n\nThe boot prints lead in two directions. One trail heads toward the main corridor, where a shadow might or might not have just moved. The other trail leads deeper into the bunker, through a door marked AUTHORIZED PERSONNEL ONLY in Ford's handwriting, with a newer addition scratched beneath it in a shaky hand: still works. come on in.\n\n\"Okay,\" Dipper says very slowly, gripping the Magnet Gun. \"Someone is in this bunker with us. They've been here since Weirdmageddon started. And they were just eating beans, like, five minutes ago.\"\n\n\"Which means they heard us come in,\" Mabel adds. \"And they hid.\"\n\nThey stand in the dim red light of the emergency lamps, surrounded by Ford's weapons and supplies, listening to the silence of the bunker. Somewhere in the darkness, someone is breathing. Someone who survived the first week of the end of the world by hiding underground and eating canned beans.\n\nDipper looks at Mabel. They can call out -- introduce themselves, try to make contact peacefully. Whoever is hiding might be friendly. They might be a survivor who needs help, or someone who knows things about what's happening on the surface. Or they can stay quiet and follow the footprints, figuring out who they're dealing with before revealing themselves. Because in Weirdmageddon, not everything that hides is hiding because it's scared. Some things hide because they're hunting.",
    choices: [
      { label: "Call out to whoever is hiding", target: "bunker_mcgucket" },
      { label: "Quietly follow the footprints", target: "bunker_footprints" }
    ]
  },

  bunker_mcgucket: {
    text: "\"Hello?\" Mabel calls out, cupping her hands around her mouth. Her voice echoes through the bunker's metal corridors, bouncing off walls and disappearing into the deeper levels. \"We're friendly! We're the Pines twins! We come in peace! We also come with a grappling hook, but that's more of a defensive thing!\"\n\nSilence. Then a clang. Then a series of clangs, getting closer, accompanied by a muttering voice that sounds like someone having an argument with themselves. Then a wild-eyed face pops around the corner, topped with a long white beard and a hat made from what appears to be an old tin can, three spoons, and some sort of satellite dish constructed from aluminum foil.\n\n\"PINES TWINS!\" Old Man McGucket shrieks, tumbling around the corner in a tangle of limbs and overalls. He's thinner than when they last saw him, his eyes wider, his beard longer. But the spark of intelligence that returned after he got his memories back -- that's still there, burning bright behind the crazy. \"Oh, jumpin' jackrabbits, I never thought I'd see a friendly face again! I thought you was Bill's henchmen come to root me out! I had my bean cannon loaded and everything!\"\n\n\"Bean cannon?\" Dipper asks, and McGucket proudly produces a device made from PVC pipe, rubber bands, and a bicycle pump that appears to launch cans of beans at high velocity. \"You built a weapon that shoots beans.\"\n\n\"Aerodynamic AND nutritious!\" McGucket cackles. Then his face falls, the manic energy deflating like a punctured balloon. \"Listen, kids. I've been down here since it started. Since Bill tore the sky open and everything went sideways. I remembered this place -- memories came back, see, after you kids helped me. I remembered working here with Stanford, building things, doing science. So when the world ended, I came to the one place I knew was safe.\"\n\nHe leads them through the bunker to a room he's turned into a makeshift command center. It's classic McGucket -- brilliant and insane in equal measure. He's wired together a periscope device from spare parts that extends through a narrow ventilation shaft all the way to the surface, giving them a fuzzy but functional view of the world above. He's built a crude radio receiver from bunker scrap. And he's been keeping meticulous notes on what he's observed, written on the walls in marker because he ran out of paper on day three.\n\n\"Come look,\" he says, guiding them to the periscope. Dipper presses his eye to the lens and his stomach drops.\n\nThe surface is worse than he imagined. The forest is transformed -- trees have been twisted into impossible shapes, their branches reaching toward the fractured sky like grasping hands. Eye-bats patrol in formations of dozens. In the distance, the Fearamid pulses with sickly light, and Dipper can see tiny shapes circling it -- Bill's hench-maniacs, the interdimensional criminals he's freed from their dimensional prisons. The town of Gravity Falls is barely recognizable, buildings warped and floating and turned inside out.\n\nBut McGucket points to something else. \"Look southeast. The Mystery Shack.\"\n\nDipper adjusts the periscope and his heart nearly stops. The Mystery Shack is still standing. Not just standing -- it's fortified. Someone has built barricades around it. There are lights in the windows. And on the roof, he can see a familiar figure standing guard with what looks like a baseball bat.\n\n\"That's Wendy,\" Dipper breathes.\n\n\"The resistance,\" McGucket confirms, pride and sadness mixing in his voice. \"They started organizing on day two. The survivors -- the ones Bill didn't turn to stone or trap in his throne. They've made the Shack their headquarters. Your Grunkle Stan is leading them, if you can believe it. Stanley Pines, resistance leader. The grifter with a heart of gold.\"\n\n\"We have to go to them,\" Mabel says immediately. \"We have to help.\"\n\n\"It's dangerous,\" McGucket warns. \"The surface is crawling with Bill's minions. I haven't been brave enough to try the trip myself. But I know the forest paths, and I've been mapping the eye-bat patrol routes from up here.\" He gestures at a crude map drawn on the wall. \"If you go at the right time, through the right paths, you MIGHT make it.\"\n\nHe pauses, tugging at his beard. \"Or you could stay here. Help me fortify. I've got plans for improvements -- a water recycling system, expanded food storage, better defenses. We could make this bunker into a real shelter. Safe. Secure. Hidden. We could survive, kids. It's not heroic, but it's alive.\"",
    choices: [
      { label: "Head to the Mystery Shack to join the resistance", target: "resistance_rally" },
      { label: "Stay with McGucket and help fortify the bunker", target: "bunker_fortify" }
    ]
  },

  bunker_footprints: {
    text: "Dipper holds a finger to his lips. Mabel nods, understanding instantly -- twin telepathy, or just twelve years of shared experience that taught them when to be loud and when to be ghosts. They follow the footprints, stepping carefully, avoiding the scattered debris that would crunch and clang and give them away.\n\nThe footprints lead deeper into the bunker, through corridors that narrow as they go. Whoever made these prints was small -- the boot treads are close together, the stride short. Not an adult, or at least not a big one. And the prints wander, sometimes doubling back, sometimes stopping in front of walls as if the person was lost or confused or looking for something specific.\n\nThey pass through a doorway marked STORAGE LEVEL 2 and into a section of the bunker that's less laboratory and more warehouse. Shelves stretch from floor to ceiling, loaded with crates and boxes labeled in Ford's methodical handwriting. Emergency rations, marked with dates from the 1980s. Dimensional stabilizers, packed in foam. Something called EXPERIMENT 78 -- DO NOT SHAKE, accompanied by a drawing of a mushroom cloud.\n\nThe footprints lead to the far corner of the warehouse, behind a wall of stacked crates. And that's where they hear it -- breathing. Fast, shallow, panicky breathing, the kind that comes from someone trying very hard to be quiet and failing.\n\nDipper peers around the edge of the crates, the Magnet Gun on his wrist humming softly. What he sees makes him stop dead.\n\nIt's Gideon Gleeful.\n\nThe former child psychic, the self-proclaimed star of Gravity Falls, is huddled in the corner like a kicked puppy. His white pompadour is flattened and dirty. His signature blue suit is torn at the shoulder and stained with something that might be mud or might be worse. His eyes are red-rimmed and enormous with fear. He looks nothing like the confident, manipulative showman who once tried to steal the Mystery Shack and destroy the Pines family. He looks like what he actually is: a scared kid.\n\nBut that's not the worst part.\n\nGideon's left hand is gold. Not painted gold, not wearing a gold glove -- solid, actual, twenty-four-karat GOLD, from fingertip to wrist. It gleams dully in the emergency light, heavy and rigid, the fingers frozen in a grasping position. And at the edges, where the gold meets his skin, there's a faint shimmer, like the transformation is still happening. Still spreading. Slowly, steadily creeping up his forearm like a golden tide.\n\n\"Don't hurt me!\" Gideon yelps when he sees them, pressing himself further into the corner, cradling his gold hand against his chest. \"Please, I ain't -- I'm not here to cause trouble, I swear on my mama's pearls! I just needed somewhere to hide!\"\n\n\"Gideon?\" Mabel stares. Of all the things she expected to find in Ford's bunker during the apocalypse, her creepy former stalker was not high on the list. \"What happened to you? What happened to your HAND?\"\n\nGideon's eyes fill with tears -- real ones, not the crocodile tears he's famous for. His lower lip trembles. \"Bill happened,\" he whispers. \"Bill Cipher happened to me.\"\n\nThe story comes out in a rush, between sobs and hiccups. When Weirdmageddon started, Gideon had been one of Bill's allies. Bill had promised him power, revenge on the Pines family, his own throne in the new world order. And for the first few hours, it seemed real. Gideon had a crown. He had minions. He had everything he'd ever wanted.\n\n\"Then I made the mistake of askin' for MORE,\" Gideon says, his voice cracking. \"I wanted to be Bill's equal, not his servant. And Bill... Bill laughed. He laughed and he touched my hand and he said, 'Here's your gold throne, kid,' and then...\" He holds up the golden hand. In the dim light, they can see it's spread another quarter inch since he started talking. \"It's a curse. It won't stop. In a few hours, I'll be a statue. A golden Gideon, stuck forever in Bill's collection.\"\n\nHe looks up at them with desperate, pleading eyes. \"But I know things. I know how Bill's palace works. I know a secret way in -- a passage Bill showed me when he still thought I was useful. I can help you. Please. Just... don't leave me here alone. I don't want to turn to gold alone.\"",
    choices: [
      { label: "Trust Gideon and hear his plan", target: "bunker_gideon_plan" },
      { label: "Don't trust Gideon - he's tricked you before", target: "bunker_gideon_refuse" }
    ]
  },

  bunker_gideon_plan: {
    text: "Dipper looks at Mabel. Mabel looks at Dipper. They have an entire conversation in the space of three seconds, conducted entirely through eyebrow movements and tiny head tilts -- the Pines twins' secret language, perfected over twelve years of shared bedrooms and sibling arguments and adventures that would make most adults faint.\n\nDipper's eyebrows say: This is Gideon. He's lied to us literally every time we've met him.\n\nMabel's eyebrows respond: His hand is turning to gold. That's not something you can fake.\n\nDipper's eyebrows: Fair point.\n\n\"Okay, Gideon,\" Dipper says, crouching down to eye level with the huddled boy. \"Talk. Tell us everything. And if you're lying -- if this is another one of your schemes -- I swear I'll feed you to the Shapeshifter myself.\"\n\nGideon blinks. \"There's a SHAPESHIFTER in here?!\"\n\n\"Focus, Gideon.\"\n\n\"Right. Right, okay.\" Gideon wipes his nose with his non-gold hand and sits up straighter, some of his old showman's instinct kicking in. Even terrified and cursed, Gideon Gleeful can't resist an audience. \"Bill Cipher has a weakness. I heard his hench-maniacs talkin' about it when they thought I wasn't listenin'. They called it the Cipher Wheel.\"\n\n\"The zodiac,\" Dipper breathes. He knows about this. He's seen it in the journals -- a circle of ten symbols, each representing a different person. The pine tree. The shooting star. The question mark. The llama. All of them connected to people in Gravity Falls. \"Ford's notes mention it. If the ten people represented by the symbols come together and perform the ritual, they can banish Bill.\"\n\n\"That's the one!\" Gideon nods eagerly. \"The tent-of-telepathy star is mine. I'm one of the symbols. You two are symbols too -- the pine tree and the shootin' star. We already got three out of ten right here in this bunker!\"\n\nDipper grabs a piece of paper and starts writing, his mind racing. \"The symbols are: Pine Tree -- that's me. Shooting Star -- Mabel. Tent of Telepathy -- Gideon. Question Mark -- Soos. Ice Bag -- Wendy. Fez -- Grunkle Stan. Six-Fingered Hand -- Great Uncle Ford. Llama -- Pacifica. Stitched Heart -- Robbie. Glasses -- McGucket.\"\n\n\"That's ten people,\" Mabel counts. \"Some of them might be at the Mystery Shack with the resistance. But Ford is... we don't know where Ford is. And some of these people might be, um...\" She doesn't finish the sentence. Turned to stone. Captured. Gone.\n\nGideon leans forward, his golden hand clinking against the floor. \"Here's what I know about gettin' into the Fearamid. Bill showed me a passage -- it goes through the base, up through what used to be the cliffs east of town. Bill used it to send his scouts out without bein' noticed. It's hidden, it's unguarded, and it leads right to the heart of the pyramid. If we can gather the symbols and get inside, we can perform the ritual right under Bill's nose.\"\n\n\"But do you know how the ritual actually works?\" Dipper presses. \"Just getting the people together isn't enough. There has to be a specific process.\"\n\nGideon's enthusiasm dims slightly. \"Well... no. I know you need the ten people and you need the wheel and you need them all to hold hands and focus or somethin'. The details were above my pay grade. But once we're in the Fearamid, maybe we can find out more. Bill keeps trophies -- records of everything that threatens him. The information might be there.\"\n\nDipper frowns. Something about this feels incomplete. The Cipher Wheel, the ten symbols, the ritual -- he knows all this from the journals. But there's something else, something Ford hinted at in his notes that Gideon clearly doesn't know about. The Axolotl. The cosmic being that Ford described as Bill's only true fear. The Cipher Wheel might banish Bill, but the Axolotl could undo Weirdmageddon entirely. Could reset the damage. Could fix everything.\n\nBut Gideon doesn't mention the Axolotl at all. His plan is partially right but potentially incomplete. Going to the Fearamid with partial knowledge could be walking into a trap.\n\nThen again, the bunker might have more answers. Ford's deeper levels, his notes, his research -- there could be information down here that fills in the gaps Gideon doesn't know about. Information about the Axolotl, about the true nature of the ritual, about what it really takes to stop a dream demon who's already won.\n\n\"So what do you say?\" Gideon asks, holding out his good hand. His golden hand shimmers, the curse creeping another millimeter up his arm. \"Are we doin' this?\"",
    choices: [
      { label: "Follow Gideon's plan to the Fearamid", target: "fearamid_approach" },
      { label: "Search for more information in the bunker first", target: "bunker_deep" }
    ]
  },

  bunker_gideon_refuse: {
    text: "\"No.\" The word comes out harder than Dipper means it to. He sees Gideon flinch, sees the hope drain from those red-rimmed eyes, and for a moment he almost takes it back. Almost. But then he remembers everything. The lies. The manipulation. The time Gideon stole the deed to the Mystery Shack. The time he summoned Bill Cipher in the first place. The time he used a giant robot to try to literally crush Dipper. The time he kidnapped Mabel. Every. Single. Time.\n\n\"We can't trust you, Gideon,\" Dipper says, and his voice is steady even though his stomach is churning. \"Every time we've trusted you, you've betrayed us. Every. Single. Time. You allied with Bill Cipher VOLUNTARILY. You chose his side. And now that it's gone wrong, you want us to bail you out? How do we know this isn't another trick? How do we know Bill didn't SEND you here to lure us to the Fearamid?\"\n\nGideon's face crumples. \"It ain't a trick! Look at my HAND, Dipper! Look what he did to me!\" He holds up the golden appendage, heavy and gleaming and horrible. The gold has crept past his wrist now, swallowing his forearm with terrible patience. \"Does this look like a TRICK to you?!\"\n\n\"I've seen weirder tricks in Gravity Falls,\" Dipper says quietly. And he has. That's the awful thing. In a town where nothing is what it seems, where shapeshifters wear your friends' faces and dream demons make deals with smiles, even a boy slowly turning to gold might be a lie.\n\nMabel hasn't said anything. She's standing behind Dipper, her arms wrapped around herself, her sweater's shooting star slightly askew. She's looking at Gideon with an expression Dipper can't quite read. Not anger. Not fear. Something sadder.\n\n\"Mabel,\" Gideon pleads, turning to her with desperate eyes. \"Mabel, you've always been the kind one. You always saw the good in people. Even me. Remember? Even after everything I did, you still --\"\n\n\"I remember,\" Mabel says softly. \"I remember seeing good in you. And I remember being wrong.\"\n\nThe silence that follows is the loudest sound in the bunker.\n\nGideon's shoulders collapse. All the showmanship, all the bravado, all the scheming intelligence drains out of him like water from a broken cup. What's left is just a ten-year-old boy, alone in a bunker during the end of the world, with a curse eating him alive and nobody willing to help.\n\n\"Please,\" he whispers. It's barely a sound. \"I don't want to die alone.\"\n\nDipper opens his mouth to say something -- he doesn't know what, maybe something about how Gideon brought this on himself, maybe something about consequences and choices and all the things Journal 3 never prepared him for -- when Gideon gasps.\n\nThe gold has reached his elbow.\n\nThey can actually see it moving now, a slow, relentless tide of metallic transformation creeping up his arm like sunrise crawling across a landscape. Gideon stares at it with wide, horrified eyes. His fingers on the golden hand don't move -- they can't, they're solid metal now, frozen in that grasping pose forever. But his other hand is shaking violently.\n\n\"It's speeding up,\" he says, his voice thin and high. \"It was -- it was going slow before but now it's -- oh no. Oh no oh no oh no.\"\n\nMabel makes a sound in the back of her throat. She steps forward, past Dipper, and sits down next to Gideon on the cold bunker floor. She doesn't say anything. She just sits there, close enough that their shoulders almost touch. Gideon looks at her with such naked gratitude that Dipper has to look away.\n\n\"We'll figure something out,\" Mabel says. But her voice has that hollow quality it gets when she's saying what she wishes were true instead of what she knows is true.\n\nThey don't figure something out. There's nothing to figure. The curse is Bill Cipher's work, and Bill Cipher's magic doesn't come with an off switch. They search the bunker frantically -- Dipper tears through Ford's notes looking for anything about transmutation curses, gold magic, anything. He finds references but no cures. Ford never solved this one. Maybe it can't be solved.\n\nThe gold reaches Gideon's shoulder by hour two. His chest by hour three. He stops talking somewhere around hour four, not because the gold has reached his mouth but because he simply runs out of words. Mabel stays beside him the entire time, holding his remaining hand, talking about nothing -- about her favorite sweaters, about Waddles, about the time she and Dipper fought a gnome army. Anything to fill the silence.\n\nBy hour six, Gideon Gleeful is a golden statue. A small boy frozen mid-sob, one hand reaching out, the other clutching the hand of a girl who stayed.\n\nMabel doesn't cry. That's the part that scares Dipper the most. She just stands up, looks at the golden figure of the boy who once terrorized them and then begged them for help, and says, very quietly, \"We should have tried harder.\"\n\nThey survive in the bunker. They eat Ford's canned food and sleep in Ford's emergency bunks and listen to the world ending above them. Mabel stops making jokes. Dipper stops reading the journals. They sit in the dim red light with a golden statue of a boy who didn't get saved, and they survive. They survive, but something important dies in that bunker, something that has nothing to do with Gideon and everything to do with who they thought they were -- kids who always found a way, kids who always saved the day. In the end, they're just kids. And sometimes kids can't save anyone.\n\nSometimes you survive, and that's the worst ending of all.",
    ending: true
  },

  bunker_fortify: {
    text: "They choose safety. They choose survival. They seal the blast doors, reinforce the walls, ration the food. It's the smart choice. The logical choice. The choice that keeps them breathing.\n\nIt's the choice that slowly kills everything else.\n\nThe first three days aren't bad, honestly. There's almost a summer camp feeling to it -- Mabel decorates their section of the bunker with drawings made from Ford's old markers, turning the cold steel walls into a gallery of shooting stars and pigs and happy memories. Dipper organizes the supplies with methodical precision, creating inventory lists and meal plans and maintenance schedules. If McGucket is with them, he tinkers endlessly, improving the ventilation, rigging up a water recycling system from spare parts, building increasingly elaborate periscope devices to monitor the surface.\n\nThey eat canned peaches for dinner and tell stories and for a little while, it almost feels okay.\n\nThe first week is when the cracks appear. Not in the bunker walls -- those hold fine. Ford built them strong. The cracks appear in the people.\n\nMabel's drawings get darker. Not scary-dark, just... less colorful. She runs out of pink marker first, then yellow, then orange. She starts drawing with the grays and the blacks, and the pictures shift from shooting stars and dance parties to the forest on fire and the sky torn open and Bill's eye staring down from above. She draws their friends -- Wendy, Soos, Candy, Grenda -- and then she stops, because she doesn't know if they're still alive, and drawing their faces makes that uncertainty a knife in her chest.\n\nDipper stops sleeping. He spends his nights poring over Ford's notes by flashlight, searching for a solution, a weapon, a way out. He fills notebook after notebook with theories and plans and equations he barely understands, his handwriting getting smaller and more cramped as the pages fill, until it looks exactly like Ford's. The resemblance scares him when he notices it, and then he goes right back to writing.\n\nThe tremors from the surface grow stronger. Sometimes they can hear sounds filtering down through fifty feet of rock and steel -- screams that might be human, laughter that definitely isn't, music that plays backward and makes your fillings ache. Bill Cipher is remaking the world above them, and they can hear it happening, and they can't do anything about it.\n\nWeek two. The power fluctuates. The emergency lights dim, brighten, dim again. The bunker's systems were never designed to run this long on backup generators. Things start breaking. The water recycler develops a concerning rattle. The ventilation fans slow down. The air gets stale, thick, tasting of metal and recycled breath.\n\nMabel stops making art entirely. She sits in her bunk, wrapped in a blanket, staring at the wall. When Dipper tries to talk to her, she responds in single words. Fine. Sure. Okay. Whatever. The sparkle -- that indefinable Mabel quality, that relentless optimism that survived gnome attacks and time travel and the literal end of summer -- is guttering out like a candle in a sealed room.\n\n\"We're safe,\" Dipper tells her every morning. It stops sounding like comfort and starts sounding like a prison sentence.\n\nWeek three. Something changes on the surface. The tremors stop. The sounds stop. The silence is somehow worse than the chaos -- at least chaos meant something was happening. Silence means it's over. Silence means Bill won completely, or Bill got bored, or something happened that's beyond their ability to hear.\n\nDipper climbs to the hatch. He puts his hand on the wheel. He stands there for six hours before Mabel finds him and gently pulls him away.\n\nWeek four. They open the hatch.\n\nThe forest is quiet. The sky is still cracked -- not the violent tear of active Weirdmageddon, but healed wrong, like a broken bone that set crooked. Daylight filters through in strange colors. The trees are alive but changed, their bark patterned with eye-like knots that seem to watch. The air smells like ozone and something sweet, like the ghost of a carnival.\n\nWeirdmageddon has burned itself out. Bill's chaos couldn't sustain itself forever -- even dream demons run out of steam eventually. But the damage is done. Gravity Falls is permanently altered, a place where the membrane between dimensions is forever thin. Weird creatures wander the streets of town, lost and confused, dimensional refugees with nowhere to go home to. The Fearamid is gone, collapsed into rubble, but its footprint remains -- a perfect triangle of dead earth where nothing will ever grow.\n\nThe survivors emerge from wherever they've been hiding. Not many. Enough. They look at the twins with hollow eyes and ask, \"Where were you?\" Not accusingly. Just wondering. Just asking.\n\nDipper and Mabel don't have a good answer. They were safe. They were underground. They survived.\n\nThey survived, and the world they knew is gone. The Mystery Shack still stands, leaning more than ever. Grunkle Stan is there, older than he should be, with a new scar across his forehead he won't explain. He hugs them so hard Mabel's feet leave the ground, and he says, \"I thought I lost you kids,\" and his voice breaks on the word \"lost\" in a way that Stanley Pines' voice has never broken before.\n\nThey survived. They're alive. And every night, Mabel dreams of what might have happened if they'd been braver, and Dipper writes in his journal about the cost of playing it safe. The sky stays cracked. The weird never fully goes away. And somewhere, in the silence between moments, a triangle shape flickers in the corner of your vision, and a voice that might be memory and might be something worse whispers: I'll be back.\n\nThey survived. It doesn't feel like winning.",
    ending: true
  },

  bunker_radio: {
    text: "The communication device is on Level Four, exactly where Ford's notes said it would be. Getting there means navigating through two more blast doors, down a ladder with rungs so cold they burn through Dipper's gloves, and past a corridor lined with warning signs in six languages, three of which aren't spoken by humans.\n\nThe room itself is small -- barely bigger than the supply closet at the Mystery Shack -- but packed with equipment that hums with a quiet, persistent energy. Unlike the rest of the bunker, the power down here seems stable, fed by something Dipper can't identify. The walls pulse with a faint blue light that has no obvious source. It's warmer, too, and the air tastes clean, almost sweet, like breathing near a waterfall.\n\nThe dimensional radio sits on a desk in the center of the room, and it looks nothing like a radio. It's a crystalline structure about two feet tall, shaped roughly like a tree with branches that split and split again into fractal patterns too fine for the eye to follow. Thin wires of gold and silver thread through the crystal, connecting to a base unit that's covered in dials, switches, and one large tuning knob. A set of headphones hangs from a hook beside it, oversized, clearly built for a man with a larger head than Dipper's.\n\nFord's notes are taped to the desk beside the device:\n\nDIMENSIONAL RADIO -- OPERATION MANUAL\n1. Power on (green switch, left panel)\n2. Calibrate crystal resonance (turn main knob slowly until humming stabilizes)\n3. Set dimensional frequency (use charts on wall -- DO NOT tune to Dimension 666, Dimension NULL, or anything labeled with a triangle)\n4. Speak clearly into crystal array\n5. LISTEN before responding. Some things that answer are not what they seem.\n\n\"Rule number five is very comforting,\" Mabel observes. \"I love how literally nothing in Ford's bunker is reassuring. Even the instruction manuals are scary.\"\n\nDipper follows the steps. The green switch triggers a low hum that vibrates through the crystal, making the fractal branches glow. The tuning knob is sensitive -- the slightest touch shifts the frequency, and as Dipper turns it, they hear fragments of... everything. Voices in languages that haven't been invented yet. Music made of mathematics. A dimension where the only sound is someone eternally clearing their throat. Static that arranges itself into patterns that almost look like faces.\n\n\"This is incredible,\" Dipper whispers, his eyes wide. \"Ford built a radio that reaches across dimensions. Across REALITIES. We could contact anyone, anywhere--\"\n\nThen the static clears, and a signal locks in.\n\nIt's faint. A whisper in the cosmic noise, barely audible over the hum of the crystal. But it's there -- a rhythmic pulse, almost like breathing, carrying something that isn't quite sound and isn't quite thought. It's communication, but at a level deeper than language. Dipper can feel it more than hear it, a presence pressing against his mind like a hand against frosted glass.\n\nThe crystal's glow shifts. The blue-white light turns golden, then pink, then a color that Dipper doesn't have a name for -- it's soothing, whatever it is. The fractal branches rearrange themselves, the whole structure leaning slightly toward the twins like a flower turning toward the sun.\n\nSomething is responding.\n\nSomething vast and patient and old is on the other end of this signal, aware of them, listening. The presence doesn't feel hostile. It doesn't feel like Bill -- there's no malice, no hunger, no cosmic joke being told at their expense. It feels like... being noticed by the ocean. Like being seen by the sky. Something so much bigger than you that the act of it paying attention to you is itself a kind of miracle.\n\nMabel grabs Dipper's arm. \"Do you feel that?\" she whispers. Her eyes are wide, but not with fear. With wonder. The color she can't name is reflecting in her pupils, and for the first time since Weirdmageddon started, she looks like herself. Awed. Hopeful. Alive.\n\nThe signal pulses again, stronger. Waiting.\n\nDipper's hand hovers over the crystal. If he speaks, if he responds, whatever is on the other end will know exactly where they are. It will know they exist, that they're reaching out, that they need help. And that could be salvation. A cosmic entity powerful enough to undo Weirdmageddon. A force older than Bill Cipher, one that exists beyond his reach.\n\nOr it could be a trap. Bill is a master of deception, and Weirdmageddon has broken the rules that normally separate dimensions. What if this signal is Bill in disguise? What if something worse than Bill is fishing in the cosmic static, dangling hope like bait? Ford's own notes warned: some things that answer are not what they seem.\n\nThe signal pulses a third time. Patient. Waiting. Not demanding. Just... there.\n\n\"What do we do?\" Mabel asks.\n\nDipper stares at the crystal. The unnamed color dances in its fractal branches, beautiful and strange and full of possibility. His finger rests on the transmit switch.",
    choices: [
      { label: "Respond to the signal", target: "bunker_signal" },
      { label: "It could be a trap - stay silent", target: "ending_bunker_silence" }
    ]
  },

  bunker_signal: {
    text: "Dipper presses the transmit switch.\n\n\"Hello?\" His voice sounds impossibly small, a twelve-year-old boy speaking into the infinite. \"This is Dipper Pines. I'm in Gravity Falls, Oregon. Dimension... uh, the regular one. Our world is being destroyed by Bill Cipher. Weirdmageddon. We need help. Is anyone there? Can anyone hear me?\"\n\nThe crystal blazes with light. Every fractal branch ignites simultaneously, the unnamed color flooding the room so completely that for a moment, Dipper can't see the walls, can't see the desk, can't see anything except the light and his sister's hand gripping his arm. The hum becomes a chord, deep and resonant, like the universe itself is vibrating in sympathy.\n\nAnd then the voice comes.\n\nIt's not a voice in the way human voices work. It doesn't enter through their ears. It enters through everything -- their skin, their thoughts, the spaces between their heartbeats. It's warm and cool simultaneously, ancient and fresh, and it carries with it the impression of pink -- not the color, exactly, but the feeling of pink. Gentleness. Patience. The particular softness of very, very old things that have chosen to be kind.\n\n\"I hear you, Dipper Pines.\"\n\nThe twins freeze. The words settle into them like stones into still water, rippling outward through their minds. Mabel's grip on Dipper's arm tightens.\n\n\"Who -- what are you?\" Dipper asks, his voice cracking.\n\n\"I am called by many names in many dimensions. Your great-uncle called me the Axolotl. It is as good a name as any. I am what I have always been -- a keeper of balances, a guardian of the spaces between. I exist in the place where broken things can be made whole.\"\n\nThe crystal projects an image into the air above the desk -- shimmering, translucent, like a hologram made of starlight. An axolotl, but not a normal one. This creature is vast, its body composed of galaxies and nebulae, its external gills like branching rivers of light that extend into dimensions the eye can't follow. It's simultaneously the most alien and the most comforting thing Dipper has ever seen. It looks like it's smiling, in the patient way that mountains smile -- not because anything is funny, but because it has seen enough of time to know that smiling is better than not.\n\n\"The Axolotl,\" Mabel breathes. \"You're real. Ford's notes -- he was trying to find you--\"\n\n\"Stanford Pines came close,\" the Axolotl says, and there's a warmth in the not-voice, something almost like affection. \"He understood more than most humans. He understood that Bill Cipher is not the greatest power in existence, merely the loudest. There are forces older than chaos, children. Order is one. Love is another. And the willingness of small, frightened creatures to stand up and say 'no' to things much bigger than themselves -- that is perhaps the oldest force of all.\"\n\n\"Can you stop Weirdmageddon?\" Dipper asks, cutting straight to the point. He can feel the weight of the question, the enormity of what he's asking. Can you save our world? Can you fix everything? Can you undo the end?\n\nThe Axolotl's image flickers. For a moment, its cosmic smile seems almost sad. \"I can. But not from here. Not through a radio signal and a crystal array, no matter how cleverly your great-uncle built them. To undo what Bill Cipher has done, the ritual must be performed in my presence. You must come to me.\"\n\n\"Come to you WHERE?\" Dipper asks. \"You're in another dimension. We don't have a working portal. We don't even have a working car.\"\n\n\"The path to me is not a physical distance. It is a journey of understanding. I exist in the In-Between -- the space that connects all dimensions, the thread that runs through every reality. To reach me, you must find the places where the walls between worlds are thinnest. And in Gravity Falls, during Weirdmageddon, those thin places are everywhere.\"\n\nThe crystal projects new images: coordinates, not in numbers but in feelings, impressions, landmarks. A clearing in the forest where the trees grow in a perfect spiral. A point in the sky where the crack between dimensions pulses with a rhythmic light. A sound, a specific frequency, that when hummed correctly, opens a door that isn't a door.\n\n\"The journey is dangerous,\" the Axolotl continues. \"Bill Cipher will sense what you are attempting. His chaos thrives on hopelessness, and hope is exactly what you carry. You will need allies. You will need courage. And you will need to understand something that Bill has never understood.\"\n\n\"What's that?\" Mabel asks.\n\nThe Axolotl's cosmic form glows brighter, and the feeling of pink intensifies -- warmth, kindness, the stubborn gentleness of creatures who refuse to stop caring even when the world gives them every reason to.\n\n\"That the smallest lights shine brightest in the deepest dark. That a shooting star and a pine tree, standing together, are more powerful than any cipher. Come to me, children. Follow the coordinates. And bring your hope with you -- you will need every last bit of it.\"\n\nThe signal begins to fade. The crystal's glow dims. But the coordinates remain, burned into Dipper's memory with perfect clarity, and in his chest, something that had been cold and tight since Weirdmageddon began loosens just slightly.\n\nHope. Small and fragile and stubborn. Like a candle that refuses to go out.",
    choices: [
      { label: "Follow the Axolotl's instructions", target: "axolotl_clue" },
      { label: "The voice seems too good to be true", target: "ending_bunker_silence" }
    ]
  },

  ending_bunker_trapped: {
    text: "\"FORGET THE MACHINE!\" Dipper screams over the Shapeshifter's roar. \"BRING DOWN THE CEILING!\"\n\nIt's a desperate plan and they both know it, but desperate is all they have left. Dipper grabs the Magnet Gun -- the one he strapped on upstairs, a lifetime ago when the biggest problem was finding supplies -- and aims it at the damaged support columns. Mabel fires her grappling hook at the already-cracked ceiling structure, hooking one of the main support beams and pulling with everything she has.\n\nThe bunker groans. Metal screams against metal. The Shapeshifter, still half-frozen and furious, looks up just as the first chunk of ceiling breaks free and crashes down. Then another. Then the support column buckles, and everything happens at once.\n\nThe ceiling comes down like the fist of an angry god. Tons of rock and reinforced steel collapse into the cryogenic chamber, burying the Shapeshifter under a mountain of debris. They can hear it shriek -- a sound like every animal in the world screaming at once -- and then the shriek is muffled, buried, silenced under the weight of Ford's collapsed fortress.\n\nFor three seconds, Dipper feels triumph.\n\nThen the rest of the ceiling comes down.\n\nThe collapse doesn't stop at the chamber. The structural damage cascades outward like dominoes, each failing support triggering the next. The corridor behind them buckles, the walls folding inward like crumpled paper. Dipper grabs Mabel and runs, but there's nowhere to run TO -- the stairway back up is already blocked by a wall of fallen rock, and ahead of them, the deeper levels are caving in one after another, each blast door crumpling under pressures it was never designed to handle.\n\nThey end up in a pocket. A small space, maybe ten feet by ten feet, where a particularly stubborn section of wall has braced against the collapse and created a tiny cave in the wreckage. Dust fills the air, thick and choking. The emergency lights are dead. The only light comes from Dipper's flashlight, which is cracked but still working, casting a jittery beam across their prison.\n\n\"Mabel?\" Dipper coughs. \"Are you okay?\"\n\n\"I'm here.\" Her voice comes from his left, shaky but alive. She's pressed against the wall, covered in dust, her grappling hook still clutched in her white-knuckled hand. \"I'm okay. I think. Are we...?\"\n\n\"We're trapped.\" Dipper sweeps the flashlight around their pocket. Solid rock on three sides. Collapsed rubble on the fourth, piled floor to ceiling, with no gaps large enough to crawl through. The ceiling above them is a tangle of bent steel beams and cracked concrete, groaning softly, held up by nothing more than friction and luck. \"The whole section came down. The stairs are buried. The deeper levels are buried. We're...\"\n\nHe doesn't finish. He doesn't have to.\n\nMabel sits down heavily. \"Air,\" she says quietly. \"How much air do we have in a space this size?\"\n\nDipper does the math in his head because doing math is better than doing panic. A ten-by-ten-by-eight-foot space. Two people breathing. No ventilation. \"Maybe... eight hours. If we stay calm. If we don't exert ourselves. If we--\" He catches himself. \"Enough. We have enough for now.\"\n\nThey sit in the dim light of the cracked flashlight, listening to the bunker settle around them. Small shifts and groans as the rubble finds its resting place. And from somewhere beyond the wall of rock -- muffled, distant, but unmistakable -- laughter.\n\nThe Shapeshifter is laughing.\n\nIt's alive. Buried, yes, trapped like them, but alive. Its laughter filters through the rubble like water through cracks, distorted and echoing, carrying the cadence of a creature that has survived for millennia and intends to survive millennia more. It's patient. It knows the rubble won't hold forever. It knows that eventually, Weirdmageddon's chaos will shake the ground again, shift the debris, create an opening. And when it does, the Shapeshifter will flow through the cracks like the living nightmare it is, and it will be free, and the twins will still be trapped.\n\n\"So that's not great,\" Mabel says.\n\nDipper scoots closer to his sister. In the dying flashlight beam, he can see her face -- dusty, scared, but trying to smile. Always trying to smile. He puts his arm around her, and she leans into him, and they sit together in their tiny pocket of surviving space while the world ends above them and a monster laughs below.\n\n\"Hey, Dipper?\" Mabel whispers.\n\n\"Yeah?\"\n\n\"When we get out of here--\"\n\n\"If.\"\n\n\"WHEN we get out of here, I'm going to eat an entire pie. Not a slice. The whole thing. And nobody gets to judge me.\"\n\nDipper almost laughs. Almost. \"Deal.\"\n\nThe flashlight flickers once, twice, and dies. The darkness is total. The laughter continues. Somewhere above them, separated by fifty feet of rock and the end of reality itself, the sky stays broken, and Bill Cipher's party rages on, and nobody knows that two kids are buried alive in a forgotten bunker, holding onto each other in the dark, running out of air, running out of time, still making promises about pie.\n\nThe rubble doesn't shift. The air gets thinner. The laughter gets closer.\n\nSome traps don't have exits. Some collapses don't have survivors. And some choices, made in desperation, have consequences that can't be undone by bravery or cleverness or love.\n\nThe darkness is very, very patient.",
    ending: true
  },

  ending_bunker_portal: {
    text: "\"We have to try,\" Mabel says, and there's something fierce in her voice, something that sounds like their Grunkle Stan on his best days -- the stubborn refusal to accept that a bad idea is actually bad when there's no good ideas left. \"We can't stay down here. We can't go up. The only way out is THROUGH.\"\n\nDipper knows she's right. He also knows that activating an uncalibrated dimensional portal during Weirdmageddon, when the barriers between dimensions are already shattered, is roughly equivalent to opening an umbrella in a hurricane and hoping it will help you fly. The math doesn't work. The physics REALLY doesn't work.\n\nBut they're out of options that work.\n\nHe reads Ford's startup notes three times, committing every step to memory. The portal requires a specific boot sequence -- power cells first, then the dimensional stabilizers, then the ring magnets that create the actual tear in spacetime. Ford's notes are covered in corrections and warnings: CALIBRATION ESSENTIAL. WITHOUT PROPER COORDINATES, DESTINATION IS RANDOM. I CANNOT STRESS THIS ENOUGH -- THIS DEVICE SHOULD NEVER BE ACTIVATED WITHOUT A FULL DIMENSIONAL LOCK.\n\nDipper ignores the warnings because what else can he do?\n\nThe portal hums to life. The ring of dark metal begins to spin, slowly at first, then faster, the symbols carved into its surface glowing with a light that shifts through every color in the spectrum and several that aren't. The air in the chamber changes, becoming heavy, electric, tasting of copper and distant stars. The crystals in the walls pulse in resonance, and the whole room vibrates with a frequency that Dipper can feel in his bones.\n\n\"It's working!\" Mabel shouts over the rising hum.\n\nThe center of the ring tears. There's no other word for it -- reality rips like wet paper, and through the tear, they can see... something. Not a destination. Not a world. A churning, spiraling maelstrom of color and light and darkness all mixed together, like someone put the universe in a blender and hit \"puree.\" It's beautiful and terrible, and it's hungry.\n\nThe portal was supposed to create a stable doorway between Point A and Point B. Without calibration, without coordinates, without a dimensional anchor, what it creates instead is a doorway between Point A and EVERYTHING. Every dimension, every reality, every possibility, all trying to push through a single opening at the same time.\n\nAnd it's already unstable when Weirdmageddon's energy hits it.\n\nThe fractured sky above Gravity Falls has been pumping chaos energy into the environment for days. That energy finds the portal like lightning finds a metal rod -- the path of least resistance. The uncalibrated tear becomes a FUNNEL, sucking in the dimensional chaos and amplifying it, turning a small hole between worlds into a screaming vortex that begins to pull at everything in the chamber.\n\nPapers fly. Equipment slides across the floor. The corkboard with Ford's Axolotl research rips free from the wall and vanishes into the maelstrom. Dipper grabs the desk, his feet leaving the floor, the portal's pull relentless and growing.\n\n\"SHUT IT DOWN!\" he screams, but Mabel is already at the control panel, slamming the emergency shutoff. Nothing happens. The portal has gone beyond the controls, feeding on Weirdmageddon's ambient chaos, growing stronger with every second. The tear widens. Through it, they can see flashes of other realities -- a dimension where everything is made of screaming mouths, a world of pure mathematics where equations drift like clouds, a reality that's entirely upside down, with oceans in the sky and cities hanging from roots.\n\n\"Dipper, I can't hold on!\" Mabel's grappling hook is anchored to the wall, the cable taut, her body horizontal in the pull. Dipper's fingers are slipping on the desk, which is itself starting to slide.\n\nThe tear reaches the ceiling. The walls. The floor. It's not just a portal anymore -- it's a wound in reality itself, and it's growing, and it's going to swallow the bunker, and then the ground above the bunker, and then whatever's above that, adding fuel to Weirdmageddon's fire. Dipper's plan to escape has instead created a new source of chaos, a dimensional breach that feeds Bill Cipher's apocalypse like gasoline on a bonfire.\n\n\"I'm sorry,\" Dipper whispers, but Mabel can't hear him over the roar.\n\nThe cable snaps. The desk breaks free. The portal takes them.\n\nFalling between dimensions isn't like falling at all. It's like being everywhere at once and nowhere at all, existing in the gaps between realities where the rules haven't been written yet. Up is a suggestion. Down is a memory. Time runs sideways, then backward, then in spirals, then not at all. Dipper can see Mabel, or a version of her, reflected in a thousand fractured realities, each one slightly different -- Mabel with blue hair, Mabel as a constellation, Mabel as a song, Mabel as a mathematical concept that tastes like strawberries.\n\nHe reaches for her hand. She reaches for his. Their fingers brush, and for one moment, they're real, they're together, they're the Pines twins from Piedmont, California, who came to Gravity Falls for a summer vacation and found more than they ever bargained for.\n\nThen the fractures pull them apart, and each fragment of broken reality carries a piece of them in a different direction, and the last thing Dipper thinks before coherent thought becomes impossible is that he should have read Ford's warnings more carefully.\n\nSomewhere, in the space between every story ever told, two children drift like leaves on an infinite wind, together and apart, lost in the beautiful, terrible everything that exists between one world and the next.\n\nThey're not gone. They can't be gone. Nothing is ever truly gone in the multiverse.\n\nBut they're very, very far from home.",
    ending: true
  },

  ending_bunker_silence: {
    text: "Dipper pulls his hand back from the transmit switch. The signal pulses once more -- patient, warm, full of that impossible color that has no name -- and then, slowly, it fades. The crystal's glow dims from gold to blue to nothing. The fractal branches settle back into their resting position, inert and dark. The room's ambient warmth drops by several degrees.\n\nWhatever was on the other end of that signal -- the Axolotl, a trick, a dream, a cosmic entity older than stars -- is gone. They let it go.\n\n\"Dipper...\" Mabel's voice is small. \"Why didn't we...?\"\n\n\"Because I don't know what that was,\" Dipper says, and the words taste like ash. \"It could have been help. It could have been Bill wearing a mask. It could have been something worse than Bill. We don't know. And right now, the only thing keeping us alive is being careful.\"\n\nMabel doesn't argue. That's how Dipper knows it's bad. The Mabel who argues, who pushes back, who insists on the hopeful interpretation -- that Mabel is still in there somewhere, but she's tired. They're both tired. Tired in a way that has nothing to do with sleep and everything to do with carrying the weight of choices that have no good options.\n\nThey seal Level Four behind them. They climb back up to the habitable section of the bunker. They eat canned peaches in silence, sitting on Ford's emergency bunks under the red emergency lights, listening to the distant rumble of Weirdmageddon shaking the earth above. The peaches taste like nothing. Everything tastes like nothing.\n\nDays pass. The bunker becomes their world.\n\nDipper builds a routine because routine is the only defense against the crushing, suffocating weight of having chosen to do nothing. Wake up. Check the generator. Check the air filtration. Check the perimeter. Eat. Read Ford's notes. Sleep. Repeat. He reads the notes obsessively, looking for another way, another option, another signal. But the dimensional radio stays silent. Whatever window they had, they closed it themselves.\n\nMabel tries to keep their spirits up. She really does. She organizes a \"bunker talent show\" (audience: one). She creates \"Bunker Mabel's Fun Corner\" where she tells jokes to the canned goods. She knits a tiny sweater for a rat that lives in the walls, naming it Sir Squeaksalot and declaring it their bunker mascot. Each attempt at joy is a little more forced than the last, a little more brittle, a little closer to breaking.\n\nOne night -- they think it's night, there's no way to tell underground -- Dipper wakes up to find Mabel sitting by the sealed hatch that leads to the surface, her hand pressed against the cold metal. She's not trying to open it. She's just touching it, feeling the vibrations of the world above transmitted through fifty feet of rock and steel.\n\n\"I can hear them,\" she whispers when she notices Dipper watching. \"Sometimes. If I press my ear to the metal, I can hear voices. I think it's Wendy. Or Soos. Or... someone. They're fighting, Dipper. They're up there fighting, and we're down here hiding.\"\n\n\"We're surviving,\" Dipper says.\n\n\"Is that enough?\"\n\nHe doesn't answer, because the honest answer is no and the kind answer is yes and he can't decide which one she needs to hear.\n\nWeeks pass. They lose count of exactly how many. The bunker's supplies hold. The generator keeps running. The air stays breathable. They are, by every measurable standard, alive and safe. Ford designed this bunker to sustain life indefinitely, and it does its job with mechanical indifference.\n\nBut safety, it turns out, has a cost that isn't measured in food or power or air. The cost is measured in the light that slowly leaves Mabel's eyes. In the way Dipper stops reading Ford's notes because he's memorized every page and none of them contain the answer he needs. In the silence that stretches between conversations, growing longer each day, filling with all the things they can't say to each other.\n\nI'm scared. I think we made the wrong choice. I think we could have saved everyone and we chose to save ourselves instead.\n\nThe signal never comes back. Whatever the Axolotl was -- hope, help, salvation -- it offered once and they refused, and the universe, vast and patient and indifferent, moved on to other things. Other chances. Other children who might have said yes.\n\nAbove them, Weirdmageddon rages, and burns, and eventually, as all fires do, begins to fade. They don't know this. They can't see it. They're safe in their bunker, alive in their bunker, imprisoned in their bunker, listening to the muffled echoes of a war they chose not to fight.\n\nSafe. Silent. Surviving.\n\nIt's the kind of ending where nobody dies and nothing is okay. The kind of ending where the heroes are still breathing but have forgotten what breathing is for. The kind of ending that's worse than a bad ending because at least bad endings have the decency to be final.\n\nThis one just goes on and on and on, quiet and safe and empty, like a room with no windows and a door that's locked from the inside.",
    ending: true
  },

  resistance_rally: {
    text: "The Mystery Shack has never looked like this before. Gone is the ramshackle tourist trap with its crooked signs and questionable taxidermy. In its place stands a fortress - a bristling, improvised stronghold that looks like someone crossed a medieval castle with a junkyard. And honestly? That's pretty much exactly what happened.\n\nSoos, wonderful beautiful genius Soos, has spent the last three days welding every piece of scrap metal he could find onto the walls of the Shack. Old car doors, refrigerator panels, stop signs, even a satellite dish - all of it hammered and welded into overlapping armor plating. The totem pole out front has been fitted with spotlights that sweep the treeline in slow, searching arcs. The gift shop windows are blocked with sandbags filled with whatever was handy, which in the Mystery Shack means a lot of bags stuffed with fake gold nuggets and bobblehead dolls.\n\nUp on the roof, Wendy Corduroy stands like a sentinel against the bleeding red sky, her red hair whipping in the unnatural wind. She's traded her lumberjack hat for a pair of welding goggles pushed up on her forehead, and she holds a crossbow that she apparently built herself out of a curtain rod and industrial rubber bands. Every few minutes, an eye-bat swoops too close, and Wendy sends a bolt whistling into the darkness. She hasn't missed yet. Not once.\n\nInside, the Shack is packed with refugees. Lazy Susan huddles near the vending machine, clutching a rolling pin like a weapon and muttering about how her diner is probably in another dimension by now. Manly Dan sits in the corner, sharpening an axe the size of a small child and looking like he's been waiting his entire life for the apocalypse. Toby Determined is trying to report on the situation into a broken microphone, interviewing anyone who will hold still long enough. A cluster of gnomes has taken up residence under the kitchen table - they showed up demanding sanctuary three hours ago, and while nobody trusts them exactly, Jeff the gnome leader made a surprisingly compelling case that even gnomes don't want to live in a world ruled by a triangle.\n\nGrunkle Stan is in the middle of it all, pacing back and forth in front of his old TV set, which now shows nothing but static and occasional flashes of Bill Cipher's laughing face. Stan's wearing his usual suit, but he's added a football helmet and what appears to be shoulder pads made from cookie sheets. He's barking orders, organizing supply runs, settling disputes between the gnomes and the townsfolk, and generally acting like the world's most unlikely military commander. And somehow, impossibly, people are listening to him. Because when the world ends, sometimes what you need isn't a hero. Sometimes you need a stubborn old man who's too ornery to know when he's beaten.\n\nSomewhere below, in the basement that most people don't even know exists, Great Uncle Ford is working on something. The sounds drift up through the floorboards - the hum of machinery, the crackle of energy, the occasional small explosion followed by Ford muttering in a language that definitely isn't English. He hasn't come upstairs in two days. Soos has been leaving sandwiches outside the basement door. Some of them have been eaten.\n\nStan and Ford aren't speaking to each other. Again. Or maybe still. It's hard to tell with those two whether any given silence is a new argument or a continuation of a thirty-year-old one. The tension between them fills the Shack like a second atmosphere, thick and heavy, and everyone can feel it.\n\nWhen Dipper and Mabel push through the reinforced front door, the whole room seems to exhale. Soos nearly crushes them in a hug. Wendy calls down from the roof with a whoop. Even the gnomes cheer, though Jeff immediately follows it up with a list of demands. Stan's face does something complicated - relief and terror and love all fighting for space on features that are more used to expressing suspicion and mild contempt. He grabs both twins and holds them, just for a second, before pulling back and gruffly asking what took them so long.\n\nThe Shack is surrounded. Bill's forces patrol the skies. The clock is ticking on whatever Bill is planning next. But for right now, in this moment, the family is together. And that has to count for something.",
    choices: [
      { label: "Talk to Grunkle Stan about the battle plan", target: "resistance_stan" },
      { label: "Find Great Uncle Ford in the basement", target: "resistance_ford" },
      { label: "Help Wendy with perimeter defense", target: "resistance_wendy" }
    ]
  },

  resistance_stan: {
    text: "Stan pulls the twins into the back office - the one he used to use for doing the Shack's taxes, which mostly involved creative fiction and a shredder. He's cleared off the desk and covered it with a map of Gravity Falls that's been marked up with red pen, X's, circles, and arrows. It looks like the planning board of a man who has watched way too many war movies and understood about half of what he saw.\n\n\"Alright, kids, here's the deal,\" Stan says, cracking his knuckles. He jabs a thick finger at the center of the map, where the Fearamid hovers above what used to be the town square. \"That big ugly triangle sits up there in his fancy pyramid, laughing at us. Every day he gets stronger. Every day more of our world turns into his personal nightmare playground. So the way I see it, we've got one option.\" He slams his fist on the desk. \"We punch him. Right in his big stupid eye.\"\n\nHe pulls a tarp off something in the corner, revealing a collection of blueprints that are half Soos's careful engineering drawings and half Stan's crayon additions. The plans show the Mystery Shack itself, but transformed - legs attached to the foundation, arms made from construction equipment, the roof reconfigured into what appears to be a giant fist.\n\n\"Soos and I have been working on this baby for days,\" Stan says, and there's genuine pride in his voice. \"We're calling it the Shacktron. We take the whole building - every board, every beam, every piece of that stupid totem pole - and we turn it into the biggest, ugliest, most beautiful giant robot you've ever seen. Then we walk it right up to Bill's front door and we knock.\" He mimes punching. \"Real hard.\"\n\nThe plans are insane. They're also, somehow, detailed enough to maybe actually work. Soos has figured out how to use the Shack's existing plumbing as hydraulic lines. The dinosaur fossils from the tourist exhibits would form part of the skeleton. The T-Rex skull would be one of the hands. Duct tape is listed as a structural component in at least fourteen places.\n\n\"Grunkle Stan,\" Dipper says carefully, \"this is... amazing. But Bill is basically a god right now. He can warp reality just by thinking about it. How is a giant robot made of wood and duct tape supposed to fight that?\"\n\nStan's jaw tightens. \"Because sometimes the universe doesn't need fancy science or magic spells or whatever nerd stuff Ford's cooking up down there. Sometimes it just needs someone too stubborn to quit.\" He pauses, and for a moment the bravado slips and something rawer shows through. \"Listen, kid. I spent thirty years trying to bring my brother back. Thirty years of people telling me it was impossible. And I did it anyway. Not because I'm smart, not because I'm special, but because I don't know how to give up. And I'm not about to learn now.\"\n\nHe straightens up, adjusting his cookie-sheet shoulder pads. \"Ford's down there with his gadgets and his equations, and he thinks that's gonna save us. But I've been in fights my whole life - real fights, dirty fights, the kind where the other guy is bigger and meaner and you've got nothing going for you except the fact that you're willing to bite. And I've won more than I've lost.\" He grins, and it's the grin of a man who has been punched in the face professionally. \"So here's the plan: we build the Shacktron, we load everyone in, and we march on that Fearamid. The whole town. Together.\"\n\nMabel looks at the blueprints, then at Stan. \"What does Ford think about this plan?\"\n\nStan's expression closes like a slammed door. \"Ford thinks we should sit in the basement and zap Bill with some science gizmo. Ford thinks brute force is beneath him. Ford thinks-\" He stops himself, takes a breath. \"Ford and I don't agree on strategy. Big surprise, right? We don't agree on anything. Never have.\" There's old pain there, decades of it, compressed into a few clipped words. \"But I'm telling you kids, sometimes the simplest plan is the best plan. Hit him hard. Hit him fast. Don't give him time to think.\"\n\nThe office is quiet except for the distant sound of eye-bats shrieking outside and the hum of Ford's machines below. Two brothers, two plans, and the fate of reality hanging in the balance.",
    choices: [
      { label: "Support Stan's Shacktron assault plan", target: "resistance_shacktron" },
      { label: "Try to get Stan and Ford to work together", target: "resistance_unite" }
    ]
  },

  resistance_ford: {
    text: "The basement of the Mystery Shack has always been strange - a hidden laboratory beneath a tourist trap, full of alien technology and impossible science. But Ford has outdone himself this time. The space has been transformed into something that looks like a mad scientist's fever dream crossed with a college dorm room during finals week.\n\nEvery surface is covered with equations. The walls, the floor, the ceiling - Ford has written on all of them in a frantic, spidery hand, using markers, chalk, and in one section what appears to be mustard. Diagrams of Bill Cipher are pinned everywhere, annotated with measurements and calculations. A web of string connects various notes like the world's most paranoid bulletin board. Empty coffee cups form small pyramids on every flat surface, which would be ironic given the circumstances if anyone had time for irony.\n\nAt the center of it all, Great Uncle Ford hunches over a workbench, his six-fingered hands moving with precise, desperate speed. He's assembling something - a device about the size of a fire extinguisher, covered in crystals and wrapped in copper wire. It hums faintly, and the air around it shimmers like heat off summer pavement.\n\n\"Dipper! Mabel!\" Ford looks up, and the twins are struck by how exhausted he looks. His eyes are ringed with dark circles, his coat is singed in several places, and his hair is doing things that hair shouldn't be able to do. But his eyes are bright - burning with the same fierce intelligence that has driven him through thirty years of dimensional exile and a lifetime of scientific obsession. \"Thank the multiverse you're safe. Come in, come in - but don't touch the blue wire. Or the red wire. Actually, don't touch any of the wires.\"\n\nHe gestures them closer to the device. \"I've been working on this for seventy-two hours straight. I call it the Quantum Destabilizer. It's designed to exploit a fundamental weakness in Bill's molecular structure.\" He pulls up a holographic display showing Bill's form, rendered in wireframe. \"You see, Bill is powerful - unimaginably powerful within his domain. He can reshape matter, manipulate minds, bend the laws of physics like wet noodles. But he is not omnipotent. He's bound by certain cosmic laws that even he cannot break.\"\n\nFord taps the display, zooming in on the wireframe. \"Bill exists as a being of pure energy occupying a geometric form. That form - the triangle - isn't arbitrary. It's a containment structure. Bill is essentially a prisoner of his own shape. The Quantum Destabilizer is designed to disrupt that containment, to make the walls of his prison shake. It won't destroy him - I don't think anything in our dimension can truly destroy Bill - but it can weaken him. Make him vulnerable. Maybe vulnerable enough to defeat.\"\n\n\"But there's a problem,\" Dipper guesses, because with Ford there's always a problem.\n\nFord sighs, pulling off his glasses and rubbing his eyes with all six fingers of one hand. \"The device requires a power source that I cannot synthesize in this laboratory. It needs a sample of pure Weirdmageddon energy - the raw, unfiltered essence of the dimensional rift that Bill tore open. It's the same energy that's warping our reality, and I need a concentrated sample of it to calibrate the Destabilizer's frequency to match Bill's resonance.\"\n\nHe gestures to a containment unit on the bench - a crystalline jar surrounded by magnetic coils. \"I built this to hold the sample. But someone would have to go out there, into the chaos, and collect it. The highest concentrations would be near the center of town, closest to the Fearamid.\"\n\nFord pauses, and his expression changes - becomes distant, almost reverent. \"There is... another possibility. Something I encountered in my travels through the multiverse. A being called the Axolotl.\" He says the name carefully, as if it might be listening. \"It exists outside Bill's influence - outside the normal dimensional hierarchy entirely. The Axolotl is ancient, perhaps the most ancient thing in all of existence. Some dimensions worship it as a god. Others don't even have a name for it.\" He lowers his voice. \"I believe the Axolotl may be the only entity in all of creation that Bill Cipher genuinely fears. If we could contact it, commune with it somehow... it might be able to help us in ways that science cannot.\"\n\nHe shakes his head, as if clearing away a dream. \"But that's speculation. Theoretical at best. The Destabilizer is concrete. It's science. And science is what I trust.\" He looks at the twins, and beneath the exhaustion there's something that might be hope. \"So. What do you think?\"",
    choices: [
      { label: "Volunteer to go get the Weirdmageddon energy", target: "resistance_mission" },
      { label: "Ask Ford more about the Axolotl", target: "axolotl_search" }
    ]
  },

  resistance_wendy: {
    text: "The ladder to the roof is a repurposed telephone pole lashed to the side of the Shack with bungee cords and what Dipper sincerely hopes is very strong rope. Climbing it in the dark, with the red-tinged sky swirling overhead and the distant screech of eye-bats echoing off the mountains, is one of the more terrifying things the twins have done today. And today has included a lot of terrifying things.\n\nWendy is waiting at the top, crouched behind a barricade made of filing cabinets and old Mystery Shack merchandise displays. She's silhouetted against the nightmare sky, crossbow resting on her shoulder, looking like some kind of post-apocalyptic lumberjack warrior. Which, Dipper reflects, is basically what she is now.\n\n\"Hey dudes,\" she says, as casually as if they'd just walked into the gift shop on a normal summer day. \"Welcome to the penthouse. Watch your step - there's a loose board near the chimney, and the last thing we need is someone falling through the roof into Grunkle Stan's oatmeal.\"\n\nThe roof has been transformed into a watchtower. Wendy has set up a perimeter of trip wires connected to cans full of bolts - a low-tech alarm system that rattles if anything tries to land on the building. She's got three crossbows positioned at different angles, a bucket of handmade bolts, and a pair of binoculars she liberated from what used to be the Gravity Falls ranger station. A walkie-talkie sits nearby, crackling with occasional static.\n\n\"So here's the situation,\" Wendy says, scanning the sky as she talks. Her voice is steady, matter-of-fact, the voice of someone who has already processed the horror and moved straight into problem-solving. \"Bill's got eye-bats on regular patrol routes. I've been tracking them for two days. They sweep the forest in a grid pattern, about twenty minutes between passes over any given spot. That gives us windows to move.\"\n\nShe pulls out a hand-drawn map - messier than Ford's scientific diagrams, more practical than Stan's war plans. It shows Gravity Falls with patrol routes marked in green, safe zones in blue, and danger areas in red. Most of the map is red.\n\n\"We're not the only ones who survived,\" Wendy continues. \"There are pockets of people all over town. I've been doing runs - sneaking out during the patrol gaps, making contact, bringing people back or dropping off supplies.\" She ticks off on her fingers. \"There's a group in the elementary school basement. Another bunch hiding in the water tower. The Northwest family barricaded themselves in their mansion, because of course they did. And there's a big group trapped in the movie theater - Gideon's prison, actually. He's been keeping people locked up there for Bill.\"\n\nHer jaw tightens at the mention of Gideon. \"That little creep switched sides the second Bill showed up. He's running around town like he owns the place, rounding up people for Bill's collection. Makes me sick.\"\n\nAn eye-bat swoops in from the east, its enormous lidless eye scanning the ground below. Wendy raises her crossbow in one fluid motion, tracks the creature for half a second, and fires. The bolt catches the eye-bat right in the pupil. It shrieks, spirals, and crashes into the treeline. Wendy is already reloading.\n\n\"Anyway,\" she says, as if she didn't just snipe a nightmare creature out of the sky mid-conversation, \"I've got two ideas and I need help with both. First: a big rescue mission. The movie theater group is the largest - maybe thirty people trapped there, guarded by some of Bill's weaker minions. If we could free them and bring them here, we'd double our numbers. But it means going deep into town, past heavy patrol routes.\"\n\nShe shifts, turning to face the twins directly. \"Second: we need more fighters. Human refugees are great, but if we're going to take on Bill, we need firepower. Supernatural firepower. The forest is full of creatures who are just as threatened by Weirdmageddon as we are. Gnomes, Manotaurs, the Multi-Bear, all of them. If someone could get out there and convince them to join us...\" She shrugs. \"Well, it'd be like herding cats. Weird, magical, occasionally violent cats. But it might give us an actual army.\"\n\nShe looks at Dipper and Mabel with an expression that is completely devoid of the casual cool she usually projects. In its place is something raw and honest. \"I'm not gonna sugarcoat it, guys. Both options are dangerous. Everything is dangerous right now. But sitting here and waiting for Bill to come to us is the most dangerous option of all. So what do you think? Rescue mission or recruitment drive?\"",
    choices: [
      { label: "Join Wendy's guerrilla rescue mission", target: "resistance_guerrilla" },
      { label: "Help Wendy recruit more fighters", target: "resistance_recruit" }
    ]
  },

  resistance_shacktron: {
    text: "Building the Shacktron takes eighteen hours. Eighteen hours of hammering, welding, bolting, duct-taping, and occasionally praying. Soos is the chief engineer, and he approaches the task with the same reverent dedication he brings to everything he loves - which means he talks to the building components like they're old friends. \"Come on, little rafter, you can do it,\" he murmurs, fitting a ceiling beam into place as a mechanical joint. \"I believe in you, dude.\"\n\nThe Mystery Shack comes apart and goes back together like the world's most insane jigsaw puzzle. The foundation cracks free from the earth, revealing the massive mechanical legs that Soos and Stan have been secretly building for days. They're made from construction equipment scavenged from an abandoned work site - excavator arms serve as the hip joints, bulldozer treads wrap around the calves for traction. The Shack itself forms the torso, its walls groaning but holding as hydraulic systems pressurize and the building rises, impossibly, off the ground.\n\nThe arms are something else entirely. The right arm is a crane boom fitted with the T-Rex skull from the Shack's exhibit - five feet of fossilized jaw that can open and close with enough force to crush a car. The left arm ends in a massive fist assembled from engine blocks and wrapped in chain link fencing. The roof sprouts antenna arrays that Ford grudgingly helped wire into a sensor system, and the totem pole is mounted on the back like a spine, its carved faces now serving as rear-view cameras.\n\nAnd yes, there is duct tape. So much duct tape. Roughly one hundred and forty-seven rolls of it, holding together joints, sealing gaps, reinforcing connections that probably should have been welded but weren't because they ran out of welding rod. Soos assures everyone that he used the industrial grade stuff, as if that makes a giant robot held together with tape any less terrifying.\n\nWhen the Shacktron takes its first step, the ground shakes. Inside the control room - formerly the living room, now fitted with a bewildering array of levers, pedals, and one repurposed arcade joystick - Stan grips the main controls and grins like a kid on Christmas morning. \"Now THIS is what I'm talking about!\" he roars. The Shacktron raises its fist to the burning sky, and the townsfolk crammed into every available space cheer so hard the walls rattle.\n\nThey march toward the Fearamid. The Shacktron moves with a lurching, ungainly gait, each step crushing pavement and leaving craters. Trees snap against its legs like twigs. Inside, it sounds like being in the belly of a mechanical whale - groaning metal, hissing hydraulics, the constant creak of wood under stresses it was never meant to endure.\n\nThe eye-bats come first. A wave of them, dozens strong, diving at the Shacktron like enormous angry houseflies. Wendy is on the roof platform, picking them off with her crossbow, but there are too many. They splat against the armor plating, their acidic tears eating into the metal. The Shacktron swats at them with its T-Rex hand, catching three at once in the fossil jaws, but more keep coming.\n\nThen the ground troops arrive - Bill's weirdness minions, creatures born from pure nightmare energy. Giant floating teeth. Sentient geometric shapes. Things that look like they crawled out of a Salvador Dali painting if Salvador Dali had been having a really bad day. They swarm the Shacktron's legs, trying to bring it down.\n\nStan wrestles with the controls, keeping the Shacktron moving forward. A warning light that Soos helpfully labeled \"BAD STUFF HAPPENING\" starts flashing. The left knee joint is taking damage. The T-Rex skull has a crack forming along the jaw. One of the hydraulic lines is leaking, leaving a trail of fluid like mechanical blood.\n\nThe Fearamid looms ahead, impossibly large, its single eye glowing like a malevolent sun. They can see Bill inside, a dark triangular shape watching their approach with what is unmistakably amusement.\n\nThe Shacktron shudders as another wave of eye-bats slams into it. A piece of armor plating tears free and spins away into the chaos. Inside, Dipper grabs a support beam as the floor tilts. The \"BAD STUFF HAPPENING\" light is now joined by another one labeled \"REALLY BAD STUFF HAPPENING.\"\n\nThey're close. But the Shacktron is falling apart around them.",
    choices: [
      { label: "Press forward to the Fearamid despite damage", target: "resistance_charge" },
      { label: "Retreat and repair", target: "resistance_retreat" }
    ]
  },

  resistance_unite: {
    text: "Getting Stan and Ford in the same room is the easy part. They're both already in the Shack, after all. Getting them to actually talk to each other without it devolving into a shouting match - that's the challenge that makes fighting Bill Cipher look like a cakewalk.\n\nDipper and Mabel find Stan in the gift shop, aggressively organizing ammunition. They find Ford in the basement, aggressively calibrating equipment. Both of them use the word \"fine\" when asked how they're doing, in that particular tone that means the exact opposite of fine. The twins exchange a look. Mabel nods. Operation Family Reunion is a go.\n\nThey lure both men to the kitchen using different pretexts - Dipper tells Ford that there's an anomalous energy reading near the refrigerator, while Mabel tells Stan that someone found a cache of snack foods. When the two brothers round the corner and see each other, the temperature in the room drops by about ten degrees.\n\n\"I have nothing to say to you,\" Stan growls, turning to leave.\n\n\"The feeling is entirely mutual,\" Ford replies, also turning to leave.\n\nMabel steps in front of the door. All four feet and eleven inches of her, wearing a sweater with a cat on it, blocking the exit like a very small, very sparkly bouncer. \"Nobody leaves this kitchen until you two figure out how to save the world together.\"\n\n\"Kid, get out of the way-\"\n\n\"Nope.\"\n\n\"Mabel, this is hardly the time for-\"\n\n\"It's EXACTLY the time. Sit down. Both of you.\"\n\nSomething in Mabel's voice - maybe the fact that it doesn't waver, that there's no laughter or silliness in it for once - makes them listen. They sit on opposite sides of the kitchen table, arms crossed, jaws set, looking anywhere but at each other. Thirty years of resentment fills the space between them like an invisible wall.\n\nDipper lays it out. Stan has the Shacktron. Ford has the Quantum Destabilizer. Separately, neither plan is enough. The Shacktron is strong but Bill is stronger. The Destabilizer might work but it has no delivery system. Together, though - a Shacktron armed with a Quantum Destabilizer - they'd have both the muscle and the precision.\n\n\"I don't need his gadget,\" Stan mutters. \"I just need to get close enough to punch-\"\n\n\"And I don't need his blunt instrument,\" Ford counters. \"If I can calibrate the Destabilizer to the correct frequency-\"\n\n\"Oh HERE we go with the frequencies-\"\n\n\"You wouldn't understand the science if I drew it in crayon, Stanley-\"\n\n\"AT LEAST I'M DOING SOMETHING INSTEAD OF HIDING IN A BASEMENT-\"\n\nThey're standing now, leaning across the table, voices rising. Decades of hurt and anger and love so tangled up it turned into hate - all of it pouring out in a kitchen that smells like old coffee and gun oil.\n\nAnd then Mabel speaks.\n\n\"You're brothers,\" she says, and her voice cracks on the word. \"You're brothers and the world is ending and you're fighting about stuff that happened before Mom and Dad were even born. I have a brother too. And if - if something happened, if we got separated for thirty years, I wouldn't spend one single second of our time together being angry. I would just be so glad to have him back.\" She's crying now, tears running down her cheeks and dripping onto her cat sweater. \"You got each other back. That's a miracle. And you're wasting it. You're wasting it because you're scared. Because it's easier to be mad than it is to say I'm sorry.\"\n\nThe kitchen is silent. Stan is staring at the table. Ford is staring at his hands - all twelve fingers spread flat on the surface, the extra digit on each hand that made him different, that started so much of the pain between them.\n\n\"She's right,\" Dipper says quietly. \"You don't have to fix everything tonight. You don't have to forgive each other for thirty years in five minutes. You just have to work together. Right now. For us. For everyone.\"\n\nStan looks at Ford. Ford looks at Stan. Something passes between them - not forgiveness, not yet, maybe not for a long time. But something. An acknowledgment. A truce. A tiny, fragile bridge thrown across a canyon that's been widening since they were seventeen years old.\n\n\"Your Shacktron,\" Ford says slowly. \"It could carry the Destabilizer. Mount it on the right arm, use the T-Rex skull as a focusing array...\"\n\n\"And your gizmo gives us an actual weapon,\" Stan admits. \"Something that might actually scratch that triangle jerk.\"\n\nThey look at each other. Stan sticks out his hand. Ford hesitates, then takes it with six fingers wrapped around five.\n\n\"Let's go save the world, Poindexter.\"\n\n\"After you, knucklehead.\"",
    choices: [
      { label: "Launch the combined assault", target: "resistance_final_battle" },
      { label: "Wait for more preparation time", target: "resistance_wait" }
    ]
  },

  resistance_mission: {
    text: "They slip out of the Mystery Shack at what should be midnight, though time doesn't mean much anymore. Wendy leads the way, moving through the forest with the silent confidence of someone who grew up climbing these trees and knows every root and hollow. Dipper follows close behind, clutching Ford's containment unit - the crystalline jar that will hold the Weirdmageddon energy sample. Mabel brings up the rear, her grappling hook at the ready, wearing her most determined expression and a sweater that reads \"APOCALYPSE BUDDIES\" in glitter letters. She knitted it during the planning session. Nobody asked how she found the time.\n\nThe forest is wrong. That's the only way to describe it. The trees are still standing, but some of them are upside down, roots clawing at the sky like bony fingers. Others have turned translucent, their trunks showing the grain of the wood like an X-ray. Occasionally they pass a tree that's just... screaming. Not in a way you can hear with your ears, but in a way you feel in your teeth and the backs of your eyeballs. Mabel pats one of these trees gently and whispers \"it's okay, tree\" as they pass.\n\nThe town is worse. They emerge from the treeline and Gravity Falls spreads before them like a painting done by someone having a nervous breakdown. Buildings float at random angles, some of them slowly rotating. The church steeple points downward. The library has turned inside out, books drifting through the air like confused birds. Main Street has become a river of something that isn't quite water - it's too thick, too luminous, and it flows uphill.\n\nWorst of all are the time bubbles. They dot the landscape like enormous soap bubbles, each one a perfect sphere of distorted air. Inside each one, a moment is trapped and repeating. Dipper sees Pacifica Northwest frozen in mid-scream, her image resetting every three seconds like a broken GIF. He sees Deputy Durland and Sheriff Blubs caught in an endless loop of trying to run, their legs pumping uselessly. He sees a family he doesn't recognize sitting down to dinner over and over and over, never reaching the table.\n\nWendy leads them around the time bubbles, marking each one on her map. \"Don't touch them,\" she warns. \"I saw a raccoon walk into one. It's still in there, eating the same garbage forever.\"\n\nThen there are the madness bubbles - smaller, pinker, and far more dangerous. These float freely, drifting on currents of warped air. One brushes past Dipper and for a terrifying instant the world turns inside out - the sky is below him, the ground above, and everything is made of eyeballs. He stumbles back, gasping, and the vision clears. \"Yeah,\" Wendy says grimly. \"Those are the bad ones.\"\n\nThey reach the town center after an hour of careful navigation. The concentration of Weirdmageddon energy is visible here - a column of swirling, crackling power that rises from a crack in the ground and spirals up toward the Fearamid high above. It's beautiful in a terrible way, like watching a nuclear explosion. Colors that shouldn't exist ripple through the column - colors that have no names, that the human eye wasn't designed to see. Looking at it too long makes Dipper's nose bleed.\n\nThe energy is raw, unstable, and powerful beyond anything Ford's instruments can measure. Ford's containment unit begins vibrating in Dipper's hands, its crystals resonating with the ambient power. This is what they came for. But getting close enough to collect a sample means walking right up to the column, right in the open, right beneath the shadow of the Fearamid itself.\n\nWendy scans the sky. \"Patrol gap in about three minutes,\" she says. \"We'll have maybe five minutes before the next eye-bat sweep. That's our window.\"\n\nMabel squeezes Dipper's hand. \"We can do this,\" she says, and he can't tell if she believes it or if she's just saying it because someone has to.\n\nThe containment unit hums in his arms. The column of Weirdmageddon energy crackles and roars. And high above, the Fearamid watches with its single burning eye.",
    choices: [
      { label: "Carefully extract the energy with Ford's device", target: "resistance_extract" },
      { label: "There's too much - they'll be overwhelmed", target: "ending_resistance_overwhelmed" }
    ]
  },

  resistance_guerrilla: {
    text: "They move through the ruins of Gravity Falls like ghosts. Wendy takes point, her crossbow a natural extension of her arms, her boots finding silent footing on debris that would crack and crumble under anyone else. She's been doing these runs for days now, and it shows - she knows which streets are patrolled, which buildings have collapsed, which shadows are deep enough to hide in when the eye-bats sweep overhead.\n\nDipper is right behind her, armed with a flashlight he's modified to switch between normal light and a black light that can detect the residue of Bill's magic. It's a small edge, but in a world where the rules have been rewritten by a chaos demon, small edges matter. Mabel flanks them, her grappling hook in one hand and a bag full of supplies in the other - food, water, first aid, and several hand-knitted sweaters because, as she puts it, \"being rescued from a nightmare dimension is no excuse for being cold.\"\n\nThe movie theater is on the far side of town, across three major patrol routes and through what used to be the commercial district. Key word: used to be. The commercial district is now a fever dream. The buildings have melted and reformed into impossible shapes - a barber shop twisted into a Mobius strip, a grocery store that exists in four dimensions simultaneously, meaning you can see inside it from every angle at once and what's inside is not groceries anymore.\n\nThey cross the first patrol route during a gap Wendy timed to the second. They cross the second by hiding inside a dumpster that, thankfully, is just a normal dumpster and not a dumpster that's been transformed into a portal to the nightmare realm. The third route is trickier - there's an eye-bat that seems to have gone off-pattern, circling lazily over the intersection like a vulture waiting for something to die.\n\nWendy studies it, muttering calculations about wind speed and bolt trajectory. Then she fires. The bolt arcs through the darkness and hits the eye-bat with a wet thwack. It drops like a stone, and they sprint across the intersection before its replacement can arrive.\n\nThe movie theater looms ahead, its marquee still displaying the last movie that was showing before Weirdmageddon: \"GRANDPA THE KID 2: GRAMPIER.\" The building itself is mostly intact - Bill's forces have been using it as a holding pen, so they've kept it in one piece. Through the grimy front windows, Dipper can see shapes moving inside. People. Prisoners.\n\nBut standing guard outside, illuminated by the sickly glow of a Weirdmageddon energy lamp, is something much worse than eye-bats. It's one of Bill's Henchmaniacs - one of the interdimensional nightmares that Bill brought through the rift to serve as his inner circle. This one is 8-Ball, and he is everything his name fails to prepare you for.\n\n8-Ball is massive - twelve feet tall, with a body like a gorilla made of shadow and malice. His head is dominated by two enormous eyeballs that look exactly like the 8-ball from a pool table - black and white, with that little circle and the number eight, except these eyes move and focus and radiate an intelligence that is ancient and cruel. His mouth is too wide, filled with teeth that are also billiard balls, and when he grins, which he does a lot, it's the grin of something that has eaten civilizations for breakfast.\n\nHe's sitting on a throne he's built out of people. Living people, frozen by some hideous magic into rigid poses, stacked and arranged like furniture. A man bent into the shape of a footstool. A woman arched into an armrest. Two teenagers fused together into a coffee table. They're alive - Dipper can see their eyes moving, wide with terror - but they can't speak or move.\n\nMabel makes a small sound of horror. Wendy's grip on her crossbow tightens until her knuckles go white.\n\n\"Okay,\" Wendy whispers, her voice flat with controlled rage. \"New plan. We free those people too.\" She looks at the twins, her green eyes hard. \"Question is - do we fight that thing head-on, or try to sneak past it and free the prisoners inside first?\"\n\nInside the theater, thirty people wait for rescue. Outside, 8-Ball sits on his throne of living furniture and picks his teeth with what appears to be a parking meter. The night stretches around them, full of red shadows and impossible stars.",
    choices: [
      { label: "Fight 8-Ball to free the prisoners", target: "resistance_8ball_fight" },
      { label: "Sneak past 8-Ball", target: "ending_resistance_eyebats" }
    ]
  },

  resistance_recruit: {
    text: "Recruiting an army from the supernatural creatures of Gravity Falls sounded like a great idea in the safety of the Mystery Shack. In practice, it's like trying to organize a school field trip where every student is a different species and they all hate each other.\n\nThe gnomes are first. Jeff and his colony have been hiding in the hollowed-out stump they call home, which has miraculously survived Weirdmageddon mostly intact, probably because even cosmic chaos finds gnomes too annoying to bother with. When Dipper and Mabel arrive, the gnomes are in the middle of an emergency council meeting, which consists of several hundred tiny bearded men arguing at the top of their tiny lungs.\n\n\"We want territorial guarantees,\" Jeff says immediately, crossing his tiny arms. \"If we fight, we want the northwest quadrant of the forest. Exclusive gnome territory. No humans, no Manotaurs, definitely no unicorns. We also want dental coverage.\"\n\n\"Jeff, the world is ending,\" Dipper says.\n\n\"All the more reason to get it in writing, kid. You think gnome lawyers grow on trees?\" He pauses. \"Actually, Gerald does grow on trees. He's a weird gnome. BUT THAT'S NOT THE POINT.\"\n\nAfter forty-five minutes of negotiation that makes Dipper want to scream into the void, the gnomes agree to join - provisionally. They form up in their giant gnome formation, which is basically all of them stacking on top of each other to make one big gnome. It's about fifteen feet tall and held together entirely by stubbornness and interlocking beards.\n\nThe Manotaurs are next. These are the half-man, half-bull creatures that live in the mountains, whose entire culture revolves around displays of toughness. Their leader, Leaderar (they're not creative with names), listens to the twins' pitch while bench-pressing a boulder.\n\n\"We will fight,\" Leaderar declares, slamming the boulder down hard enough to crack the ground. \"But only in single combat. One-on-one. Mano-a-mano. Or in our case, Manotaur-a-whatever.\"\n\n\"Bill has an army of thousands,\" Dipper points out.\n\n\"Then we will fight thousands of single combats!\" Leaderar flexes so hard his fur ripples. \"SEQUENTIALLY!\"\n\nDipper decides to take what he can get.\n\nThe Multi-Bear agrees to help almost immediately, because the Multi-Bear is a reasonable being who understands that the end of all reality is bad for everyone. He joins the group with quiet dignity, all seven of his heads nodding in solemn agreement, while his bear body lumbers along and the one head that really likes the band Babba hums softly to itself.\n\nThe Hide-Behind - that mysterious creature that's always just out of sight, hiding behind trees and telephone poles - is harder to recruit because they literally can't find it. They know it's there because they can hear it breathing, and occasionally a tree will rustle for no reason, but every time they turn around it's behind them, and when they turn back it's behind them again. Eventually Mabel just shouts her pitch to the empty forest and something invisible makes a sound that might be agreement.\n\nAnd then there are the unicorns.\n\nThe unicorns are the worst.\n\nCelestabellabethabelle, the lead unicorn, tosses her rainbow mane and laughs when Mabel explains the situation. \"Oh, sweetie,\" she says in a voice dripping with condescension. \"That sounds like a YOU problem. We're unicorns. We're too pure and beautiful for war.\"\n\n\"Your forest is on fire,\" Mabel points out.\n\n\"It's a METAPHORICAL fire.\"\n\n\"It is a LITERAL fire. I can see it from here. Those are actual flames.\"\n\nThe unicorn glances over her shoulder at the very real flames consuming the edge of the enchanted grove, then turns back with a dazzling smile. \"We'll be sending thoughts and prayers. Mostly thoughts. Actually, mostly prayers. Actually, mostly just vibes. Good vibes only, sweetie!\"\n\nMabel's eye twitches. She takes a deep breath. Behind her, Dipper quietly updates his notebook: \"Unicorns: still the worst.\"\n\nThe assembled army - gnomes, Manotaurs, the Multi-Bear, presumably the Hide-Behind, and zero unicorns - gathers at the edge of the forest. They're ridiculous. They're absurd. A stack of gnomes, a herd of muscular bull-men who insist on fighting one at a time, a seven-headed bear, and an invisible thing. Against a reality-warping triangle demon and his army of interdimensional nightmares.\n\nBut they showed up. When the world was ending, they showed up.",
    choices: [
      { label: "Convince them by showing them what Bill will do to their homes too", target: "resistance_army" },
      { label: "Give up and go back to the Shack", target: "resistance_rally" }
    ]
  },

  resistance_charge: {
    text: "\"FULL SPEED AHEAD!\" Stan roars, slamming the throttle lever forward with both hands. The Shacktron lurches, groans, and charges. Every joint screams in protest. The left leg is dragging, the hydraulic fluid is pooling on the floor of the control room, and at least three of the warning lights have burned out entirely from overuse. But the Shacktron moves, because the Shacktron is made of the Mystery Shack, and the Mystery Shack has always been too stubborn to fall down.\n\nThe Fearamid looms above them like a mountain of nightmares. Its surface ripples and shifts, bricks rearranging themselves into new configurations, windows opening and closing like blinking eyes. The single great eye at the top swivels and focuses on the approaching Shacktron, and the pupil dilates with what can only be described as delight.\n\nBill Cipher's voice booms across the landscape, amplified to the volume of a thunderstorm, each word shaking the broken ground beneath them. \"WELL WELL WELL! LOOK WHO BUILT THEMSELVES A LITTLE TOY! OH, THIS IS PRECIOUS. REALLY. I'M GENUINELY ENTERTAINED. THAT ALMOST NEVER HAPPENS ANYMORE!\"\n\nHe snaps his fingers - the sound echoes like a cannon shot - and the sky splits open. Not the way it split for Weirdmageddon, with the rift and the bleeding colors. This is different. This is a door, and through it pours Bill's army. All of it. Every nightmare, every monster, every impossible horror that Bill dragged through from the dimension of his exile. Teeth and Keyhole and Hectorgon and Pyronica and Paci-Fire and Kryptos and all the others, the named ones and the nameless ones, the ones that have shapes and the ones that are just screaming.\n\nThey hit the Shacktron like a wave hitting a sandcastle.\n\nThe T-Rex skull arm is the first to go. A creature made entirely of mouths latches onto it and bites through the fossil bone like it's made of chalk. The arm tears free at the shoulder and crashes to the ground, taking a chunk of the Shack's east wall with it. Wind howls through the gap, carrying the smell of ozone and something that might be brimstone.\n\nThe left leg, already damaged, buckles under the weight of a dozen eye-bats that have fused together into a single enormous mass of wings and watching eyes. The Shacktron staggers, catches itself on its remaining arm, and keeps moving forward in a desperate three-limbed crawl. Inside, people are thrown against walls. The gnomes tumble like bowling pins. Manly Dan catches Lazy Susan as she falls and sets her upright without breaking stride.\n\nStan is still at the controls, hauling on levers that are bending under the strain. His football helmet is cracked. There's blood on his face from a cut above his eye. He doesn't care. He can see the Fearamid, right there, close enough to touch, and Stanley Pines did not survive thirty years of guilt and loneliness and sleeping on a cot in a secret basement to give up when he's THIS CLOSE.\n\nBut the Shacktron is dying. Everyone can feel it. The building that has been their home, their fortress, their last sanctuary - it's coming apart around them. The walls crack. The floor tilts. The mechanical systems whine and fail one by one, like instruments in an orchestra dropping out until only a single violin remains.\n\nThe final blow comes from Bill himself. He extends one finger - one tiny black finger on his tiny black hand - and fires a beam of pure chaos energy. It hits the Shacktron dead center. The Shack splits in half. The two halves topple in opposite directions, spilling people and gnomes and broken machinery across the blasted landscape.\n\nBill floats down from the Fearamid, spinning his cane, his single eye crinkled in amusement. \"A FOR EFFORT!\" he announces to the stunned, scattered survivors. \"F FOR EVERYTHING ELSE! NOW HOLD STILL - I WANT TO GET YOUR POSES JUST RIGHT.\"\n\nHis eye glows gold, and one by one, the survivors freeze. Not in ice. In gold. Solid, gleaming gold, their expressions locked in whatever they were feeling at the moment of transformation - terror, defiance, grief, anger. Stan is frozen mid-swing, his fist raised toward Bill in one final act of useless, beautiful stubbornness.\n\nBill arranges them around his throne room like trophies. He puts Stan and Ford on either side of his throne, facing away from each other. He poses Dipper mid-trip, one foot forward, arms pinwheeling. He puts Mabel's golden form in a display case with a label that reads \"SHOOTING STAR - DO NOT TOUCH.\" He poses Soos giving a thumbs-up and Wendy in a dab because Bill thinks dabs are hilarious.\n\nIn the silence of the Fearamid's throne room, surrounded by golden statues that used to be heroes, Bill Cipher sits on his throne, puts his feet up on a frozen Lazy Susan, and laughs and laughs and laughs.",
    ending: true
  },

  resistance_retreat: {
    text: "\"PULL BACK!\" Ford's voice crackles through the Shacktron's intercom. \"THE STRUCTURAL INTEGRITY IS BELOW CRITICAL! WE HAVE TO RETREAT!\"\n\nStan's hands tighten on the controls. Every fiber of his being screams to keep going, to push forward, to close that last distance to the Fearamid and land at least one good punch on Bill Cipher's smug triangular face. But he looks back at the people packed into the Shacktron's interior - the townsfolk, the gnomes, the twins, all of them bouncing and sliding as the mechanical building lurches and groans - and something in his chest overrides his stubbornness. Something stronger than anger.\n\nThe Shacktron turns. It's agonizing to watch - the giant robot pivoting on its damaged legs, the broken arm dragging, every step a negotiation between failing machinery and the remnants of duct tape. Behind them, Bill's forces howl in triumph, surging forward like a tide of nightmares.\n\nThey're not going to make it. Dipper can see the math. The Shacktron is too slow, too damaged, and Bill's army is too fast. They'll be overtaken before they reach the forest, let alone the bunker. Someone has to buy time.\n\nStan sees it too. He's always been better at math than he lets on - you can't run cons for thirty years without being able to calculate odds. He does the calculation now, in the time it takes to draw a breath, and he doesn't like the answer. But he accepts it. Because that's what you do when the math says someone has to lose so everyone else can win. You accept it, and you make sure it's you.\n\n\"Soos,\" Stan says, his voice unnaturally calm. \"There's an old pickup truck in the scrapyard we passed. Red one. You see it?\"\n\nSoos, battered and bruised but still standing at his engineering station, peers through a crack in the wall. \"Yeah, Mr. Pines, I see it. But dude, what-\"\n\n\"It still runs. I checked it two days ago while scavenging. Keys are in the visor.\" Stan unstraps himself from the command chair. \"Ford, you're on controls. Take the Shacktron to the bunker. You know where it is.\"\n\nFord turns from his console, and the look on his face says he understands. He understands exactly what Stan is doing. \"Stanley, no.\"\n\n\"Not a debate, Poindexter.\" Stan pulls off his cracked football helmet and sets it on the chair, almost gently. He straightens his suit - the same suit he wore every day at the Mystery Shack, now torn and stained and missing half its buttons. He looks, in that moment, not like a hero. Heroes look confident. Stan looks terrified. But he also looks decided, and with Stan Pines, decided is everything.\n\n\"Grunkle Stan-\" Mabel starts, and her voice breaks.\n\n\"Hey. Hey, look at me, sweetie.\" Stan kneels down and takes Mabel's face in his rough, scarred hands. \"I'm gonna be fine. This is what I do, remember? I'm a showman. I'm gonna give them the best show they've ever seen. The old razzle-dazzle.\" He winks, and his eyes are shining. \"You take care of your brother. And your brother takes care of you. Deal?\"\n\n\"Deal,\" Mabel whispers, and she hugs him so hard he wheezes.\n\nStan ruffles Dipper's hat. \"Hey, kid. You're the smartest person I know. And yeah, I'm including Ford in that.\" He glances at his brother. \"Don't tell him I said that.\"\n\n\"I heard you, Stanley.\"\n\n\"Good. Then hear this too.\" Stan walks to Ford and stands in front of him. Two brothers. Two old men. A lifetime of love and hurt between them. \"Take care of my kids.\"\n\nFord's voice is thick. \"Our kids.\"\n\nStan grins. It's the realest smile he's given in thirty years.\n\nThen he's gone. Out the emergency hatch, sliding down the Shacktron's damaged leg, hitting the ground running. He reaches the red pickup truck, hot-wires it because the keys aren't actually in the visor and he lied about that because he didn't want Soos to worry, and peels out in a spray of gravel and apocalyptic ash.\n\nThe truck roars toward Bill's advancing army, headlights blazing, horn honking. Stan rolls down the window and sticks his head out, yelling at the top of his lungs. \"HEY! HEY, YOU UGLY INTERDIMENSIONAL NIGHTMARES! YOU WANT A PIECE OF STANLEY PINES? COME AND GET IT! I ONCE PUNCHED A PTERODACTYL! I ONCE OUTRAN THE COLOMBIAN MILITARY! I ONCE ATE A WHOLE ROTISSERIE CHICKEN IN ONE SITTING AND I'D DO IT AGAIN!\"\n\nBill's forces turn. Of course they turn. Stan is impossible to ignore - he's the loudest, most obnoxious distraction in the history of diversionary tactics, and he's driving straight at them telling jokes.\n\n\"A TRIANGLE WALKS INTO A BAR! THE BARTENDER SAYS, 'WHY THE POINTY FACE!' HA! GET IT? BECAUSE YOU'RE POINTY, BILL! YOU'RE A POINTY LITTLE-\"\n\nThe Shacktron reaches the bunker. Ford seals the door. They survive. All of them. All of them except one stubborn, brave, impossible old man who drove a red pickup truck into the apocalypse telling jokes, because that's how Stanley Pines wanted to go out.\n\nNot with a whimper. Never with a whimper. With the best material he had.",
    ending: true
  },

  resistance_final_battle: {
    text: "The combined Shacktron stands at the edge of Gravity Falls like something out of a dream a giant robot would have about being a bigger giant robot. Soos has integrated Ford's Quantum Destabilizer into the right arm assembly, replacing the lost T-Rex skull with a focusing array made from every mirror, lens, and reflective surface in the Mystery Shack. The disco ball from Stan's secret party room sits at the center of the array like a gleaming mechanical heart.\n\nThe plan is elegant in its simplicity: get close to Bill, fire the Destabilizer, weaken him enough to defeat him. Ford has calculated the optimal firing range, the necessary power output, and the exact harmonic frequency that should disrupt Bill's geometric containment structure. Stan has calculated how many things he can punch on the way there. Both contributions are essential.\n\nThey march. The ground shakes with each step. Inside the Shacktron, the unlikely crew of defenders watches through reinforced windows as the landscape of broken Gravity Falls passes beneath them. Floating buildings drift aside like startled fish. Time bubbles pop as the Shacktron's electromagnetic field disrupts them, freeing the people trapped inside, who stumble away dazed and confused but alive.\n\nBill sees them coming, of course. He always sees everything. His single eye narrows, and for the first time since Weirdmageddon began, something flickers across his triangular face that might be concern. Or it might be excitement. With Bill, it's hard to tell, and honestly it might be both.\n\n\"WELL, PINE TREE AND SHOOTING STAR AND THE REST OF YOU MEAT PUPPETS!\" his voice thunders across the shattered sky. \"BACK FOR ROUND TWO? I ADMIRE YOUR PERSISTENCE. IT'S WRONG AND MISGUIDED AND ULTIMATELY FUTILE, BUT I ADMIRE IT.\"\n\nHe sends his forces. They come in waves - eye-bats first, then the ground troops, then the Henchmaniacs themselves. The Shacktron wades through them with mechanical determination. Stan works the controls, smashing through demons with the left fist while Ford calibrates the Destabilizer on the right arm. Wendy picks off anything that gets too close. Soos keeps the engines running through sheer willpower and frequent pep talks to individual machine parts.\n\nThey reach the Fearamid. Ford shouts the signal. Stan pulls the trigger.\n\nThe Quantum Destabilizer fires. A beam of concentrated anti-weirdness energy lances from the Shacktron's arm and strikes Bill Cipher directly in his eye. The effect is immediate and dramatic. Bill's golden glow flickers. His form distorts, the crisp edges of his triangle wavering like a reflection in disturbed water. He screams - not in pain exactly, but in outrage, the scream of a being who has never been successfully challenged before.\n\n\"WHAT- WHAT IS THIS?\" Bill's voice cracks, modulates, drops an octave. His eye spins wildly. \"WHAT DID YOU DO TO ME?\"\n\n\"Quantum destabilization!\" Ford shouts, unable to keep the triumph from his voice. \"I'm disrupting your geometric containment structure! Without it, your energy has no form - you'll dissipate!\"\n\nBill flickers again. For one glorious moment, it looks like it's working. His triangle shape warps, stretches, starts to come apart at the edges. The Henchmaniacs freeze, looking at their master with something that might be terror.\n\nThen Bill laughs. It starts small - a chuckle that sounds like breaking glass - and builds to a roar that shakes the Fearamid to its foundation. \"OH FORDSY. FORDSY, FORDSY, FORDSY. DID YOU REALLY THINK I DIDN'T PLAN FOR THIS?\"\n\nHe snaps his fingers, and the Destabilizer beam bends. Not deflects - bends, curving through the air like a garden hose, redirected back at the Shacktron. The feedback surge overloads every crystal in the focusing array. The disco ball explodes in a shower of mirrored fragments. The Destabilizer crumbles, falling from the Shacktron's arm in pieces.\n\n\"NICE TRY, THOUGH! A-MINUS FOR CREATIVITY! F-MINUS FOR RESULTS!\"\n\nFord stares at his ruined weapon, and for a terrible moment his face shows nothing but despair. Then something shifts behind his eyes. A memory. A clue. A last, desperate hope.\n\n\"The Axolotl,\" he whispers. \"It's the only way. Bill is too powerful for science alone. We need something that exists outside his rules entirely.\" He turns to the twins, and his expression is fierce and afraid in equal measure. \"I know how to reach it. I learned the ritual in a dimension so far from here that Bill's name is spoken only as a curse. But it will require everything we have left.\"\n\nThe Shacktron stands battered but unbroken before the Fearamid. Bill hovers above, his power restored, his eye blazing with malicious joy. And somewhere beyond all of it - beyond dimensions, beyond reality, beyond the furthest edge of everything - something ancient stirs.",
    choices: [
      { label: "Ford knows how to summon the Axolotl", target: "axolotl_ritual" },
      { label: "Make a last stand against Bill", target: "ending_resistance_laststand" }
    ]
  },

  resistance_wait: {
    text: "\"We need more time,\" Ford insists, his six-fingered hands flying across the workbench as he makes adjustments to the Quantum Destabilizer. \"Another day. Maybe two. I need to recalibrate the harmonic frequency modulator, reinforce the containment crystals, run at least three more simulations-\"\n\n\"We don't HAVE another day,\" Stan growls from the doorway, but Ford waves him off, and the twins convince Stan to give Ford the time he needs. After all, they reason, better to go in with a finished weapon than a half-baked one.\n\nIt's the wrong call. They just don't know it yet.\n\nThe first sign that something is wrong comes twelve hours later, when Wendy's perimeter alarms start going off. Not one at a time, the way they do when a lone eye-bat strays too close. All at once. Every alarm, every trip wire, every motion sensor, all screaming in unison.\n\nWendy scrambles to the roof and what she sees drains the color from her face. She keys the walkie-talkie with hands that only tremble slightly. \"Uh, guys? Everyone? We have a problem. A really, really big problem.\"\n\nBill's entire army is assembled outside the Mystery Shack. Not a patrol. Not a raiding party. The whole thing. Thousands of nightmares arranged in neat rows that stretch back to the horizon, a sea of impossible creatures, geometric horrors, and twisted forms that the human brain refuses to fully process. Eye-bats darken the sky in such numbers that the already-red sky turns black. The Henchmaniacs stand at the front like generals reviewing their troops - 8-Ball, Teeth, Pyronica, Keyhole, Hectorgon, all of them, their grotesque forms casting shadows that seem to writhe independently.\n\nAnd above them all, floating with his hands behind his back and his cane tucked under one arm, is Bill Cipher himself. He's enormous - fifty feet tall, his single eye filling half the sky, his glow turning the forest gold. He looks like a stained-glass window from the church of something that should never be worshiped.\n\n\"KNOCK KNOCK!\" Bill's voice shakes the walls of the Shack. Dust falls from the ceiling. Windows crack. The gnomes under the kitchen table begin screaming. \"OH COME ON, YOU'RE SUPPOSED TO SAY 'WHO'S THERE?' FINE. I'LL DO BOTH PARTS. KNOCK KNOCK. WHO'S THERE. BILL. BILL WHO. BILL CIPHER, THE ALL-SEEING, ALL-KNOWING MASTER OF YOUR PATHETIC DIMENSION, HERE TO TURN YOUR LITTLE CLUBHOUSE INTO A SNOW GLOBE!\"\n\nThe defenses hold for about four minutes. The reinforced walls, the scrap metal armor, the sandbags and trip wires and everything Soos built with love and duct tape - all of it lasts exactly four minutes against the combined assault of Bill's forces. The eye-bats strip away the outer armor like piranhas. The Henchmaniacs tear through the walls like tissue paper. And Bill himself simply reaches down with one enormous hand and peels the roof off the Mystery Shack like opening a tin can.\n\nSunlight - wrong sunlight, red and golden and sick - pours into the exposed interior. Everyone looks up and sees Bill's eye looking down at them, filling the sky, and the pupil dilates with pure, delighted malice.\n\n\"AW, LOOK AT ALL THE LITTLE PEOPLE!\" Bill coos, his voice now soft and somehow worse for it. \"YOU WERE WAITING FOR THE PERFECT MOMENT, WEREN'T YOU? HERE'S A LITTLE SECRET, PINE TREE - THE PERFECT MOMENT WAS YESTERDAY. TODAY? TODAY THE MOMENT BELONGS TO ME.\"\n\nHe snaps his fingers. The sound is like the world cracking in half.\n\nThe Mystery Shack begins to shrink. Not collapse - shrink. The walls pull inward, the ceiling drops, the floor rises, everything compacting and miniaturizing like someone is turning a zoom dial in reverse. The people inside shrink with it. Dipper watches his hands get smaller, the room getting smaller, everything getting smaller, the world outside getting bigger, until the Mystery Shack is the size of a shoebox and they are the size of chess pieces.\n\nBill picks up the tiny Shack between two fingers, holds it up to his enormous eye, and peers inside. His gaze fills every window, inescapable, all-seeing. Inside, tiny figures pound on tiny walls and scream tiny screams that Bill finds absolutely hilarious.\n\n\"PERFECT!\" he announces, setting the miniature Mystery Shack on a shelf in his Fearamid, right next to a snow globe of Paris and a bottled galaxy. \"I ALWAYS WANTED A DOLLHOUSE! AND LOOK - IT COMES WITH ITS OWN LITTLE DOLLS!\"\n\nInside the tiny Shack, the Pines family and the townsfolk of Gravity Falls live on - alive, aware, but forever small. Ford's unfinished Destabilizer sits on a workbench the size of a postage stamp. The perfect weapon, almost ready. Almost. But almost, it turns out, doesn't save the world.",
    ending: true
  },

  resistance_extract: {
    text: "Dipper runs. The moment Wendy gives the signal, he sprints from the shadow of a collapsed building toward the column of Weirdmageddon energy, Ford's containment unit clutched to his chest like a football. His heart hammers so hard he can feel it in his fingertips. The air gets thicker as he approaches the column, syrupy and electric, tasting of copper and ozone and something that might be the flavor of reality coming apart at the seams.\n\nThe containment unit's crystals are singing. Not metaphorically - they're producing an actual audible tone, a high clear note that resonates with the Weirdmageddon energy like a tuning fork finding its frequency. Ford designed the crystals to attract and contain small amounts of the energy, and they're doing their job almost too well. The unit vibrates in Dipper's hands, pulling toward the column like a compass needle pointing north.\n\nHe reaches the base of the column and holds out the containment unit. The effect is immediate. Weirdmageddon energy peels away from the column in ribbons, spiraling into the crystalline jar like water circling a drain. The energy is visible - swirling bands of color that exist outside the normal spectrum, colors that make Dipper's eyes water and his brain itch. Inside the containment unit, the sample glows and churns, pressing against the magnetic coils that keep it contained.\n\n\"That's enough!\" Wendy hisses from her position. \"Dipper, pull back! We've got maybe ninety seconds before the next patrol!\"\n\nBut something is wrong. The column of energy has noticed the extraction. Not in an intelligent way - it's not alive, exactly - but in the way a river notices a dam. The flow has changed. The energy is being drawn toward the containment unit faster now, more than the crystals can safely hold. Warning lights that Dipper didn't know the unit had begin blinking red.\n\nHe yanks the unit away from the column and runs. Behind him, the column of Weirdmageddon energy pulses, sending out a shockwave of warped reality that turns the pavement into taffy and the nearest building into a flock of startled pigeons. The shockwave passes over Dipper and for one horrible instant he can see through his own hands, can see his bones and blood vessels and the tiny electrical impulses firing through his nerves, and then it passes and he's solid again and still running.\n\nMabel meets him halfway, grabbing his arm and pulling him toward the rendezvous point. Wendy covers their retreat, her crossbow snapping bolts at a pair of eye-bats that have appeared on the horizon, drawn by the energy disturbance. They duck into the ruins of what used to be Greasy's Diner, pressing against the wall as the eye-bats sweep overhead, their enormous pupils scanning the ground.\n\nThe containment unit hums in Dipper's arms, warm and glowing. Inside, the sample of Weirdmageddon energy swirls in its crystalline prison - enough, Ford said, to power the Quantum Destabilizer. Enough to give them a weapon that might actually hurt Bill Cipher.\n\nBut the extraction didn't go unnoticed. High above, in the Fearamid, Bill's eye swivels. He felt it - a tiny disruption in the flow of his chaos, like a mosquito bite on the skin of his new reality. Insignificant, maybe. But Bill Cipher didn't conquer multiple dimensions by ignoring insignificant things.\n\n\"Pyronica,\" he says, and one of his Henchmaniacs steps forward - a being of living flame, her body a constantly shifting inferno of pink and orange fire, her smile the hottest part of her. \"Someone's been playing in my sandbox. Go find out who and make them wish they hadn't.\"\n\nPyronica streaks from the Fearamid like a comet, leaving a trail of burning air behind her. She can feel the containment unit's signature - a cold spot in the warm chaos of Weirdmageddon, a tiny pocket of order in her master's beautiful disorder.\n\n\"She's coming,\" Wendy says, spotting the fireball descending toward the town. \"Move. NOW.\"\n\nThey run. Through the broken streets, past the floating buildings and the screaming trees and the rivers of not-water. Pyronica's heat follows them, melting the asphalt behind them into bubbling pools. The containment unit grows brighter, its crystals resonating with the approaching Henchmaniac's energy. Mabel fires her grappling hook, swinging them over a gap in the road where the ground simply stops existing. Wendy lays down covering fire with her crossbow, which does nothing to Pyronica but does slow down the eye-bats.\n\nThey make it back to the Shack with Pyronica's fire licking at their heels. Soos slams the reinforced door shut behind them. The fireproof insulation that Ford insisted on installing holds, barely, turning the Shack's interior into a sauna but keeping the flames out.\n\nFord takes the containment unit with hands that tremble - not with fear, but with excitement. \"You did it,\" he breathes, holding the swirling sample up to the light. \"This is perfect. This is exactly what I needed.\" He carries it to the basement like a man carrying a newborn, and the sounds of furious scientific activity resume immediately.\n\nTwo hours later, Ford emerges with the completed Quantum Destabilizer. It's finished. It's ready. And the look in his eyes says he believes it might actually work.",
    choices: [
      { label: "Use the destabilizer immediately", target: "resistance_final_battle" },
      { label: "Wait for the perfect moment", target: "resistance_wait" }
    ]
  },

  resistance_8ball_fight: {
    text: "There's a moment, right before a fight, when everything gets very clear. The fear doesn't go away - it just stops mattering. Dipper has felt this before, in encounters with gnomes and shape-shifters and Bill himself, but never quite like this. Never standing in the shadow of a twelve-foot nightmare demon who uses human beings as furniture.\n\nWendy makes the first move. She steps out from behind the corner, crossbow leveled, and puts a bolt directly between 8-Ball's eight-ball eyes. The bolt bounces off. Of course it does - 8-Ball is made of stuff that crossbow bolts can't scratch. But it gets his attention, which was the point.\n\n\"HEY!\" 8-Ball's voice sounds like billiard balls clacking together in a dryer. He stands up from his throne of frozen people, all twelve feet of him unfolding like a nightmare origami, and turns those terrible pool-ball eyes toward Wendy. \"WHO THREW THAT? WHO WANTS TO BE MY NEW OTTOMAN?\"\n\nWendy fires again, already moving, drawing 8-Ball away from the theater entrance. The plan, such as it is, runs like this: Wendy keeps 8-Ball busy while Dipper and Mabel free the prisoners inside. It's not a great plan. But it's a plan that involves Wendy Corduroy being Wendy Corduroy, and Dipper has learned to never bet against that.\n\n8-Ball charges after Wendy with surprising speed for something his size. His footsteps crack the pavement. His billiard-ball teeth gnash and clack in his too-wide mouth. Wendy vaults over a burned-out car, rolls, comes up firing. She's not trying to hurt him - she's trying to make him angry enough to chase her and too angry to think. It's working. 8-Ball bellows and swings a fist the size of a refrigerator at the car, sending it tumbling end over end down the street.\n\nMeanwhile, Dipper and Mabel slip into the theater. Inside, it's worse than they expected. The movie theater has been converted into Bill's personal collection gallery. The thirty prisoners are arranged in rows like an audience, frozen in their seats by the same magic 8-Ball used for his furniture. Their eyes track Dipper and Mabel as they enter - the only part of them that can still move. Thirty pairs of terrified, pleading eyes.\n\nDipper pulls out the journal - Journal 3, battered and burned but still legible. He flips to the section on paralysis magic, scanning Ford's cramped handwriting for a counterspell. \"Here,\" he says, finding it. \"Ford wrote about this kind of stasis field. It's maintained by a focal point - a magical anchor somewhere in the room. If we destroy it, the field collapses and everyone goes free.\"\n\nMabel is already searching. She tears through the theater - behind the screen, under the seats, in the projection booth. She finds it in the concession stand, of all places: a small crystal embedded in the popcorn machine, glowing with a sickly green light. \"Found it!\" she calls, and brings her grappling hook down on it with every ounce of strength in her twelve-year-old body.\n\nThe crystal shatters. A wave of energy pulses outward. And thirty people gasp simultaneously, the stasis field releasing them all at once. They slump in their seats, dazed and shaking, but alive and free.\n\nOutside, the fight has escalated. 8-Ball has cornered Wendy against a wall, and he's reaching for her with hands that could crush concrete. But Wendy has something 8-Ball doesn't - she grew up in Gravity Falls, and she knows every inch of this town, including the fact that the wall she's backed against is the wall of the old hardware store, and behind it is a shelf stocked with three hundred pounds of industrial-strength adhesive.\n\nShe ducks 8-Ball's grab, kicks the wall hard enough to shatter it, and the shelf falls. Three hundred pounds of glue explodes across 8-Ball in a tidal wave of sticky, setting quickly in the open air. He roars, thrashes, and finds himself stuck. Not permanently - he's strong enough to tear free eventually - but long enough.\n\nDipper and Mabel emerge from the theater with thirty freed prisoners, and Wendy falls in beside them, breathing hard, grinning, a cut on her forehead leaking blood that she wipes away with her sleeve.\n\n\"Did you just trap a demon with hardware store supplies?\" Dipper asks.\n\n\"Dude, have you seen how much industrial adhesive costs? That's like, eight hundred dollars worth of glue. Totally worth it.\"\n\nBehind them, 8-Ball's roars of rage echo through the empty streets. He'll get free. They all know that. But right now, thirty people are breathing free air for the first time in days, and some of them are crying, and some of them are hugging, and one of them is Pacifica Northwest, who hugs Mabel so hard that both girls nearly fall over.\n\nThe question now is what to do with their victory. They've scored a win, freed people, and proven that Bill's forces aren't invincible. But they're deep in enemy territory, 8-Ball won't stay stuck forever, and every minute they spend here is another minute for Bill to notice what happened.",
    choices: [
      { label: "Return to the Shack with the rescued people", target: "resistance_rally" },
      { label: "Push deeper into town to rescue more people", target: "ending_resistance_too_far" }
    ]
  },

  resistance_army: {
    text: "Dipper climbs onto a rock at the edge of the forest clearing and looks out at the assembled supernatural creatures of Gravity Falls. It's the most ridiculous army in the history of warfare. A fifteen-foot gnome stack wobbles unsteadily. The Manotaurs flex and pose for nobody in particular. The Multi-Bear sits calmly, seven heads watching with seven different expressions. And somewhere, presumably, the Hide-Behind is hiding behind something.\n\n\"Listen,\" Dipper says, and his voice cracks because he's twelve and his voice does that sometimes, but he pushes through it. \"I know you all have your own problems with each other. Gnomes and Manotaurs don't get along. The unicorns won't even show up. Half of you have tried to kidnap my sister at some point.\" He pauses. \"Actually, Jeff, we should really talk about that someday.\"\n\n\"Noted and ignored!\" Jeff calls from the gnome stack.\n\nDipper continues. \"But here's the thing. Bill Cipher isn't just destroying the human world. He's destroying ALL of it. Your world too. The gnome tunnels? They're being flooded with Weirdmageddon energy. The Manotaur caves? Half of them have been turned into pocket dimensions full of eyeballs. The enchanted forest? It's literally on fire. Bill doesn't care about humans versus magical creatures. To him, we're ALL just toys.\"\n\nHe points toward the horizon, where the Fearamid hovers and the sky bleeds red. \"If we don't stop him, there won't be a Gravity Falls for ANY of us. No forest for the gnomes. No mountains for the Manotaurs. No anywhere for anyone. Bill wants to turn the entire universe into his personal playground, and when he's done playing with it, he'll throw it away. That's what he does. He told me himself - he's done it to other dimensions before. They're just gone. Erased. Like they never existed.\"\n\nThe creatures are listening now. Even the Manotaurs have stopped flexing. The Multi-Bear's heads have all turned to face Dipper, seven pairs of eyes reflecting the red sky.\n\nMabel steps up beside her brother. She's holding something - a photo from the Mystery Shack's wall, slightly singed at the edges. It shows the whole town of Gravity Falls on a summer day: humans and gnomes and the occasional Manotaur in the background, all going about their lives in this weird, wonderful place where the impossible is just Tuesday.\n\n\"This is what we're fighting for,\" Mabel says, holding up the photo. \"Not because we're the same. Because we're all from HERE. This weird little town where gravity falls up sometimes and gnomes steal your tomatoes and there's a seven-headed bear who likes pop music. This is our home. ALL of ours. And nobody - not even an all-powerful dream demon from another dimension - gets to take it from us.\"\n\nThe gnome stack cheers. The Manotaurs stamp their hooves so hard the ground shakes. The Multi-Bear roars with seven mouths, and from somewhere behind a large rock, an invisible creature makes a sound of emphatic agreement.\n\nThey march. The ragtag supernatural army moves through the forest alongside the Shacktron, and it should look absurd - it DOES look absurd - but it also looks like something else. It looks like hope. Ridiculous, improbable, held-together-with-duct-tape-and-stubbornness hope.\n\nBut hope has a traitor in its midst.\n\nGideon Gleeful watches from the shadows. He's been watching the whole time, his cherubic face split by a grin that doesn't reach his cold blue eyes. He's been feeding information to Bill since day one - troop numbers, defense layouts, everything. Bill promised him power. Bill promised him Mabel. Bill promised him the town, rebuilt to his specifications, with a big white mansion on the hill and everyone in Gravity Falls finally showing him the respect he deserves.\n\nGideon slips away from the marching army and whispers into a crystal that glows with Bill's light. \"They're coming,\" he says. \"All of them. Everything they've got. The path through Miller's Pass.\"\n\nBill's eye glows. \"EXCELLENT WORK, KID. I KNEW I KEPT YOU AROUND FOR A REASON. BESIDES THE ENTERTAINMENT VALUE OF WATCHING YOU TRY TO REACH DOORKNOBS.\"\n\nThe trap springs at Miller's Pass. The narrow canyon was always a chokepoint, and Bill has filled it with every nightmare at his disposal. They hit the army from above, below, and both sides simultaneously. The gnome stack topples. The Manotaurs charge bravely but are overwhelmed by sheer numbers. The Multi-Bear fights like the seven-headed warrior he is, but even he cannot hold back a tide of chaos.\n\nThe Shacktron takes three direct hits from Bill's energy beams and crumbles, its duct-tape joints finally giving way, spilling its crew across the canyon floor. Stan, Ford, the twins, Wendy, Soos - all of them scattered and stunned.\n\nGideon struts through the carnage, straightening his tiny suit. \"Well well well,\" he says, standing over the fallen Pines family. \"How the tables have turned. Or should I say, how the BILLS have come due? Ha! Get it? BILL? I've been working on that one.\"\n\nBill descends, and he's laughing, and the laugh is the sound of a universe that has run out of miracles. He snaps his fingers, and Gideon freezes mid-gloat, his smug expression locked in gold as the transformation takes him. Bill tilts his golden statue at an angle, considering it.\n\n\"I SAID I'D REWARD YOU, KID. I DIDN'T SAY WHAT THE REWARD WOULD BE. WELCOME TO MY COLLECTION!\"\n\nThe last thing the Pines family sees before their own transformation is Gideon's golden face, forever frozen in an expression of betrayed surprise. It would be satisfying if they weren't about to join him.",
    ending: true
  },

  ending_resistance_overwhelmed: {
    text: "The Weirdmageddon energy is alive. Not alive like an animal or a person - alive like a storm is alive, like an avalanche is alive, a force of nature with no consciousness but an unstoppable momentum that doesn't care what's in its path. And right now, Dipper is in its path.\n\nThe containment unit overloads. Ford's crystals, carefully grown over months in the basement laboratory, crack one by one, each break releasing a pulse of reality-warping energy that expands outward in rings. The first pulse turns the ground beneath Dipper's feet to glass. The second reverses gravity for three seconds, sending him tumbling upward before slamming him back down. The third makes the air taste like the color blue, which shouldn't be possible but is somehow the least disturbing thing that's happened today.\n\nMabel runs toward her brother, and Wendy runs with her, but the energy is spreading too fast, pooling around them like floodwater, and this flood doesn't carry debris - it carries impossible things. Fragments of other dimensions bleed through: a rain of fish bones from a world where the ocean is made of skeletons, a blast of music from a dimension where sound has weight, a wave of pure geometric light from a place where math is the only thing that exists.\n\n\"The containment failed!\" Dipper shouts, dropping the cracked unit. \"There's too much energy! It's-\"\n\nHe doesn't finish the sentence because a time bubble forms around his left hand. He watches his own fingers age and de-age in rapid succession, flickering between baby-small and wrinkled-old, and the sensation is so wrong, so fundamentally disturbing, that his brain simply refuses to process it.\n\nWendy grabs his other arm and pulls, trying to drag him out of the energy field, but it's too late. The Weirdmageddon energy has reached critical density, and it does what Weirdmageddon energy does at critical density: it opens a rift. Not a big one - not like the portal that started all of this. A small one. Personal. A custom-fitted tear in reality designed for exactly three people.\n\nThe time bubble expands. It swallows Dipper first, then Mabel as she reaches for him, then Wendy as she reaches for both of them. For one crystalline instant, they can see outside the bubble - they can see the town, the Fearamid, the burning sky - and then the bubble closes and the outside world vanishes and there is nothing but the loop.\n\nThey're in the town square. It's the day Weirdmageddon started. Bill's rift is opening above them, the sky is tearing apart, and people are running and screaming. Dipper knows what happens next because he lived it. The sky bleeds. The eye-bats pour through. Bill descends in his terrible glory and announces the end of everything.\n\nThen it resets. They're in the town square again. The rift opens. Bill descends. The world ends. Reset. Town square. Rift. Bill. End. Reset.\n\nThe same horrible day, playing on an endless loop.\n\nAt first they fight it. They run in different directions, try to change the sequence of events, scream at people who can't hear them because the people are just echoes, recordings, shadows of a moment burned into the fabric of time. Nothing works. The loop resets every forty-seven minutes, snapping them back to the town square like rubber bands, ready to watch the worst day of their lives all over again.\n\nAfter a while - and \"a while\" could mean anything inside a time bubble, where minutes and centuries are the same thing - they stop fighting. They sit on the steps of the library, the three of them in a row, and watch the end of the world like a movie they've seen too many times. Wendy counts the eye-bats as they pour through. Mabel names them. Dipper takes notes in a journal that resets with everything else, so the notes are never saved, but the habit of writing them down feels like sanity, and sanity is worth holding onto.\n\n\"We'll get out of this,\" Wendy says, every single loop, at exactly the same moment - twenty-three minutes in, when Bill's shadow first falls over the square. She says it because she believes it, or because she needs to believe it, or because saying it is the only thing she can do that feels like resistance.\n\nMabel nods. \"Definitely,\" she says. \"Absolutely. One hundred percent.\" And she smiles, because Mabel Pines smiles at the dark and dares it to blink first.\n\nDipper says nothing. He writes in his journal and watches the sky break open and counts another loop, another iteration, another replay of the day everything went wrong. Somewhere outside the bubble, the real Weirdmageddon continues. Somewhere outside, their family fights on without them. Somewhere outside, time moves forward.\n\nInside the bubble, the worst day plays again.",
    ending: true
  },

  ending_resistance_eyebats: {
    text: "Sneaking past 8-Ball seems like the smarter play. He's big, he's distracted, he's currently using a mailbox as a toothpick. If they can just slip around the side of the theater, they can get in through the emergency exit, free the prisoners, and be gone before the Henchmaniac even notices. It's a good plan. It's a sensible plan. It accounts for almost everything.\n\nIt does not account for the eye-bats.\n\nThe eye-bat perched on the theater's roof is almost invisible against the dark red sky. Its wings are folded, its enormous eye half-lidded, and it looks for all the world like a gargoyle - if gargoyles were made of veiny membrane and malevolent intent. It's a sentry, positioned here specifically because Bill knows there are survivors in the theater and survivors attract rescuers and rescuers are entertaining to catch.\n\nWendy sees it first, but a fraction of a second too late. \"DOWN!\" she hisses, shoving the twins behind a dumpster. But the eye-bat's pupil has already dilated. It's already seen them. And eye-bats, unlike most of Bill's creations, don't attack alone.\n\nThe sound it makes is less a screech and more a frequency - a vibration that travels through the air and the ground and the bones and the teeth, a signal that says HERE in a language that every eye-bat in a mile radius understands. The sentry's wings snap open, that single enormous eye blazing like a searchlight, and it screams its location to the sky.\n\nThey come from everywhere. From behind buildings and under bridges and out of shadows that shouldn't be deep enough to hide them. Dozens of eye-bats, then scores, then more than Dipper can count, pouring out of the darkness like a river of wings and watching eyes. They fill the sky above the theater in a swirling vortex, their combined gaze creating a cone of light that pins the three heroes to the ground like butterflies in a display case.\n\n8-Ball looks up from his people-throne and grins his billiard-ball grin. \"Well well,\" he rumbles, standing to his full twelve-foot height. \"Looks like the appetizers came to me.\"\n\nWendy fights. Of course she does. She puts three bolts into the swarm, each one taking down an eye-bat, but three out of a hundred means nothing. She grabs a piece of rebar from the rubble and swings it like a bat, knocking eye-bats out of the air, but more keep coming, always more, their wings beating the air into a hurricane that pins her red hair back and steals her breath.\n\nMabel fires her grappling hook, catching the theater's fire escape, but an eye-bat bites through the rope and she drops back to the ground. Dipper tries to run, to draw them away, but the eye-bats are everywhere, a living ceiling of membrane and malice that leaves no gap to slip through.\n\nThe eye-bats don't attack with claws or teeth - that's not what they do. They grab. Dozens of tiny clawed feet latch onto clothing, hair, skin, and they lift. Dipper feels his feet leave the ground and his stomach drops as he rises, carried upward by a dozen eye-bats working in concert. Below him, Mabel is lifted by her sweater, spinning slowly, and Wendy is borne up despite her furious struggling, her crossbow falling from her hands and clattering to the pavement far below.\n\nThey rise above the rooftops. Above the ruined town. Above the floating debris and the time bubbles and the screaming trees. They rise until the Fearamid fills their entire field of vision, enormous and dark and alive with Bill's power, its single eye watching their approach with the lazy interest of a cat watching mice delivered to its doorstep.\n\nBill meets them at the entrance, hovering with his cane behind his back, his bow tie perfectly straight, his eye half-lidded with amusement. \"ROOM SERVICE!\" he announces. \"I DIDN'T ORDER ANYTHING, BUT I'LL TAKE IT. YOU KNOW ME - I LOVE SURPRISES. WELL, NO, I DON'T. I'M OMNISCIENT. BUT I LOVE PRETENDING TO BE SURPRISED. IT'S A HOBBY.\"\n\nHe examines each of them in turn, spinning them with one finger like ornaments on a tree. \"PINE TREE. SHOOTING STAR. AND... RED? IS THAT YOUR THING? RED? I'LL WORK ON A BETTER NICKNAME. ANYWAY, WELCOME TO THE FEARAMID. I'D GIVE YOU THE TOUR BUT YOU'RE NOT GOING TO BE IN ANY SHAPE TO ENJOY IT.\"\n\nHis eye glows gold, and the familiar warmth of transformation magic washes over them. The last thing Dipper sees before he becomes a golden decoration is Mabel's face, and she's not crying. She's angry. Furious. A twelve-year-old girl turned to gold with an expression of such defiant rage that even Bill pauses for a moment before setting her on his mantelpiece.\n\n\"NICE FACE, KID,\" he admits. \"I'LL PUT YOU WHERE THE LIGHT CATCHES IT.\"",
    ending: true
  },

  ending_resistance_laststand: {
    text: "No Axolotl. No cosmic intervention. No miraculous last-minute save from a being that exists outside the rules. Just them. Just the Pines family and their friends, standing in the shadow of the Fearamid, armed with nothing but stubbornness and love and the absolute unshakeable refusal to let the universe end without a fight.\n\nStan cracks his knuckles. Ford adjusts his glasses. Wendy picks up a piece of the ruined Destabilizer and holds it like a club. Soos raises his fists, which he has wrapped in duct tape for reasons that are unclear but somehow appropriate. Mabel loads her grappling hook. Dipper opens Journal 3 to the last blank page and writes, quickly, in handwriting that shakes but doesn't stop: \"We fought. Remember that.\"\n\nBill descends from the Fearamid, and he is enormous now, swollen with the power of a conquered dimension, his form flickering between his usual triangle and something larger, something that fills the sky, something that has too many angles and too many eyes and too many dimensions for a human mind to fully comprehend.\n\n\"THIS IS IT?\" Bill sounds almost disappointed. \"THIS IS YOUR BIG FINALE? NO SECRET WEAPON? NO DEUS EX MACHINA? JUST... YOU?\" His eye scans them, reading their thoughts, tasting their determination. And something in his expression shifts. Not fear - Bill doesn't feel fear. But something adjacent to it. Something like recognition. \"HUH. YOU ACTUALLY THINK YOU CAN WIN. THAT'S... THAT'S GENUINELY FASCINATING. I'VE DESTROYED CIVILIZATIONS THAT HAD ARMADAS. I'VE CRUSHED DIMENSIONS THAT HAD GODS ON THEIR SIDE. AND YOU PEOPLE - WITH YOUR DUCT TAPE AND YOUR CROSSBOW AND YOUR SPARKLY SWEATERS - YOU THINK YOU HAVE A SHOT.\" A pause. \"I KINDA RESPECT THAT. I MEAN, YOU'RE WRONG. BUT I RESPECT IT.\"\n\nThey charge. All of them, together, running at an omnipotent being like it's a schoolyard bully instead of a cosmic horror. Stan reaches him first, throwing a punch that has thirty years of guilt and anger behind it. His fist passes through Bill's form, of course - you can't punch a being of pure energy - but the intent of it, the sheer audacity, makes Bill flicker.\n\nFord fires what's left of the Destabilizer - a single burst, barely enough energy to light a lamp. But it's aimed perfectly, hitting Bill's eye at the exact frequency that disrupts his containment. Bill winces. Actually winces. It's not damage, not really, but it's discomfort, and discomfort is something Bill hasn't felt since before the universe had a name.\n\nWendy hurls herself at one of Bill's outstretched hands, climbing his arm like she's climbing a tree, which is essentially what she grew up doing. She reaches his bow tie and pulls, and somehow, impossibly, it comes loose. Bill yelps and grabs for it, momentarily distracted by the indignity.\n\nSoos wraps his duct-taped fists around Bill's cane and pulls. The cane comes free. Soos holds it up triumphantly, and for one second, one beautiful ridiculous second, he's a handyman holding a cosmic artifact and grinning like he just fixed a leaky faucet.\n\nMabel fires her grappling hook at Bill's top corner. The hook catches, and she swings, arc-ing through the air in a blur of sweater and determination, and as she passes Bill's eye she slaps a sticker on it. A rainbow sticker. Bill's vision is momentarily obscured by a sparkly rainbow, and his howl of outrage is the most satisfying sound in the multiverse.\n\nFor thirty seconds, they are magnificent.\n\nThen Bill gets bored.\n\nHe doesn't even snap his fingers. He just... stops allowing it. The amusement drains from his eye like water down a sink, replaced by something cold and ancient and utterly indifferent. He grows. Not gradually - instantly. One moment he's the size of a building; the next he fills the sky, his form blocking out the red sun, his shadow falling over the entire town like a blanket being pulled over a corpse.\n\n\"THAT WAS FUN,\" he says, and his voice doesn't boom anymore. It just IS. It's everywhere, in the air and the ground and inside their skulls. \"BUT HERE'S THE THING ABOUT LAST STANDS, PINE TREE. THEY'RE CALLED LAST STANDS BECAUSE THEY'RE THE LAST THING YOU DO.\"\n\nStan's golden transformation happens mid-swing. His fist, still cocked back for another punch, freezes and hardens and turns the color of afternoon light. The expression on his face - defiant, furious, loving - is captured perfectly in precious metal.\n\nFord goes next. He's reaching for something in his coat - one last invention, one last desperate idea - but gold takes his fingers before they find it. His glasses catch the light as they crystallize. His six-fingered hand, extended toward some theoretical salvation, becomes a golden sculpture of hope that will never be fulfilled.\n\nWendy is climbing Bill's form when the gold takes her. She's fifteen feet off the ground, muscles straining, red hair streaming behind her, and the transformation catches her in motion - a golden girl frozen in mid-climb, the most determined statue in the history of the world. She doesn't even get to finish saying what she was saying, which, knowing Wendy, was probably something incredibly cool.\n\nSoos goes down holding Bill's cane. He's still grinning. The gold seals that grin forever.\n\nMabel is last. She sees them all go - Stan, Ford, Wendy, Soos - and she doesn't scream and she doesn't cry. She turns to Dipper, and the look on her face is the look of someone who has run out of everything except love. \"Bro-bro,\" she says, and reaches for his hand.\n\nThe gold takes them both at the same moment. Brother and sister, hands clasped, turned to golden crystal in the shadow of the Fearamid. Mabel's shooting star sweater gleams. Dipper's journal, pressed to his chest, becomes a golden book that will never be read again.\n\nBill looks at his collection. Six golden statues arranged in a semicircle at his feet, each one frozen in a moment of impossible courage. He considers them for a long time. Then he picks them up, one by one, and sets them on pedestals inside the Fearamid's throne room.\n\nHe puts Stan and Ford together. He puts Wendy and Soos together. He puts Dipper and Mabel in the center, hands clasped, facing his throne.\n\n\"YOU KNOW WHAT THE PROBLEM WITH BRAVERY IS?\" Bill says to the golden twins, settling into his throne. \"IT ONLY WORKS IN STORIES WHERE THE AUTHOR IS ON YOUR SIDE. IN THIS STORY?\" He props his feet up. \"I'M THE AUTHOR.\"\n\nThe Fearamid hums. The red sky pulses. Gravity Falls belongs to Bill Cipher, and the six bravest people who ever lived are decorations in his living room. Their fight was beautiful. Their courage was real. And none of it mattered.\n\nIn Journal 3, pressed against Dipper's golden chest, the last page reads: \"We fought. Remember that.\" But there's no one left to read it. No one left to remember. Just a golden book in a golden boy's hands, in a throne room that will stand forever, in a world that has already ended.\n\nSometimes courage isn't enough. Sometimes love isn't enough. Sometimes you do everything right and fight with everything you have and it still doesn't matter, because the thing you're fighting is bigger than right, bigger than love, bigger than everything you are and everything you'll ever be. Sometimes the universe doesn't care how brave you were.\n\nThe Fearamid stands. The golden statues gleam. And Bill Cipher, master of all he surveys, doesn't think about them again.",
    ending: true
  },

  ending_resistance_too_far: {
    text: "Victory tastes good. Maybe too good. The rush of freeing thirty people from 8-Ball's prison, of watching Wendy trap a Henchmaniac with hardware store supplies, of seeing Mabel shatter the stasis crystal with one perfect swing - it makes them feel invincible. And invincible is a dangerous feeling in a town that belongs to a chaos god.\n\n\"There are more people out there,\" Dipper says, his eyes bright, Journal 3 open in his hands. \"Ford's maps show clusters of survivors all over town. The elementary school, the water tower, the church basement. If we can free them too-\"\n\n\"Dude,\" Wendy says, and there's a warning in her voice that Dipper doesn't hear because he's already planning routes. \"Dude, we should go back. Regroup. 8-Ball's going to get free, and when he does-\"\n\n\"Five more minutes,\" Dipper says. \"The elementary school is only three blocks away. We can be in and out before-\"\n\nMabel puts her hand on Dipper's arm. She has that look she gets sometimes, the one that's equal parts love and worry. \"Bro-bro, Wendy's right. We pushed our luck. Let's take the win and go home.\"\n\nBut Dipper is already moving. The thirty freed prisoners are being guided back toward the Mystery Shack by Pacifica, who has risen to the occasion with a commanding presence that suggests bossiness is just leadership without a crisis. With the civilians handled, Dipper feels free to push deeper. Just a little deeper. Just three more blocks.\n\nTwo blocks in, the streets change. They've crossed an invisible line, a boundary between the areas Bill has left as a playground and the areas he's claimed as his own. The difference is immediate and nauseating. The pavement isn't pavement anymore - it's a surface that breathes, that rises and falls in slow undulations. The buildings aren't ruined; they're transformed, twisted into shapes that serve Bill's aesthetic of cheerful madness - a hardware store with the doorway replaced by a grinning mouth, a flower shop where the flowers are eyes on stems.\n\nThen the giggling starts.\n\nIt comes from everywhere and nowhere, a high-pitched tittering that bounces off walls that shouldn't be able to bounce sound. Teeth appears first - literally Teeth, the Henchmaniac who is exactly what his name implies: a giant pair of legs with a mouth where the body should be, every surface covered in molars and incisors and canines that chatter and click in an endless dental percussion.\n\nThen Keyhole materializes from a door that wasn't there a second ago - a flat, keyhole-shaped being who moves through locks and keyholes the way fish move through water. He slides out of a padlock on a gate and unfolds into his full size, which is about eight feet of vaguely threatening geometry.\n\nThey're not alone. Behind them, around them, above them - Bill's creatures emerge from every shadow and surface, dozens of minor nightmares that aren't important enough to have names but are plenty important enough to block every escape route. The trap snaps shut so cleanly that it barely makes a sound.\n\nWendy raises her crossbow, but Teeth just grins - his entire body grins, because his entire body is a grin - and the crossbow dissolves into a shower of splinters. Wendy stares at her empty hands. \"Well,\" she says. \"That's new.\"\n\n\"RUN!\" Dipper shouts, but there's nowhere to run to. The streets behind them have sealed, the buildings have closed their door-mouths, and Keyhole has locked every exit with a casual wave of his flat body.\n\nThey're brought before Bill. It's a short trip - they're already in his territory, and territory means Bill can rearrange geography to suit his needs. One step they're on Main Street; the next they're in the Fearamid's throne room, standing on a floor that shifts between marble and living flesh and something that might be frozen screams.\n\nBill is sitting sideways in his throne, legs crossed, spinning his cane like a baton. He looks bored. Then he sees who Teeth and Keyhole have brought him, and his eye lights up like a Christmas tree. A Christmas tree that wants to destroy all of reality, but still.\n\n\"PINE TREE! SHOOTING STAR! AND RED! OH MAN, THIS IS LIKE CHRISTMAS AND MY BIRTHDAY AND THE HEAT DEATH OF A UNIVERSE ALL ROLLED INTO ONE!\" He floats down from his throne, circling them, his eye examining each of them with an unsettling intimacy. \"YOU KNOW, I HAD A BET WITH PYRONICA ABOUT HOW LONG IT WOULD TAKE YOU TO DO SOMETHING STUPID. SHE SAID A WEEK. I SAID THREE DAYS. LOOKS LIKE MAMA'S GETTING A NEW PAIR OF DIMENSIONAL SCISSORS!\"\n\nHe snaps his fingers, and something happens that is, somehow, worse than being turned to gold. Worse than being frozen. Worse than being trapped in a time bubble.\n\nThey become socks. Specifically, sock puppets. Dipper feels his body flatten, soften, become fabric. His arms disappear. His legs fuse together. His mouth becomes a felt crease. He can still see, still think, still feel, but he's a sock puppet - a soft, floppy piece of cloth with button eyes and yarn hair.\n\nBill puts them on his hand. His fingers work their fabric mouths. \"HI, I'M PINE TREE AND I HAVE A BIG HEAD FULL OF BAD IDEAS!\" he says in a squeaky voice, making the Dipper puppet bob up and down. \"I'M SHOOTING STAR AND I LOVE GLITTER AND POOR LIFE CHOICES!\" The Mabel puppet waves its tiny felt arms. \"I'M RED AND I'M TOO COOL FOR THE APOCALYPSE!\" The Wendy puppet does a little spin.\n\nThe Henchmaniacs howl with laughter. Teeth laughs so hard several of his teeth fall out and scurry back into place. Bill performs an entire puppet show - \"THE FALL OF THE PINES FAMILY: A TRAGEDY IN THREE ACTS\" - for his assembled demons, using the three sock puppets to reenact a wildly inaccurate version of events where Bill is the hero and everyone else is a bumbling sidekick.\n\nInside the sock puppet that used to be Dipper Pines, a twelve-year-old boy stares out through button eyes at a laughing audience of nightmares, and thinks: I should have listened to Wendy. I should have taken the win and gone home.\n\nThe puppet show continues. Act Two is about to begin. And somewhere in the back of the theater of horrors, Bill is already working on Act Three.",
    ending: true
  },

  // ===== FEARAMID BRANCH =====
  fearamid_approach: {
    text: "The Fearamid hangs in the sky like a wound in the world. Bill Cipher's fortress -- a colossal inverted pyramid of obsidian stone and crackling red energy -- rotates slowly above what used to be the town square of Gravity Falls. Lightning arcs from its peak into the shattered sky, where the interdimensional rift has torn open a swirling vortex of nightmarish colors no human eye was meant to see. The clouds aren't clouds anymore. They're alive, pulsing like organs, shot through with veins of golden light that spell out Bill's name over and over in a language older than Earth.\n\nDipper stares up at it, his hat clutched in one hand, Journal 3 pressed against his chest like a shield. Beside him, Mabel grips her grappling hook so tightly her knuckles have gone white. Her favorite sweater -- the one with the shooting star -- is torn at the sleeve and covered in dust from the collapsed water tower they'd hidden behind twenty minutes ago.\n\n\"We have to get up there,\" Dipper says, though his voice cracks on the last word. He clears his throat and tries again. \"Ford is probably inside. If anyone knows how to stop Bill, it's him.\"\n\n\"Dipper, look at the TOWN,\" Mabel whispers.\n\nHe looks. He wishes he hadn't. Gravity Falls has become a nightmare landscape. Main Street is buckled and split, with entire buildings floating ten, twenty, fifty feet in the air, rotating lazily like they've forgotten which way is down. The public library hangs upside-down, its books raining out in a constant waterfall of fluttering pages. Lazy Susan's diner has been stretched like taffy, elongated into an impossible spiral that corkscrews into the ground. The water tower -- the one that wasn't collapsed -- is walking on its support legs like a massive metal spider, patrolling the streets.\n\nWorst of all are the time bubbles. They drift through the wreckage like soap bubbles, translucent and shimmering with rainbow colors that would be beautiful if they weren't so terrifying. Inside each one, Dipper can see people trapped in loops -- Deputy Durland running from a mailbox over and over, Toby Determined tipping his hat to nobody again and again and again, their faces frozen in whatever expression they wore when the bubble caught them. The bubbles drift on unfelt winds, bumping gently off floating debris, occasionally merging with each other to create bigger, more complex loops.\n\nEye-bats circle overhead in lazy figure-eights, their massive singular eyes scanning the ground like searchlights. They're Bill's surveillance system -- giant eyeballs with bat wings, each one connected directly to Bill's consciousness. If one spots you, Bill knows exactly where you are.\n\nThe path to the Fearamid cuts straight through the worst of it. There's no safe detour. No back road. The Fearamid sits at the exact center of Weirdmageddon's chaos, and to reach it, you have to walk through the apocalypse.\n\n\"Okay, here's what I'm thinking,\" Dipper says, pulling out a crumpled map he's been sketching. \"We can either try to move fast and low through the shadows -- hide behind debris, time our movements between eye-bat patrols, avoid the time bubbles. It'll be slow, but if we're careful, we might reach the base without being spotted.\"\n\nMabel peers at the Fearamid, then back at Dipper. \"Or?\"\n\n\"Or we just... run for it. Straight shot. The Fearamid can't be more than half a mile away. If we sprint, we could be there in minutes. Maybe Bill won't even notice us. We're pretty small compared to everything else going on.\"\n\nMabel bites her lip. A time bubble drifts past them, close enough that they can hear the faint echoing sound from inside -- someone saying \"Wait, what was I--\" over and over. She shudders.\n\n\"Small is good,\" she says. \"Small and sneaky. Like mice. Mice in a sweater.\"\n\n\"That doesn't make sense.\"\n\n\"YOUR FACE doesn't make sense. Let's just pick a plan, bro-bro.\"\n\nAbove them, the Fearamid pulses with light, and for one horrible moment, the giant eye at its center seems to look directly at them. Then it sweeps on, scanning the horizon. They have seconds to decide.",
    choices: [
      { label: "Sneak through the shadows to avoid detection", target: "fearamid_sneak" },
      { label: "Charge straight toward the Fearamid boldly", target: "fearamid_charge" }
    ]
  },

  fearamid_charge: {
    text: "\"You know what? I'm TIRED of sneaking around!\" Mabel declares, standing up from behind the rubble. \"Bill already wrecked our town, kidnapped our friends, and ruined my ENTIRE scrapbooking schedule for the summer! We're the Pines twins! We don't hide!\"\n\nSomething ignites in Dipper's chest -- not quite bravery, maybe something closer to the feeling you get when you're so scared and angry that the two emotions crash into each other and produce something reckless. He stands up next to his sister.\n\n\"Yeah,\" he says, straightening his hat. \"Yeah! Let's go get him!\"\n\nThey charge. Straight down Main Street, leaping over cracks in reality, dodging floating mailboxes and tumbling cars. Mabel runs with her grappling hook held high like a sword. Dipper clutches the journal and pumps his legs harder than he ever has. The wind whips at their faces. Above them, eye-bats scatter in surprise -- nobody has been brave enough (or stupid enough) to run through the open like this since Weirdmageddon began.\n\nFor about thirty glorious seconds, it feels like it might actually work. The Fearamid looms larger and larger. They can see the chains dangling from its base, the swirling energy at its peak. They're close. They're so close.\n\nThen the sky laughs.\n\nNot the wind. Not thunder. The actual sky opens a mouth made of clouds and laughs, and the sound is high-pitched and manic and unmistakable. It's Bill. Of course it's Bill. Bill is everywhere now -- he IS Weirdmageddon.\n\n\"WELL, WELL, WELL!\" Bill Cipher's voice booms from every direction at once, echoing off floating buildings and reverberating through the twins' bones. \"IF IT ISN'T MY FAVORITE PAIR OF MEAT PUPPETS! PINE TREE AND SHOOTING STAR, COMING RIGHT TO MY FRONT DOOR!\"\n\nA massive golden eye materializes in the sky above them. Bill's pupil contracts as he looks down at them, and his voice drips with theatrical delight.\n\n\"YOU KNOW, I WAS JUST SAYING TO EIGHT-BALL -- I WAS SAYING, 'HEY EIGHT-BALL, WHAT'S THE ONE THING THIS PARTY IS MISSING?' AND HE SAID 'MORE SCREAMING,' AND I SAID 'NO, NO, MORE DECORATIONS!' AND HERE YOU TWO ARE, DELIVERING YOURSELVES LIKE PIZZA! I LOVE PIZZA!\"\n\nDipper skids to a halt. Mabel crashes into him. They look up at the eye, and for the first time, the enormity of what they've done hits them. They've run directly into the open, and the most powerful being in the universe is staring right at them.\n\n\"Dipper,\" Mabel whispers. \"I think we made a mistake.\"\n\n\"Ya think?!\"\n\nBill snaps his fingers. The sound is impossibly loud, like a thunderclap condensed into a single sharp crack. A wave of golden light ripples outward from the snap, washing over everything it touches. The floating debris turns to gold. The cracked pavement turns to gold. The wave races toward them like a shockwave, and there is absolutely nowhere to run.\n\nDipper grabs Mabel's hand. She grabs his. They squeeze tight.\n\nThe gold hits Dipper first. It starts at his feet -- a cold, heavy, paralyzing sensation like being encased in ice, except the ice is metal and it's climbing. His legs lock in mid-stride. His torso stiffens. His arm, still reaching for Mabel, freezes in place. The last thing to go is his face, and in the final half-second before the gold reaches his eyes, he sees Mabel beside him, her mouth open in a scream that never finishes, golden light crawling up her neck.\n\nThen everything is still.\n\nBill floats down to admire his new decorations. Two golden statues stand in the middle of Main Street -- a boy frozen in mid-run, one hand stretched out, his face caught between determination and terror. A girl beside him, mouth open, grappling hook raised, sweater frozen mid-flutter.\n\n\"GORGEOUS!\" Bill announces, clapping his tiny hands together. \"REALLY CAPTURES THE WHOLE 'FUTILE HEROISM' AESTHETIC I'VE BEEN GOING FOR.\"\n\nHe snaps his fingers again and teleports the golden twins to his throne room, where they're placed on either side of his massive throne. Dipper becomes a hat rack -- Bill hangs a tiny fez on his outstretched hand and cackles every time he looks at it. Mabel becomes an armrest. The Henchmaniacs think they're hilarious.\n\nThe worst part -- the absolute worst part -- is that they're still aware. Behind the gold, trapped inside the metal, Dipper and Mabel can still see, still hear, still think. They just can't move. Can't speak. Can't blink. They watch Bill's endless party from behind golden prisons, watching the demon celebrate his victory while the world burns outside.\n\nForever is a very long time when you're twelve.",
    ending: true
  },

  fearamid_sneak: {
    text: "\"Shadows it is,\" Dipper whispers, pulling Mabel behind the wreckage of a pickup truck that's been flipped upside-down and frozen in place three feet off the ground. \"Stay low, move between cover, and whatever you do, don't look the eye-bats directly in their... eye. According to the journal, they detect attention. If you stare at them, they can feel it.\"\n\n\"So we have to sneak past giant flying eyeballs without LOOKING at them?\" Mabel hisses. \"That's like telling me not to look at a puppy!\"\n\n\"These puppies will vaporize us.\"\n\n\"Fair point.\"\n\nThey begin their careful journey through the ruins of Gravity Falls. It's painstaking, terrifying work. Every few yards, they have to stop and press themselves flat against whatever cover they can find -- a collapsed wall, a floating dumpster, the hollowed-out shell of the Gravity Falls Gossiper newspaper office. The eye-bats sweep overhead in regular patterns, their massive pupils dilating and contracting as they scan the ground. Dipper times the intervals, counting under his breath. Fourteen seconds between sweeps. Fourteen seconds to move.\n\nThey dart from cover to cover like a pair of desperate rabbits. Past the ruins of Greasy's Diner, where the jukebox is still somehow playing the same country song on an endless loop. Past the town hall, which has been turned inside-out so that all the furniture and files are on the outside and the rooms are exposed to the open air. Past a time bubble that contains Old Man McGucket running in circles and cackling -- though honestly, Dipper isn't sure if that's the bubble's fault or if McGucket would be doing that anyway.\n\nAfter what feels like an hour but is probably fifteen minutes, they reach the base of the Fearamid's shadow. The fortress floats about two hundred feet above the town square, blotting out the fractured sky. Up close, it's even more terrifying -- the obsidian surface is covered in symbols and carvings that seem to move when you aren't looking directly at them. Faces in the stone. Eyes that blink. Mouths that almost smile.\n\nAnd there, dangling from the Fearamid's lowest point, is a massive chain. It's as thick as a tree trunk, each link the size of a car, made of some dark metal that seems to absorb light. The chain plunges into the ground like an anchor, keeping the Fearamid tethered to this exact spot above the town square. Smaller chains branch off from it, connecting to various floating buildings that orbit the pyramid like captured moons.\n\n\"That's our way up,\" Dipper says, pointing.\n\n\"There's a small problem,\" Mabel replies, pointing lower.\n\nAt the base of the chain, sitting on the ground and gnawing on what appears to be an entire fire hydrant, is Teeth. He's one of Bill's Henchmaniacs -- a massive set of disembodied teeth arranged in a perpetual grin, with tiny arms and legs sticking out at odd angles. He's not the most intimidating of Bill's minions (that honor goes to 8-Ball or Pyronica), but he's still a supernatural demon with the power to bite through solid steel, and he's sitting right where they need to go.\n\nTeeth hums tunelessly to himself as he chews on the fire hydrant, producing an awful grinding-metal sound. He doesn't seem particularly alert -- his eyes (wedged between his molars) are half-closed, and he keeps nodding off between bites. But he's right there, blocking the only way up.\n\n\"We need to get past him,\" Dipper whispers, flipping through the journal frantically. \"Teeth -- minor demon, summoned from the Nightmare Realm, weakness... weakness... come on, Ford, you wrote about everything else...\"\n\n\"I have silly string,\" Mabel offers, rummaging through her sweater pockets. She pulls out an impossible amount of supplies -- silly string, glitter, a marble, three stickers, half a candy bar, and what appears to be a small bottle of perfume. \"And I have an idea.\"\n\n\"You always have an idea.\"\n\n\"My ideas are GREAT and you know it.\"\n\nDipper looks at Teeth, then at the chain, then back at his sister. They could try to distract the demon -- lure him away with some clever trick. Or they could wait for a patrol change. Bill's Henchmaniacs rotate guard positions, and if they're patient, Teeth might leave on his own. But patience is dangerous here. Every minute they spend on the ground is another minute the eye-bats might spot them.",
    choices: [
      { label: "Distract Teeth with a clever trick", target: "fearamid_distract" },
      { label: "Wait for a patrol change", target: "fearamid_wait" }
    ]
  },

  fearamid_distract: {
    text: "\"Okay, here's the plan,\" Mabel whispers, and Dipper is immediately both impressed and terrified because Mabel's plans are either brilliant or catastrophic and there is never any in-between. \"You know how in the journal it says demons are attracted to chaos and sensory overload? And you know how Teeth specifically is obsessed with things he can bite?\"\n\n\"Yeah...?\"\n\n\"So what if we give him something he REALLY wants to chomp on, but like, over THERE?\" She points to a spot about fifty yards from the chain's base, where a collapsed awning from the hardware store creates a sheltered alcove.\n\nDipper starts to see where this is going. \"You want to bait him.\"\n\n\"I want to create the most IRRESISTIBLE chew toy a giant set of demon teeth has ever seen.\" Mabel's eyes are gleaming with that particular light that means she's either about to save their lives or get them killed. \"Step one: the silly string creates a web structure -- something crunchy and satisfying. Step two: the glitter makes it sparkle, because everything is better with glitter. Step three...\" She pulls out the journal and flips to a page Dipper marked earlier. \"This symbol. You said it means 'delicious' in the demon tongue, right?\"\n\n\"I said it MIGHT mean delicious. It could also mean 'explosion.' The dialect is unclear.\"\n\n\"Close enough!\"\n\nWorking quickly and quietly, they construct their trap. Mabel weaves silly string into an elaborate web between two pieces of floating debris, packing it with glitter and shaping it into something that, if you squint, looks like a giant sparkling candy cane. Dipper carefully draws the symbol from the journal onto the structure using a marker. The symbol glows faintly when he completes it -- which is either a very good sign or a very bad sign.\n\n\"Now we need something to get his attention,\" Mabel says. She pulls out the small bottle of perfume -- it's one of her homemade concoctions, a mixture she calls \"Eau de Mabel\" that smells like a combination of sugar, pine needles, and pure enthusiasm. She sprays it liberally on the bait. The scent drifts on the supernatural wind.\n\nTeeth's eyes snap open. Every surface of his massive dental array starts salivating. A sound comes from deep within his enamel -- a low, hungry moan.\n\n\"WHAT IS THAT SMELL,\" Teeth groans, his tiny legs already carrying him away from the chain. \"IT SMELLS LIKE... LIKE CRUNCHING. LIKE THE MOST SATISFYING CRUNCH IN THE UNIVERSE.\"\n\nHe waddles toward the bait, his entire being focused on this strange, glittery, incredible-smelling thing. The moment he reaches it, he opens wide and chomps down. The silly string, glitter, and magic symbol all crunch together in his jaws, and Teeth makes a sound of pure ecstasy. He's completely absorbed. He won't be moving for a while.\n\n\"GO GO GO!\" Dipper hisses.\n\nThey sprint for the chain. Mabel fires her grappling hook at the lowest link, and it catches with a satisfying clank. They haul themselves up, climbing hand over hand, feet scrambling against the dark metal. The chain sways gently beneath their weight, but it holds -- it's anchoring an entire floating pyramid, after all. Two kids are nothing.\n\nThey climb for what feels like forever, the wind picking up as they gain altitude. Below them, Gravity Falls shrinks into a patchwork of destruction. Above them, the Fearamid's underside looms like a dark ceiling, covered in more of those shifting symbols.\n\nFinally, they reach a gap in the Fearamid's base -- a spot where the chain passes through an opening in the stone. They squeeze through and tumble onto a cold, dark floor.\n\nThe inside of the Fearamid is wrong. That's the only word for it. The corridors stretch in directions that don't exist -- not just left and right and up and down, but sideways through dimensions that human eyes aren't built to process. Staircases run along the walls and across the ceiling. Doors open onto rooms that are bigger on the inside than the outside, or rooms that contain other rooms that contain the first room. Gravity shifts with every step -- one moment the floor is beneath them, the next it's a wall, and they're walking along what used to be the ceiling.\n\nBill's laughter echoes from everywhere and nowhere, a constant background noise like the world's worst elevator music. It's not directed at them -- Bill is laughing at something else, entertaining his demons in his throne room far above. But the sound follows them through every corridor, bouncing off impossible angles.\n\n\"This place is giving me a headache,\" Mabel mutters, walking on what she's pretty sure is a wall. \"And not the fun kind of headache you get from eating ice cream too fast.\"\n\nDipper consults the journal, flipping to Ford's notes on interdimensional architecture. \"According to Ford, the Fearamid's layout is based on non-Euclidean geometry. The throne room should be at the center-top, but the lower levels...\" He traces a diagram with his finger. \"The lower levels are where Bill keeps his prisoners.\"",
    choices: [
      { label: "Navigate toward Bill's throne room", target: "fearamid_throne" },
      { label: "Explore the Fearamid's lower levels", target: "fearamid_lower" }
    ]
  },

  fearamid_wait: {
    text: "\"Let's wait,\" Dipper decides, pulling Mabel behind a chunk of floating sidewalk that provides decent cover. \"The Henchmaniacs rotate guard positions. I've been watching them from the water tower -- Teeth should move in about ten minutes when his shift ends.\"\n\n\"Ten minutes,\" Mabel repeats, hugging her knees. \"Ten minutes isn't so bad. I can hold my breath for like forty seconds. That's way less than ten minutes.\"\n\n\"You don't need to hold your breath.\"\n\n\"I KNOW, I'm just saying I COULD.\"\n\nThey huddle behind the debris and wait. The ruined town around them groans and creaks, reality straining under the weight of Weirdmageddon. Somewhere in the distance, a building crashes to the ground with a sound like thunder. An eye-bat screeches overhead, its shadow passing over them like a dark cloud. They press closer to their cover.\n\nFive minutes pass. Then seven. Teeth is still gnawing his fire hydrant, showing no signs of leaving. Dipper's count must have been off. The Henchmaniacs don't keep perfect schedules -- they're demons of chaos, not office workers.\n\n\"Dipper,\" Mabel says suddenly, her voice tight. \"Don't move.\"\n\nHe freezes. Then he sees it. A time bubble is drifting toward them. It's about the size of a beach ball, shimmering with iridescent light, and it's floating directly at their hiding spot with the lazy inevitability of a soap bubble on a summer breeze. Inside it, Dipper can see a tiny loop playing -- a leaf falling from a tree, over and over, caught in an endless autumn.\n\nThey scramble backward, but the floating sidewalk they're hiding behind limits their movement. The time bubble bobs closer, closer, close enough that Dipper can feel the weird tingling emanation it gives off -- a sensation like static electricity mixed with deja vu.\n\n\"Go left!\" Dipper whispers urgently.\n\nMabel dives left, but not quite fast enough. The edge of the time bubble brushes against her sweater sleeve -- the right one, the one that's already torn. And something terrible happens.\n\nMabel's arm freezes. Not like the golden statue freeze from Bill's power, but a temporal freeze -- her forearm starts looping. Her hand reaches forward, pulls back, reaches forward, pulls back, reaching-pulling-reaching-pulling, the same half-second of movement repeating over and over and over. Her sleeve ripples in a wind that only exists in that one frozen moment.\n\n\"MABEL!\" Dipper grabs her other arm and PULLS. For a horrible, stretching instant, he can feel the time bubble pulling back, trying to draw more of Mabel into its loop. Her looping arm tugs toward the bubble like a magnet. Mabel's face is a mask of pure terror -- she can feel it, the sensation of being stuck, of being caught in an eternal repetition, of the same nerve signals firing again and again without end.\n\nDipper plants his feet and heaves with everything he has. With a sound like tearing fabric -- or maybe tearing TIME -- Mabel's arm rips free of the bubble's influence. They tumble backward together, crashing onto the broken pavement. The time bubble, disturbed by the interaction, wobbles and floats away, carrying its little looping leaf off into the ruined sky.\n\nMabel cradles her arm, breathing hard. \"That was... that was the worst thing I've ever felt,\" she gasps. \"I could feel my arm doing the same thing over and over, and I couldn't stop it, and it felt like it was going to be forever, Dipper. FOREVER.\"\n\n\"I know. I know. Are you okay?\"\n\nShe flexes her fingers. They work. The loop is broken. \"I'm okay. But those things are HORRIBLE.\"\n\nDipper looks at the retreating time bubble, then back at the chain. Teeth has finally stopped chewing and seems to be dozing off. The path to the chain is as clear as it's going to get.\n\nBut something is nagging at Dipper's brain. The time bubbles. They trap things in loops. They're dangerous and unpredictable. But what if... what if you could control one? What if instead of being a hazard, a time bubble could be a weapon? He remembers that Ford's supply bag, which they found earlier in the bunker, contained some specialized containers for capturing anomalous phenomena. One of them might be able to hold a small time bubble.\n\nIt's a crazy idea. But in a world where the sky is full of screaming and a triangle rules the universe, crazy ideas might be the only kind worth having.",
    choices: [
      { label: "Run for the chain now while the path is clear", target: "fearamid_climb" },
      { label: "The time bubbles give Dipper an idea - could they weaponize one?", target: "fearamid_timebubble" }
    ]
  },

  fearamid_climb: {
    text: "\"Now! Go now!\" Dipper hisses, and they break from cover like startled deer. Teeth is slumped against the chain's base, his eyes closed, a thin line of drool running down his porcelain surface. He's out cold -- or whatever the demon equivalent of sleep is. The twins tiptoe past him, close enough to smell his breath (which smells, predictably, like industrial-strength mouthwash and brimstone), and reach the chain.\n\nUp close, the chain is enormous. Each link is taller than Dipper and made of some dark, otherworldly metal that's warm to the touch, like it's alive. Strange symbols are etched into every surface -- Bill's cipher, of course, the triangle-and-eye motif repeated in endless variations. But there are also older symbols, ones that look like they predate Bill entirely. Ford's handwriting in the journal identified them as belonging to an ancient language called the \"Speech of the Axolotl,\" which Dipper files away for later because right now he has bigger problems.\n\nLike climbing.\n\n\"Mabel, grappling hook.\"\n\n\"Way ahead of you, bro-bro.\"\n\nMabel fires the grappling hook upward. It catches on a link about thirty feet above them and holds. They climb, hand over hand, Mabel pulling herself up with the hook's rope while Dipper wedges his fingers and feet into the gaps between links. The metal is warm and slightly slippery -- covered in a thin layer of something that Dipper desperately hopes is condensation and not demon sweat.\n\nThey climb. And climb. And climb. The chain sways in winds that shouldn't exist -- not natural wind, but the breath of Weirdmageddon itself, hot and cold in alternating waves, carrying sounds from other dimensions. Dipper hears whispers in languages he doesn't understand, snatches of music played on instruments that haven't been invented, and underneath it all, Bill's constant, distant laughter.\n\nBelow them, Gravity Falls falls away. From up here, the town looks like a toy set that a giant child has stomped on and then set on fire. The Mystery Shack is a tiny shape in the distance, miraculously still standing. The lake has turned an unnatural purple. The forest is petrified -- every tree turned to stone. And everywhere, everywhere, the time bubbles drift like deadly soap bubbles, catching the fractured light.\n\n\"Don't look down,\" Mabel says.\n\n\"I already looked down.\"\n\n\"How bad is it?\"\n\n\"Don't look down.\"\n\nThey're about halfway up -- maybe a hundred feet off the ground, the Fearamid still looming above them -- when they hear it. A sound like geometric shapes being shuffled. A crystalline tinkling, like wind chimes made of mathematics.\n\nKryptos drops out of the sky and hovers directly in front of them.\n\nHe's one of Bill's Henchmaniacs -- a small, angular demon shaped like a cipher key, with a single eye and a body made of interconnected geometric shapes that rotate and shift constantly. He's not big. He's not even particularly scary-looking. But he can fly, and they're hanging from a chain a hundred feet in the air, and that changes the equation considerably.\n\n\"INTRUDERS,\" Kryptos announces in a voice like a calculator having a nervous breakdown. \"TWO HUMAN LARVAE. DESIGNATIONS: PINE TREE AND SHOOTING STAR. PRIORITY TARGETS. INITIATING APPREHENSION PROTOCOL.\"\n\n\"Oh no,\" Dipper breathes.\n\nKryptos extends a geometric arm and fires a beam of blue-white energy at the chain just above them. The metal glows red where the beam hits it. He's trying to sever the chain -- or at least shake them off.\n\nThe chain shudders violently. Mabel's grappling hook slips, and she drops two feet before catching herself on a link, her legs dangling over nothing. Dipper wraps his arms around his link and holds on for dear life. The wind howls. Kryptos fires again, and the chain lurches sideways like a snake.\n\n\"WE HAVE TO DO SOMETHING!\" Mabel screams over the wind.\n\nDipper's mind races. They're exposed on this chain -- no cover, no leverage, no way to fight a flying demon while clinging to metal links a hundred feet up. But Mabel's grappling hook has range, and off to the left, maybe forty feet away, there's a window in the Fearamid's surface -- a dark opening in the obsidian stone that leads to the fortress's interior.\n\nAlternatively, they could try to fight Kryptos directly. He's one of the weaker Henchmaniacs -- the journal says he's insecure about his power level and overcompensates with aggression. Maybe if they can knock him off balance, literally or figuratively, they can keep climbing.\n\nKryptos charges another shot. The chain groans beneath them. Time is running out.",
    choices: [
      { label: "Mabel uses her grappling hook to swing to a window", target: "fearamid_lower" },
      { label: "Fight Kryptos and keep climbing", target: "ending_fearamid_fall" }
    ]
  },

  fearamid_timebubble: {
    text: "\"Mabel, wait. I have an idea.\" Dipper reaches into the supply bag they salvaged from Ford's underground bunker -- the one packed with strange devices and containers labeled in Ford's cramped, precise handwriting. He pulls out a glass cylinder about the size of a thermos, its surface etched with containment symbols that glow faintly blue. The label reads: ANOMALY CONTAINMENT VESSEL -- CLASS 3. CAUTION: DO NOT USE FOR COFFEE.\n\n\"Ford built these to capture supernatural phenomena,\" Dipper explains, turning the cylinder over in his hands. \"They can hold anything from a ghost to a gravity anomaly. And a time bubble is basically a localized temporal anomaly, which means...\"\n\n\"Which means you want to catch one,\" Mabel finishes. \"Like a firefly. A horrible, time-destroying firefly.\"\n\n\"If we can trap a small time bubble in this container, we'd have a weapon. Think about it -- we could release it at just the right moment and trap an enemy in a time loop. Even one of Bill's Henchmaniacs. Maybe even Bill himself.\"\n\nMabel stares at him. \"Dipper, that thing almost ate my arm. My ARM. The arm I use for knitting, punching, and high-fiving. I am not a fan of time bubbles.\"\n\n\"I know. I know it's dangerous. But everything about this is dangerous. We're two twelve-year-olds trying to break into a demon's fortress during the apocalypse. At some point, dangerous stopped being a reason not to do something.\"\n\nShe considers this. Then she sighs. \"Ugh, you're right. I hate it when you're right. Okay, how do we catch one?\"\n\nThey spend the next several minutes tracking a small time bubble -- one about the size of a grapefruit, drifting lazily through the ruins near their hiding spot. Inside it, a hummingbird flaps its wings in an endless loop, frozen in a single wingbeat repeated infinitely. It's actually kind of beautiful, in a deeply unsettling way.\n\nDipper approaches it carefully, the containment vessel held out in front of him like a net. His hands are shaking. He can feel the bubble's temporal field from three feet away -- that weird tingling, the sensation of deja vu, the feeling that time itself is hiccupping.\n\n\"Easy... easy...\" He inches the open end of the vessel toward the bubble. The containment symbols on the glass begin to glow brighter, reacting to the temporal energy. The bubble seems to sense the vessel and drifts slightly away, like a soap bubble caught in a draft.\n\n\"Come on,\" Dipper mutters. \"Come on, come on, come on...\"\n\nHe lunges. The vessel closes around the time bubble with a sound like a clock striking thirteen. The glass flares with light, the symbols spinning and rearranging themselves as they work to contain the captured anomaly. For a terrifying moment, the vessel vibrates violently in Dipper's hands, and he's sure it's going to shatter and release the time loop right into his face.\n\nThen the symbols lock into place. The glowing subsides. Inside the vessel, the tiny time bubble floats peacefully, the hummingbird still looping its endless wingbeat. Contained. Controlled. A piece of broken time in a bottle.\n\n\"Ha HA!\" Dipper holds the vessel up triumphantly. \"It worked!\"\n\n\"Great! Wonderful! Now please stop holding the time grenade over your head like a trophy,\" Mabel says nervously.\n\nDipper carefully tucks the vessel into the supply bag. He can feel it humming against his back, warm and strange, a heartbeat of captured time. It's powerful. He knows it's powerful. But he also knows it's unpredictable.\n\nTime isn't meant to be bottled. It's wild and fundamental, one of the basic forces of reality, and shoving a piece of it into a glass tube is like trying to keep lightning in a jar. It works until it doesn't. And when it stops working, the results could be catastrophic.\n\n\"Okay,\" Dipper says, squaring his shoulders. \"We've got our weapon. Now we need to get up there.\" He looks up at the Fearamid, still floating above them like a dark promise. The chain is still there. Teeth is still guarding it, but starting to look drowsy.\n\nMabel flexes her fingers. \"Time bubble in the bag, grappling hook in the hand, and a mostly positive attitude. Let's do this.\"\n\nBut a quiet voice in the back of Dipper's mind whispers a warning: using this weapon means releasing broken time into an already broken world. If it hits the wrong target, or if it interacts badly with the Fearamid's energy, or if Bill finds a way to turn it against them... the consequences could be worse than anything they're trying to prevent.\n\nThe question is: do they bring it, or leave it behind?",
    choices: [
      { label: "Bring the time bubble weapon to the Fearamid", target: "fearamid_throne" },
      { label: "It's too dangerous - go to the Fearamid without it", target: "fearamid_climb" }
    ]
  },

  fearamid_throne: {
    text: "After navigating the impossible corridors of the Fearamid -- walking on ceilings, climbing through rooms that fold in on themselves, passing doorways that open onto views of galaxies that shouldn't exist -- the twins finally find Bill's throne room. They hear it before they see it: the sound of a party. Not a human party with music and laughter, but a DEMON party with shrieking, explosions, the sound of reality being torn like wrapping paper, and underneath it all, Bill Cipher's cackling voice telling jokes that make the walls bleed.\n\nDipper presses his eye to a crack in the massive double doors and immediately wishes he hadn't.\n\nThe throne room is the size of a football stadium, carved from the same dark stone as the rest of the Fearamid but decorated with trophies of Bill's conquest. And by trophies, Dipper means people. The petrified forms of those who opposed Bill line the walls like statues in a museum -- town council members, forest creatures, even a few unlucky government agents, all frozen in stone with expressions of terror etched permanently on their faces. Their eyes are still alive. Dipper can see them moving behind the stone, watching, trapped.\n\nIn the center of the room, Bill lounges on a throne made of human-sized playing cards that constantly shuffle and rearrange themselves. He's enormous -- at least thirty feet tall in this form, his golden body radiating light that casts sharp geometric shadows across the floor. His single eye surveys his domain with smug satisfaction.\n\nThe Henchmaniacs are everywhere. 8-Ball is arm-wrestling Pyronica (a tall, elegant demon made of living fire) and losing badly. Hectorgon is trying to organize a conga line but nobody is cooperating. Paci-Fire -- a giant baby-faced demon with a pacifier -- is having a screaming tantrum in the corner that's causing localized earthquakes. Keyhole is trying to pick his own lock. And Teeth, apparently recovered from guard duty, is gnawing on what looks disturbingly like a park bench.\n\nIt's chaos. Literally -- these are beings of chaos, and their idea of a party is barely distinguishable from a natural disaster.\n\n\"How do we...\" Mabel starts, but she trails off because Bill has turned his eye toward the doors. Toward them.\n\n\"WELL, COME ON IN!\" Bill's voice booms through the doors so loudly that both twins stumble backward. \"I CAN SEE YOU, PINE TREE. I'VE GOT REALLY GOOD PERIPHERAL VISION -- WELL, I'VE GOT REALLY GOOD VISION PERIOD. I'M A GIANT EYE. THAT'S SORT OF MY THING.\"\n\nThe doors swing open on their own. Every Henchmaniac in the room turns to look at them. Dozens of mismatched, inhuman eyes focus on two very small, very scared twelve-year-olds standing in a doorway that's fifty feet tall.\n\nBill floats down from his throne, shrinking as he descends until he's hovering at their eye level -- a three-foot-tall triangle in a top hat, his eye curved in what passes for a smile. He extends a thin black arm.\n\n\"PINE TREE! SHOOTING STAR! MY TWO FAVORITE CARBON-BASED LIFEFORMS! I'M SO GLAD YOU COULD MAKE IT TO MY LITTLE SHINDIG. DID YOU RSVP? I DON'T THINK YOU RSVP'D. THAT'S KIND OF RUDE, BUT HEY, I'M A MAGNANIMOUS HOST.\"\n\n\"We're here to stop you, Bill,\" Dipper says, and his voice only shakes a little bit, which he considers a victory.\n\nBill's eye widens with delight. \"STOP ME? OH, PINE TREE. PINE TREE, PINE TREE, PINE TREE.\" He circles them slowly, floating in a lazy orbit. \"I HAVE LITERALLY REWRITTEN THE LAWS OF PHYSICS. I TURNED YOUR MAYOR INTO A PIECE OF FURNITURE. I MADE TIME MY PERSONAL PLAYTHING. AND YOU TWO -- A KID WITH A HAT AND A KID WITH A SWEATER -- ARE GOING TO STOP ME?\"\n\nHe pauses. His eye narrows to a slit. Then it widens again, and he throws his tiny arms wide.\n\n\"I LOVE IT! THIS IS THE BEST ENTERTAINMENT I'VE HAD SINCE I REARRANGED THE CONSTELLATIONS TO SPELL RUDE WORDS! SO! WHAT'S YOUR PLAY, KIDS?\"\n\nDipper and Mabel exchange a look. They've made it this far. They're standing in front of the most powerful being in the universe, surrounded by his demons, deep inside his fortress. The smart thing would be to have a plan. The Pines thing is to figure it out as they go.",
    choices: [
      { label: "Challenge Bill directly", target: "fearamid_challenge" },
      { label: "Offer to make a deal with Bill", target: "fearamid_deal" }
    ]
  },

  fearamid_lower: {
    text: "The lower levels of the Fearamid are nothing like the chaotic party atmosphere above. Down here, the corridors are narrow, dark, and dripping with something that looks like liquid shadow. The walls are lined with cells -- not modern cells with bars, but ancient-looking chambers sealed with glowing symbols, each one containing something Bill wants to keep locked away. Some cells hold creatures Dipper has never seen before -- beings from other dimensions, captured during Weirdmageddon and kept as curiosities. One cell contains what appears to be an entire miniature galaxy, swirling in the darkness. Another holds a creature made entirely of sound, its formless body producing a constant low hum that vibrates Dipper's teeth.\n\nBut most of the cells hold people. Townspeople who resisted. Creatures from the forest who tried to fight back. A family of manotaurs, depowered and miserable. A cluster of gnomes, huddled together for warmth. Even the Multibear, all his heads hanging low.\n\n\"This is horrible,\" Mabel whispers, pressing her hand against the glass of a cell containing Pacifica Northwest, frozen in crystal with tears still visible on her cheeks. \"Dipper, we have to help them.\"\n\n\"We will. But first we need to find--\"\n\n\"Dipper? MABEL?\"\n\nThe voice is weak, hoarse, barely above a whisper, but unmistakable. Dipper would know that voice anywhere -- the voice of late-night scientific explanations, of warnings about the supernatural, of a man who spent thirty years lost between dimensions and came back stranger and wiser.\n\n\"GRUNKLE FORD!\"\n\nThey find him in one of the larger cells, near the end of the corridor. Stanford Pines -- Great Uncle Ford, the Author of the Journals, the man with six fingers on each hand -- is slumped against the wall of his prison. He looks terrible. His glasses are cracked. His coat is torn. The bags under his eyes suggest he hasn't slept in days. But when he sees the twins, a light comes into his face that wasn't there before.\n\n\"Kids. Oh, kids.\" He presses his six-fingered hands against the cell's barrier -- a shimmering field of energy that sparks when he touches it. \"How did you get in here? You shouldn't be here. It's too dangerous.\"\n\n\"Yeah, we noticed,\" Mabel says. \"The giant demon party upstairs was kind of a giveaway.\"\n\n\"Ford, we came to rescue you,\" Dipper says, already scanning the cell's barrier for a weakness. \"And to stop Bill. How do we stop him?\"\n\nFord closes his eyes. When he opens them, they're filled with a complicated mixture of hope and fear. \"There are... there are two possibilities. Two paths.\"\n\nHe sits up straighter, wincing as he moves. Bill hasn't been gentle with him. \"The first is the Zodiac. The Cipher Wheel. You've seen the symbol in the journal -- ten symbols arranged in a circle, each one corresponding to a specific person. If the right people stand in the right positions and join hands, the Zodiac generates a frequency that can destabilize Bill's power. It's the prophecy, Dipper. The prophecy I spent decades trying to understand.\"\n\n\"So we just need to get the right people together?\" Mabel asks, her eyes bright.\n\n\"It's not that simple.\" Ford shakes his head. \"We need ALL ten. Every symbol, every person, in exactly the right position. And some of those people... I don't even know where they are. The Zodiac is powerful, but it's fragile. An incomplete circle won't just fail -- it'll BACKFIRE.\"\n\n\"You said two possibilities,\" Dipper presses. \"What's the second?\"\n\nFord's voice drops to barely a whisper. He leans close to the barrier, and Dipper leans in to hear.\n\n\"The Axolotl.\"\n\nThe word hangs in the air like a secret. Dipper feels the hair on the back of his neck stand up, though he doesn't know why.\n\n\"There is a being,\" Ford continues, his voice trembling with something between awe and terror, \"that exists outside of time and space. Outside of ALL dimensions. A cosmic entity so vast and ancient that even Bill -- Bill who fears NOTHING -- is afraid of it. It's called the Axolotl. A great cosmic salamander, Dipper. The guardian of the in-between spaces. I've spent decades researching it. I have fragments of a summoning incantation. A piece of an ancient map that shows the way to its dimension.\"\n\nHe pulls a crumpled piece of paper from inside his coat -- somehow Bill's demons didn't find it -- and presses it against the barrier. Dipper can see symbols, coordinates, a fragment of text in a language he doesn't recognize.\n\n\"The Axolotl is the one being in existence that has the power to truly undo what Bill has done,\" Ford says. \"Not just defeat him -- UNDO him. But reaching the Axolotl... that's a journey in itself. And I don't know what it would ask in return.\"\n\nFord looks at both of them, his cracked glasses reflecting the glow of his prison.\n\n\"What do you want to do, kids?\"",
    choices: [
      { label: "Free Ford and attempt the Zodiac ritual", target: "fearamid_zodiac" },
      { label: "Ask Ford about the Axolotl", target: "fearamid_axolotl_info" }
    ]
  },

  fearamid_challenge: {
    text: "\"I challenge you, Bill Cipher!\" Dipper steps forward, his voice ringing through the throne room with a conviction that surprises even him. He holds up Journal 3, open to the page about Bill, and jabs his finger at the illustration. \"You think you're all-powerful? You think nobody can touch you? I've been studying you all summer. I know your weaknesses. I know your history. And I know you can be beaten!\"\n\nThe throne room goes silent. Every Henchmaniac stops what they're doing. 8-Ball's arm-wrestling match pauses mid-flex. Pyronica's flames flicker with interest. Even Paci-Fire stops screaming. Every eye in the room turns to the small boy with the pine tree hat who just challenged the most powerful being in the universe.\n\nBill stares at Dipper for a long, long moment. His eye is unreadable -- that single, golden pupil fixed on the boy like a spotlight.\n\nThen he starts laughing. Not his usual manic cackle, but a slow, genuine laugh that builds and builds until the entire Fearamid is shaking with it. Tears (of fire) stream from his eye. He slaps his tiny knee.\n\n\"OH MAN. OH MAN OH MAN OH MAN.\" Bill wipes his eye and takes a deep, theatrically shaky breath. \"I NEEDED THAT. I REALLY DID. OKAY, KID, YOU KNOW WHAT? I RESPECT THE AUDACITY. I REALLY DO. SO I'LL TELL YOU WHAT -- I'LL GIVE YOU A SPORTING CHANCE.\"\n\nHe snaps his fingers, and the Henchmaniacs clear to the edges of the room, forming a loose circle like spectators at a boxing match. Bill floats down to the floor and, with a shimmer of golden light, shrinks himself to the twins' size -- a three-foot-tall triangle hovering just off the ground.\n\n\"THERE. FAIR AND SQUARE. SAME SIZE. MANO A MANO. OR, WELL, MANO A TRIANGULO. LET'S SEE WHAT YOU'VE GOT, PINE TREE.\"\n\nDipper knows this is a trap. He KNOWS it. Bill is still infinitely more powerful than him, regardless of size. But they're here, and there's no backing down now.\n\nThe fight begins, and it is immediately, spectacularly unfair.\n\nDipper charges with the journal held like a shield. Bill snaps his fingers and the floor beneath Dipper turns to lava -- bright orange, radiating heat, bubbling. Dipper leaps sideways, landing on a floating tile that Bill helpfully provides (hovering six inches above the molten floor). The tile starts shrinking.\n\nMabel swings her grappling hook at Bill. He catches it with one hand, examines it curiously, and turns it into a live snake. Mabel screams and drops it. The snake turns into flowers. The flowers turn into bees. The bees turn back into a grappling hook. Bill giggles.\n\n\"COME ON, KIDS! I'M GOING EASY ON YOU!\"\n\nDipper flips through the journal, looking for anything useful. Barrier spells -- no, those require preparation. Banishment rituals -- no, those require ingredients they don't have. Bill's weaknesses: ego, overconfidence, inability to resist a challenge to his intelligence--\n\nBill snaps his fingers again. The ceiling turns into snakes. Actual living snakes, hundreds of them, dangling down and hissing. One drops onto Dipper's shoulder and he swats it away, only for it to turn into a rubber duck that quacks indignantly.\n\n\"THIS IS THE BEST TUESDAY I'VE HAD IN EONS!\" Bill announces, dodging a punch from Mabel that passes right through his body. \"OR IS IT WEDNESDAY? TIME DOESN'T REALLY WORK ANYMORE. MY BAD.\"\n\nDipper fights with everything he knows. He tries the mystic symbols from Journal 2, which produce a brief flash that makes Bill flinch. He uses the supernatural combat techniques from Journal 3, which are designed for fighting ghosts and prove mostly useless against a dream demon. He even tries the simple stuff -- throwing rocks, which Bill turns into butterflies; tackling, which results in Dipper passing through Bill's body and hitting the wall.\n\nThrough it all, Bill is laughing. Not cruelly -- delightedly. He's having the time of his life. This is all a game to him. The twins are toys and the Fearamid is his playroom.\n\nBut Dipper notices something. Every time Bill warps reality -- every time he turns the floor to lava or the ceiling to snakes or a grappling hook to flowers -- there's a tiny delay. A fraction of a second where Bill's attention is focused on the transformation. His eye narrows in concentration. He's powerful beyond measure, but he's not literally omnipotent. Each act of reality-warping requires a moment of focus.\n\nWhich means he can be distracted. And if he can be distracted, he can be tricked.\n\nDipper locks eyes with Mabel across the chaotic room. She nods. She sees it too.",
    choices: [
      { label: "Use the time bubble weapon on Bill (if they have it)", target: "fearamid_timebubble_bill" },
      { label: "Try to outsmart Bill with a riddle", target: "fearamid_riddle" }
    ]
  },

  fearamid_deal: {
    text: "\"Wait!\" Mabel steps forward, hands raised. Dipper grabs her sleeve, but she shakes him off. \"Bill, wait. We don't want to fight.\"\n\nBill pauses mid-hover, his eye widening with surprise and then narrowing with interest. The Henchmaniacs murmur among themselves. This isn't what they expected.\n\n\"Mabel, what are you DOING?\" Dipper hisses.\n\n\"Shh. Trust me.\" She turns back to Bill and takes a deep breath. \"We want to make a deal.\"\n\nThe word 'deal' sends a visible ripple through the room. Bill's body flickers with blue light -- the color of his deal-fire, the supernatural contract flame that binds parties to their agreements. It's the one thing about Bill that's actually reliable: he keeps his deals. Not because he's honorable, but because deal-fire is a force even older than he is, and breaking a deal has consequences even a being of pure chaos can't escape.\n\n\"A DEAL!\" Bill's voice drops to a purr -- soft, intimate, dangerous. He floats closer, his eye half-lidded with pleasure. \"NOW YOU'RE SPEAKING MY LANGUAGE, SHOOTING STAR. I DO LOVE A GOOD DEAL. WHAT DID YOU HAVE IN MIND?\"\n\n\"Our family,\" Mabel says, her voice firm even as her hands tremble at her sides. \"Stan, Ford, Soos, Wendy -- everyone we care about. You let them go. Free. Unharmed. No tricks, no curses, no turning them into furniture.\"\n\nBill circles them slowly, his eye gleaming. \"AND IN RETURN?\"\n\nMabel swallows hard. \"We leave. Dipper and I leave Gravity Falls. Forever. We never come back. You get the town, your Weirdmageddon, your party -- all of it. No more Pines twins getting in your way. No more mystery-solving. No more journals. We're gone.\"\n\nThe silence in the throne room is absolute. Dipper stares at his sister like she's lost her mind. Maybe she has. But there's something in her eyes -- not defeat, exactly, but a fierce protectiveness. She'd give up everything to save the people she loves. That's who Mabel is.\n\n\"Mabel, we can't--\" Dipper starts.\n\n\"SHUSH, PINE TREE, THE ADULTS ARE TALKING.\" Bill flicks his finger and Dipper's mouth seals shut -- not painfully, just suddenly, like a zipper closing. He'll get his voice back, but not until the negotiation is done.\n\nBill floats in front of Mabel, eye to eye. This close, she can see the infinity swirling in his pupil -- galaxies, dimensions, the burning wreckage of reality itself. He extends his hand, and blue fire erupts around it, dancing along his fingers.\n\n\"LET ME GET THIS STRAIGHT. I RELEASE YOUR FAMILY -- ALIVE, WELL, NO TRICKS -- AND YOU TWO LEAVE GRAVITY FALLS FOREVER. NEVER RETURN. NEVER INTERFERE WITH MY PLANS AGAIN.\"\n\n\"That's the deal.\"\n\n\"AND IF YOU BREAK YOUR END -- IF EITHER OF YOU SETS FOOT IN GRAVITY FALLS AGAIN -- THE DEAL IS VOID AND EVERYONE YOU LOVE GETS TURNED INTO THROW PILLOWS.\"\n\n\"...Fine.\"\n\nBill's eye curves into a crescent of pure satisfaction. He extends his burning hand further. The blue fire is warm but not hot -- inviting, almost. The color of trust. The color of lies dressed up as truth.\n\n\"Mabel, DON'T,\" Dipper tries to say, but his sealed mouth only produces a muffled grunt.\n\n\"YOU KNOW WHAT, SHOOTING STAR? I'LL EVEN SWEETEN THE POT. I'LL GIVE YOUR FAMILY THEIR MEMORIES OF THIS SUMMER. ALL THE GOOD PARTS. THEY'LL REMEMBER YOU FONDLY. WON'T THAT BE NICE?\"\n\nHis hand hovers in the air between them. Blue fire crackles. The deal is right there, waiting to be sealed. One handshake. One agreement. Her family goes free.\n\nBut deals with Bill Cipher are NEVER what they seem. The words are true -- he'll keep every letter of the agreement. But the spirit? The intention? Bill is a master of the loophole, a connoisseur of the technically-correct. \"Leave Gravity Falls\" could mean a lot of things to a being who perceives reality in twelve dimensions. \"Forever\" is flexible when you control time.\n\nMabel looks at Bill's burning hand. She looks at Dipper, whose eyes are screaming warnings his mouth can't form. She looks at the petrified figures on the walls -- the people Bill has already trapped.\n\nHer hand reaches toward Bill's.\n\nDipper strains against his sealed lips. He can feel the words trying to escape: Don't do it. It's a trap. Please, Mabel, PLEASE.",
    choices: [
      { label: "Accept Bill's deal", target: "ending_fearamid_deal" },
      { label: "Refuse the deal", target: "fearamid_challenge" }
    ]
  },

  fearamid_zodiac: {
    text: "\"The Zodiac,\" Dipper says firmly. \"If there's a prophecy that can stop Bill, we have to try it. Ford, tell us everything.\"\n\nFord nods, a grim determination settling over his features. First, though, they need to get him out of this cell. Dipper examines the barrier -- it's a containment field powered by Bill's energy, but the runes maintaining it are etched into the floor around the cell. If they can disrupt the runes, the barrier should drop.\n\nMabel pulls out her grappling hook and, with a practiced swing, drags the hook across the floor, scratching through one of the critical symbols. The barrier flickers, stutters, and collapses with a sound like a television losing its signal. Ford stumbles forward, and both twins catch him.\n\n\"Thank you, kids.\" Ford steadies himself and immediately pulls out the crumpled paper from his coat. \"Now, listen carefully. The Zodiac -- the Cipher Wheel -- consists of ten symbols. Each one corresponds to a specific person whose unique energy signature resonates with that symbol. Pine Tree -- that's you, Dipper. Shooting Star -- Mabel. The six-fingered hand -- that's me. The crescent moon -- could be anyone, but I believe it's our friend Pacifica. The question mark -- Soos. The ice bag -- Wendy. The pentagram -- Gideon. The llama -- well, that's complicated. The fish symbol -- possibly McGucket. And the fez...\"\n\nFord trails off. His face falls.\n\n\"The fez,\" he repeats quietly. \"That's Stanley.\"\n\n\"Grunkle Stan!\" Mabel exclaims. \"Where IS he?\"\n\n\"Bill separated us when he captured us. Stan could be anywhere in the Fearamid. Or...\" Ford doesn't finish the sentence, but his expression says what his words won't.\n\nThey spend the next frantic minutes freeing who they can from the cells. Soos is three cells down from Ford, dazed but functional. Wendy is further along, bruised and furious and ready to fight. They find McGucket in a cell near the end of the corridor, having the time of his life because he's already forgotten where he is and has been keeping himself entertained by making a tiny banjo out of his shoelaces.\n\nBut Pacifica is encased in crystal, not just in a cell. Gideon is nowhere to be found. Stan is missing. And several of the other symbols remain mysteries.\n\n\"Ford,\" Dipper says, panic rising in his voice, \"we don't have everyone. We have you, me, Mabel, Soos, Wendy, and McGucket. That's six out of ten. Can we make it work with six?\"\n\nFord's face is grave. \"An incomplete Zodiac is not just ineffective, Dipper. It's DANGEROUS. The ritual generates enormous amounts of anti-Bill energy. With all ten present, that energy is perfectly balanced -- channeled, focused, directed. Without all ten...\" He pushes his cracked glasses up his nose. \"The energy has nowhere to go. It becomes wild. Unstable. Like a bomb without a casing.\"\n\n\"So it could explode,\" Mabel says flatly.\n\n\"It could do worse than explode. It could partially work -- weaken Bill enough to enrage him while simultaneously releasing uncontrolled mystical energy that could damage reality even further. The cure could be worse than the disease.\"\n\nDipper looks at their small, battered group. Soos, who is trying to comfort a crying gnome he freed from a nearby cell. Wendy, who has fashioned a weapon from a broken chain link and looks ready to take on every demon in the Fearamid. McGucket, who is playing his shoelace banjo and humming. Ford, brilliant and exhausted and scared.\n\n\"What if we try to improvise?\" Dipper asks. \"Substitute people for the missing symbols? Find others who might resonate with the right frequencies?\"\n\n\"It's possible,\" Ford admits. \"Theoretically. But the margin for error is virtually nonexistent. One wrong person in one wrong position, and the whole thing collapses -- violently.\"\n\nAbove them, the Fearamid shudders. Bill's laughter rolls through the structure like thunder. Whatever he's doing up in his throne room, he's having a good time. Which means the world is having a very bad time.\n\n\"We have to decide,\" Ford says. \"We can attempt the Zodiac with who we have and hope it's enough. Or...\" He pauses, his eyes flicking to the piece of paper in his coat. \"There's another option. A longer shot, perhaps, but one that doesn't require ten specific people standing in a circle.\"\n\n\"The Axolotl,\" Dipper says.\n\nFord nods slowly. \"The Axolotl.\"",
    choices: [
      { label: "Push through with the incomplete zodiac", target: "ending_fearamid_zodiac_fail" },
      { label: "Abandon the zodiac and search for the Axolotl instead", target: "axolotl_ritual" }
    ]
  },

  fearamid_axolotl_info: {
    text: "\"Tell me about the Axolotl,\" Dipper says, leaning close to the barrier. \"Tell me everything.\"\n\nFord's eyes light up -- not with the brightness of hope, exactly, but with the fervent glow of a scientist who has spent decades chasing an answer and finally has someone willing to listen. He settles into a cross-legged position on the floor of his cell, as if preparing to give a lecture -- which, knowing Ford, is exactly what he's about to do.\n\n\"When I first entered Bill's dimension thirty years ago,\" Ford begins, his voice low and measured, \"I thought I understood the structure of reality. I was wrong. I thought there were perhaps a dozen dimensions, layered on top of each other like pages in a book. The truth is so much stranger, Dipper. There are infinite dimensions -- infinite realities, each one as vast and complex as our own. And between them, in the spaces where dimensions overlap, in the cracks between worlds... there are beings.\"\n\nHe holds up six fingers. \"Not demons like Bill. Not ghosts. Not any of the supernatural creatures documented in my journals. I'm talking about something much, much older. Cosmic entities. Beings that existed before dimensions themselves were formed. They don't live IN reality -- they ARE aspects of reality. Gravity has a being. Light has a being. Time has a being. And the spaces between things -- the gaps, the transitions, the liminal zones -- those belong to the Axolotl.\"\n\n\"A salamander,\" Mabel says skeptically. \"The secret weapon against Bill Cipher is a salamander.\"\n\n\"Not just any salamander,\" Ford says, and there's something like reverence in his voice. \"The Axolotl is a cosmic entity that takes the form of a great pink salamander -- miles long, drifting through the void between dimensions. It is the guardian of transitions. Birth and death. Endings and beginnings. The space between one breath and the next. And most importantly...\" Ford leans forward, his cracked glasses catching the glow of the barrier. \"...the Axolotl governs the transition between existence and nonexistence. It is the one being in all of creation that can truly UNMAKE something. Not destroy -- destruction is easy. UNMAKE. Return something to the state before it existed. Erase it not just from the present, but from the past and future simultaneously.\"\n\nDipper feels a chill run down his spine that has nothing to do with the Fearamid's damp corridors. \"And Bill is afraid of it?\"\n\n\"Bill is TERRIFIED of it. I've seen him flinch at the mere mention of its name. The Axolotl is the one thing in the multiverse that could not just defeat Bill Cipher, but unmake him entirely -- remove him from reality as if he never existed. No memory, no legacy, no trace. Complete ontological erasure.\"\n\n\"Onto-what-now?\" Mabel asks.\n\n\"It means erased from BEING, Mabel. Erased from the very concept of existing.\"\n\nMabel considers this. \"So like when I accidentally delete a photo on my phone and it's not even in the trash anymore?\"\n\nFord blinks. \"...Essentially, yes.\"\n\n\"Okay, I get it. That IS scary.\"\n\nFord reaches into his coat and pulls out two items. The first is the crumpled paper they saw before -- covered in symbols, coordinates, and fragments of text. The second is something Dipper didn't notice until now: a small piece of ancient parchment, yellow with age, bearing a portion of a map drawn in an ink that shimmers between colors.\n\n\"I've been researching the Axolotl for decades,\" Ford says, pressing both items against the barrier. \"These are my findings. The paper contains a fragment of a summoning incantation -- not complete, but potentially enough to establish contact. The map shows a portion of the path to the Axolotl's dimension, a place I call the In-Between. It exists in the spaces between all other dimensions -- you can access it from anywhere, if you know how.\"\n\nDipper studies the materials through the barrier, committing what he can to memory. The incantation is in a language he doesn't recognize, but the phonetics are written out in Ford's careful handwriting. The map is more abstract -- not a map of physical space, but a map of dimensional relationships, showing how to navigate from one reality to the next to find the gaps between them.\n\n\"There's something else,\" Ford adds, his voice dropping even lower. \"The Axolotl doesn't act for free. It's not a vending machine. Every account I've found of people who successfully contacted the Axolotl describes the same thing: it asks for something in return. A price. Not gold or treasure -- the Axolotl has no use for material things. It asks for something personal. Something meaningful. A memory. A relationship. A piece of who you are.\"\n\n\"That sounds... not great,\" Mabel says.\n\n\"It's not,\" Ford agrees. \"But consider the alternative. Bill Cipher, unchallenged, ruling our dimension for eternity. The Axolotl's price might be high, but Bill's price is everything.\"\n\nThe cell hums with energy. Above them, the Fearamid groans as Bill does something particularly catastrophic to the world outside. Time is running out -- for the town, for the dimension, for everything.",
    choices: [
      { label: "Use Ford's information to search for the Axolotl", target: "axolotl_clue" },
      { label: "Stay and try to defeat Bill here and now", target: "fearamid_zodiac" }
    ]
  },

  fearamid_timebubble_bill: {
    text: "Dipper's hand goes to the supply bag on his back. He can feel the time bubble vessel humming through the fabric -- that captured piece of broken time, the hummingbird still looping its endless wingbeat inside the glass. It's been waiting for this moment. Or maybe Dipper has been waiting for this moment. Either way, this is it.\n\n\"MABEL, KEEP HIM BUSY!\" Dipper shouts, pulling the containment vessel from the bag. It glows in his hands, the symbols on its surface spinning with blue-white light. Inside, the tiny time bubble pulses like a heartbeat.\n\nMabel doesn't need to be told twice. She launches herself at Bill with the reckless abandon of someone who has absolutely nothing left to lose, swinging her grappling hook in wide arcs. Bill dodges easily -- laughing, always laughing -- but Mabel's attack serves its purpose. Bill's attention is on her. His eye tracks the grappling hook as it whistles past his bow tie.\n\n\"OH, SHOOTING STAR, YOU'RE A RIOT! A REAL SPITFIRE! GET IT? BECAUSE PYRONICA IS LITERALLY MADE OF--\"\n\nDipper unscrews the cap of the containment vessel.\n\nThe time bubble surges upward the moment the seal is broken, expanding from grapefruit-sized to basketball-sized in an instant. The air around it shimmers and distorts. The hummingbird inside is gone -- released from its loop, the little bird vanishes into the Fearamid's impossible corridors. What remains is pure temporal energy, a sphere of concentrated time-loop, hungry for something to catch.\n\nDipper hurls it at Bill.\n\nThe throw is perfect -- arcing through the air, trailing iridescent light, heading directly for the center of Bill's body. For one glorious, heart-stopping moment, it seems like the most brilliant plan Dipper Pines has ever conceived.\n\nThe time bubble hits Bill dead center. It engulfs him entirely, and for a fraction of a second, the throne room witnesses something unprecedented: Bill Cipher, frozen. Caught in a loop. His eye wide with surprise, his arm half-raised, his body flickering as time tries to trap him in a single repeating moment.\n\nThe Henchmaniacs gasp. Mabel cheers. Dipper pumps his fist.\n\nThen Bill's eye narrows.\n\n\"CUTE,\" he says from inside the time bubble, and his voice should be looping but it ISN'T. He's speaking normally. He's MOVING normally. He reaches out with one hand and grabs the edge of the time bubble like it's a soap bubble, holding it between his fingers.\n\n\"REALLY CUTE, PINE TREE. YOU TRIED TO TRAP A BEING OF PURE CHAOS IN A TIME LOOP. THERE'S JUST ONE TEENSY LITTLE PROBLEM WITH THAT PLAN.\"\n\nHe opens his mouth -- or rather, the bottom of his triangular body splits open to reveal a void of absolute darkness, deeper than space, colder than nothing.\n\n\"I EAT CHAOS FOR BREAKFAST.\"\n\nBill swallows the time bubble.\n\nThe effect is instantaneous and catastrophic. Bill's body convulses, then EXPANDS, golden light pouring from every edge. He absorbs the temporal energy the way a fire absorbs gasoline. The time that was supposed to trap him instead feeds him, adds to his power, gives him control over yet another fundamental force of reality.\n\nBill grows. Three feet becomes six. Six becomes twelve. Twelve becomes thirty, forty, fifty. He fills the throne room, his eye blazing with a light so intense that Dipper and Mabel have to shield their faces. The Henchmaniacs scatter, pressing themselves against the walls.\n\n\"OH WOW,\" Bill says, his voice shaking the Fearamid to its foundations. \"OH WOWWWW. IS THIS WHAT TIME TASTES LIKE? THIS IS AMAZING! I CAN SEE EVERY MOMENT THAT EVER WAS OR WILL BE! I CAN SEE THE BEGINNING OF THE UNIVERSE AND THE END AND THE BORING PARTS IN THE MIDDLE! THANKS FOR THE SNACK, PINE TREE!\"\n\nDipper's blood turns to ice. He can feel it -- the wrongness radiating from Bill. The triangle demon hasn't just absorbed the time bubble. He's absorbed time ITSELF, or at least a piece of it. He can control temporal loops now. He can trap anyone, anywhere, in any moment, forever.\n\nBill turns his enormous eye to the twins, and when he speaks, his voice is almost gentle. Almost kind. Which makes it infinitely worse.\n\n\"YOU KNOW WHAT? I WAS GOING TO TURN YOU INTO FURNITURE LIKE EVERYONE ELSE. BUT THAT GIFT DESERVES A SPECIAL REWARD. HOW ABOUT THIS -- I'LL LET YOU RELIVE YOUR WORST DAY. OVER AND OVER. FOREVER. YOU'LL REMEMBER EVERY LOOP. YOU'LL FEEL EVERY SECOND. BUT YOU'LL NEVER BE ABLE TO CHANGE A SINGLE THING. ISN'T THAT POETIC?\"\n\nHe snaps his fingers. Blue-gold fire engulfs the twins. Reality folds. And then they're back at the beginning of the worst day of their lives -- the day they found out Grunkle Stan's secret, the day they thought they'd lost each other, the day everything fell apart. They live through it. Every terrible moment. Every ounce of pain and confusion and betrayal.\n\nThen it starts over.\n\nAnd over. And over. And over. Each time, they remember the previous loop. Each time, they know what's coming but can't change it. Bill's laughter follows them through every iteration, a constant companion in their eternal prison.\n\nSomewhere outside the loop, Bill sits on his throne, larger and more powerful than ever, occasionally glancing at the two shimmering temporal bubbles that contain the Pines twins. He uses them as nightlights. They give off a lovely glow.",
    ending: true
  },

  fearamid_riddle: {
    text: "\"Hey Bill!\" Dipper shouts, and there's something in his voice that makes Bill pause mid-reality-warp. Not fear. Not bravery. Something sharper. Something that sounds almost like... confidence. \"I bet you can't answer a riddle.\"\n\nThe room goes quiet. Bill's eye narrows to a pinprick, then widens to fill most of his face. His body language shifts from playful to intensely focused, like a predator that just spotted something interesting.\n\n\"A RIDDLE?\" Bill's voice is dangerously soft. \"YOU'RE CHALLENGING MY INTELLIGENCE? ME? THE BEING WHO HAS EXISTED FOR TRILLIONS OF YEARS? WHO HAS SEEN THE BIRTH AND DEATH OF GALAXIES? WHO KNOWS THE SECRET OF EVERY MYSTERY IN THE UNIVERSE?\"\n\n\"Yeah,\" Dipper says, keeping his voice steady. \"One riddle. If you can answer it, we surrender. No fight. We'll be your decorations or your hat rack or whatever. But if you CAN'T answer it...\" He lets the implication hang.\n\nBill floats closer. His eye is doing that thing where it spirals slightly, the pupil rotating like a loading icon. Dipper read about this in the journal -- it's a tell. Bill is intrigued. And Bill cannot, absolutely CANNOT, resist a challenge to his ego. It's his fundamental weakness, the one flaw in his otherwise incomprehensible mind. Tell Bill Cipher he can't do something, and he'll move heaven and earth (sometimes literally) to prove you wrong.\n\n\"DEAL,\" Bill says instantly. No blue fire this time -- this isn't a formal deal, just a bet. Bill's word against Dipper's. \"HIT ME WITH YOUR BEST SHOT, PINE TREE.\"\n\nDipper takes a deep breath. He's been thinking about this since the fight started -- since he noticed Bill's weakness for ego challenges. The riddle has to be something that can't be answered with raw knowledge. Something that targets Bill's blind spot. Something that asks about the one thing an all-knowing, all-seeing demon might not understand.\n\n\"Here's the riddle,\" Dipper says. \"I am something you have never experienced. I cannot be calculated or predicted. The more you know, the less you understand me. I am the opposite of everything you are. What am I?\"\n\nBill freezes. His eye stops spiraling. For one, two, three seconds, the most powerful being in the universe is absolutely still.\n\nThen he starts processing. His eye flickers through colors -- gold, red, blue, green, white -- as he cycles through trillions of potential answers. Dipper can almost hear the calculations happening, the vast cosmic intelligence rifling through every fact, every piece of knowledge, every secret it has ever absorbed.\n\n\"EASY,\" Bill says. \"THE ANSWER IS CHAOS. NO WAIT -- RANDOMNESS. NO, THAT'S NOT IT EITHER. HOLD ON.\" His eye narrows. \"DEATH? NO, I'VE SEEN PLENTY OF DEATH. LOVE? NO, I UNDERSTAND LOVE -- IT'S A BIOCHEMICAL RESPONSE IN MEAT CREATURES. HMMMM.\"\n\nDipper says nothing. He lets Bill think. Because the real answer -- the answer Dipper is banking on -- is something Bill literally cannot comprehend. The answer is HUMILITY. The experience of not knowing. The feeling of being small, of admitting weakness, of accepting that some things are beyond you. Bill has never, in his trillion years of existence, experienced a single moment of genuine humility. He can't even conceptualize it. It's his ultimate blind spot.\n\nBut the riddle isn't really the point. The point is the TIME it buys them. Bill is distracted, his vast intelligence churning through possible answers, his ego refusing to admit defeat. And while he's distracted, Mabel -- brilliant, observant, underestimated Mabel -- is looking at the throne room with different eyes.\n\nShe sees it.\n\nIn the wall behind Bill's throne, partially hidden by the shadow of the massive playing-card structure, there's a crack. Not a decorative crack or a damage crack, but a STRUCTURAL crack -- a line of flickering, iridescent light that runs from floor to ceiling, pulsing with an energy that doesn't match anything else in the Fearamid. It's not Bill's energy. It's not Weirdmageddon energy. It's something else entirely. Something older.\n\nMabel tugs Dipper's sleeve. He follows her gaze. He sees the crack. And his eyes widen because the light coming through it is pink -- a soft, living pink, the color of sunrise, the color of hope, the color of something that exists beyond Bill's reach.\n\n\"THE ANSWER IS-- WAIT, I'VE ALMOST GOT IT-- IT'S ON THE TIP OF MY-- DO I EVEN HAVE A TONGUE? RHETORICAL QUESTION, DON'T ANSWER THAT.\"\n\nBill is still puzzling over the riddle, but he won't be distracted forever. His attention is already starting to wander back to them. They have seconds, maybe, before he gives up on the riddle and gets angry instead.\n\nDipper looks at the crack. Mabel looks at the crack. They look at each other.",
    choices: [
      { label: "Exploit the crack in the Fearamid", target: "fearamid_crack" },
      { label: "Run while Bill is distracted", target: "ending_fearamid_chase" }
    ]
  },

  fearamid_crack: {
    text: "They move while Bill is still muttering to himself, his vast intelligence spiraling through increasingly desperate answers to the riddle. Dipper grabs Mabel's hand and they dash across the throne room, weaving between petrified figures and scattered party debris, heading straight for the crack in the wall behind the throne.\n\nUp close, the crack is extraordinary. It runs from the floor to the ceiling in a jagged line about two inches wide, and the light pouring through it is unlike anything they've seen. It's pink and gold and somehow WARM -- not physically warm, but emotionally warm, the way a memory of home feels warm, the way a hug from someone you love feels warm. The light carries a smell, too: petrichor -- the scent of rain on dry earth -- mixed with something floral and ancient.\n\nDipper presses his eye to the crack and nearly falls over from what he sees.\n\nBeyond the wall, hidden inside the deepest structural layer of the Fearamid, there's a chamber. And in that chamber, floating in empty space like an eye in a storm, is a dimensional rift. Not the violent, screaming rift that Bill tore open in the sky above Gravity Falls -- this is something gentler. More deliberate. It shimmers like a pool of liquid light, pink and gold, its edges perfectly circular, its surface rippling with patterns that look almost like... breathing.\n\nAnd through the rift, Dipper can see SOMETHING. A vast pink void. A place of incredible peace and silence. And moving within that void, so enormous that Dipper can only see a small portion of it through the rift, a shape. A curve of smooth pink skin. A feathery external gill, waving gently in currents that don't exist. An eye -- dark, ancient, impossibly kind -- that seems to look right back at him.\n\n\"Mabel,\" Dipper whispers. \"I think I know why Bill built the Fearamid HERE.\"\n\nIt clicks into place like the final piece of a puzzle. Bill didn't choose this location randomly. He built his fortress directly on top of a natural weak point between dimensions -- a place where the barrier between their world and the Axolotl's dimension is thinnest. He built the Fearamid AROUND this rift, not just to use its energy, but to BLOCK it. To keep it sealed. To make sure nothing could come through from the other side.\n\nBecause the Axolotl has been trying to reach them. This whole time, through all of Weirdmageddon, the one being in existence that Bill fears has been pushing against the barrier, trying to break through, trying to help. And Bill built an entire fortress to keep it out.\n\n\"He's AFRAID,\" Mabel breathes, understanding dawning on her face. \"Bill built this whole place because he's scared of what's on the other side of that crack.\"\n\n\"WHAT ARE YOU TWO DOING OVER THERE?\" Bill's voice suddenly booms across the throne room. He's abandoned the riddle and is staring at them with an eye that has gone from amused to alarmed in the span of a heartbeat. \"GET AWAY FROM THAT WALL. NOW. I MEAN IT.\"\n\nNo laughter. No jokes. No theatrics. Bill Cipher is SCARED, and it transforms him entirely. His golden body flickers with red light. The Henchmaniacs sense it and shrink back. The air in the throne room crackles with barely contained panic.\n\n\"HENCHMANIACS! STOP THEM! STOP THEM NOW!\"\n\nThe demons surge forward. 8-Ball charges across the room. Pyronica hurls bolts of fire. Teeth gnashes across the floor like a horrible bowling ball. But they're too late, and they're too slow, because Mabel has already fired her grappling hook into the crack.\n\nThe hook catches on something inside the wall. Mabel throws her weight against it, and the crack WIDENS. An inch. Two inches. A foot. The pink light intensifies, pouring through the gap like water through a breaking dam. The Fearamid shudders. Bill SCREAMS -- not his theatrical scream, but a real one, a sound of genuine terror that shakes the foundations of reality.\n\n\"NO! NO NO NO NO NO! YOU DON'T KNOW WHAT YOU'RE DOING! YOU DON'T KNOW WHAT'S IN THERE!\"\n\nDipper grabs the edge of the crack and pulls with everything he has. Mabel pulls beside him. The crack widens further. Through it, the dimensional rift beckons -- that pool of pink-gold light, that glimpse of the vast, peaceful void beyond. The Axolotl's dimension. The In-Between.\n\nThe crack is wide enough to squeeze through now. Beyond the wall, the rift awaits. They could jump through -- leave this dimension, enter the Axolotl's realm, seek the one being that can truly stop Bill. But they don't know what's on the other side. They don't know if they can come back. They don't know what the Axolotl will ask of them.\n\nOr they could try something else. The crack is destabilizing the Fearamid's structure. If they widen it further -- much further -- the whole fortress might come apart. The rift's energy could tear the Fearamid to pieces, destroying Bill's seat of power. But an uncontrolled collapse could be catastrophic, for them and for whatever's left of Gravity Falls.\n\nBill is bearing down on them, his body distorted with rage and fear, reality warping around him like a heat haze. The Henchmaniacs are closing in from all sides. They have seconds to choose.",
    choices: [
      { label: "Jump through the dimensional rift", target: "axolotl_dimension" },
      { label: "Try to widen the crack to destroy the Fearamid", target: "ending_fearamid_collapse" }
    ]
  },

  ending_fearamid_fall: {
    text: "\"We can take him!\" Dipper shouts, swinging one arm at Kryptos while clinging to the chain with the other. Mabel joins in, lashing out with her grappling hook. The wind screams around them. A hundred feet of empty air yawns below.\n\nKryptos darts backward, easily avoiding their attacks. He's flying. They're hanging from a chain. The math is not in their favor.\n\n\"GEOMETRIC SUPERIORITY CONFIRMED,\" Kryptos announces, and fires another beam at the chain -- not above them this time, but at the link Dipper is gripping. The metal turns white-hot under his fingers. Dipper yelps and shifts his grip, scrambling for the next link up, but the sudden movement throws off his balance.\n\nHis foot slips.\n\nFor one eternal second, Dipper is hanging by one hand, his fingers sliding over warm metal, his legs kicking in empty air. He can see everything -- the ruined town below, the Fearamid above, Mabel's face twisted in horror as she reaches for him. Kryptos hovering nearby, charging another shot. The wind pulling at his hat, his clothes, his grip.\n\n\"DIPPER!\" Mabel lunges for him, letting go of her own link to grab his wrist. She catches him. But the sudden weight is too much -- her fingers, already aching from the climb, can't hold both of them. They slip. One finger at a time. Pinky. Ring finger. Middle.\n\nThey fall.\n\nThe sensation of falling from a great height is not what you'd expect. It's not a rush. It's not exciting. It's a cold, hollow certainty that the ground is coming and there's absolutely nothing you can do about it. The wind steals Dipper's scream from his mouth. Mabel's hand finds his, and they grip each other as the world rushes upward.\n\nDipper closes his eyes. This is it. This is how it ends. Not with a clever plan or a dramatic sacrifice, but with a fall. A stupid, pointless fall because they tried to fight a flying demon while hanging from a chain and that was never going to work and he should have known better and--\n\nThey stop.\n\nNot slowly, like a parachute. INSTANTLY. One moment they're falling at terminal velocity, the next they're hanging in midair, completely motionless, as if the universe pressed pause. Dipper's stomach lurches violently. Mabel makes a sound that's half-scream and half-hiccup.\n\nA golden light engulfs them. A familiar shape materializes around them -- a triangle, an eye, a bow tie.\n\n\"WHOA THERE, KIDDOS!\" Bill Cipher's voice is cheerful, almost parental. \"CAN'T HAVE MY FAVORITE TOYS BREAKING BEFORE I'M DONE PLAYING WITH THEM!\"\n\nHe didn't save them out of kindness. There's no kindness in Bill Cipher. He saved them because they have VALUE -- entertainment value, trophy value, the value of conquered enemies displayed like medals on a general's chest.\n\nBill carries them back to the Fearamid, floating through the walls as if the stone is made of smoke. He deposits them in the throne room, where the Henchmaniacs gather around to look at the new acquisitions. Then Bill raises his hand, and crystal begins to grow around the twins -- clear, hard, beautiful crystal that climbs their legs, their torsos, their arms.\n\n\"PERFECT PRESERVATION,\" Bill explains to his demons, as if giving a museum tour. \"THEY'LL STAY EXACTLY LIKE THIS FOREVER. FULLY CONSCIOUS, FULLY AWARE, BUT COMPLETELY IMMOBILE. ISN'T SCIENCE WONDERFUL?\"\n\nThe crystal reaches Dipper's chin. He can still see, still hear, still think. He watches Mabel beside him, encased to her shoulders, her eyes wide and terrified and alive. They'll be alive in these crystals. That's the cruel part. They'll be alive and aware and unable to move, speak, or do anything but watch as Bill's eternal party rages around them.\n\nThe crystal closes over Dipper's head. The sound cuts out. The world becomes a slightly distorted view through translucent crystal, like looking through a window in the rain. He can see the throne room. He can see the Henchmaniacs returning to their revelry. He can see Bill floating back to his throne, shrinking the twins' crystal prisons to a manageable size and placing them on a shelf.\n\nBetween Dipper's crystal and Mabel's, there are about six inches of empty space. Close enough to see each other. Too far to touch. They stare at each other through the crystal, and Dipper tries to communicate everything through his eyes -- I'm sorry. I love you. I tried.\n\nMabel blinks back. He thinks she understands.\n\nBill uses them as conversation starters at his parties. \"OH THOSE? THOSE ARE THE PINES TWINS. THEY TRIED TO FIGHT ME. ADORABLE, RIGHT?\" The demons laugh every time. The twins don't laugh. The twins don't do anything. They just watch, and wait, and hope for a rescue that may never come.\n\nThe worst part is that hope never quite dies. It would be easier if it did.",
    ending: true
  },

  ending_fearamid_deal: {
    text: "Mabel takes Bill's hand.\n\nThe deal-fire erupts between their palms -- brilliant blue, burning cold, the light of a contract sealed in supernatural law. It races up Mabel's arm and down Bill's, binding them in a web of blue light that pulses once, twice, three times, and then sinks into their skin. The deal is sealed. Unbreakable. Eternal.\n\nDipper's mouth unseals and his first word is \"NO!\" but it's too late. The fire has already burned. The contract is written in the fabric of reality itself.\n\n\"PLEASURE DOING BUSINESS WITH YOU, SHOOTING STAR,\" Bill says, and his eye is curved in the most satisfied expression Dipper has ever seen on anyone, human or demon. He snaps his fingers.\n\nThings happen quickly after that. In the cells below, Ford's barrier drops. Soos stumbles out of his cell, blinking. Wendy kicks open her door (it was already open, but she kicks it anyway). Throughout the Fearamid, every captured human is released -- the crystal around Pacifica shatters, the stone prisons crack open, the time bubbles pop. People stumble out into the corridors, confused and afraid but FREE.\n\nBill is keeping his word. Technically. Precisely. Every letter of the deal fulfilled.\n\nGrunkle Stan materializes in front of them, blinking and disoriented. \"Kids? What happened? Where are we? Why is everything triangles?\"\n\n\"Stan!\" Both twins run to hug him. He hugs back, tight and fierce, and for a moment everything feels like it might be okay.\n\nThen Bill coughs politely.\n\n\"AHEM. NOT TO BREAK UP THE HALLMARK MOMENT, BUT WE HAD A DEAL, SHOOTING STAR. TIME TO PAY UP.\"\n\n\"Mabel, what deal?\" Stan demands, looking between his niece and the floating triangle. \"What did you do?\"\n\n\"I...\" Mabel's voice cracks. \"I saved you. I saved everyone.\"\n\n\"AT THE VERY REASONABLE PRICE OF LEAVING GRAVITY FALLS FOREVER!\" Bill interjects cheerfully. \"HONESTLY, I'M PRACTICALLY A PHILANTHROPIST. SOMEONE SHOULD GIVE ME AN AWARD.\"\n\nStan's face goes white. Ford, who has just arrived in the throne room, goes even whiter. \"Mabel, no. Tell me you didn't.\"\n\n\"I had to,\" Mabel whispers. \"He was going to--\"\n\n\"TICK TOCK!\" Bill interrupts. \"THE DEAL-FIRE WAITS FOR NO ONE. TIME TO GO, KIDS.\"\n\nHe snaps his fingers, and a portal opens behind the twins. But it's not a portal back to the normal world. It's a portal to ELSEWHERE -- a dimension of grey. No color. No features. No horizon. Just an infinite, flat, grey expanse stretching in all directions under a sky that's the same shade of nothing.\n\nDipper and Mabel feel the deal-fire pulling them toward it. Their feet slide on the Fearamid floor. Stan grabs Dipper's arm. Ford grabs Mabel's. They pull. But you can't fight a deal sealed in supernatural contract fire. It's like trying to hold back the tide.\n\n\"KIDS, FIGHT IT!\" Stan yells, digging his heels in. \"DON'T LET GO!\"\n\n\"We can't--\" Dipper's fingers slip from Stan's grip. Mabel's hand slides from Ford's. The deal-fire is too strong, the contract too binding. They were promised to this exile, and reality itself is enforcing the terms.\n\nThe twins stumble backward through the portal. The grey dimension swallows them. The last thing Dipper sees is Stan's face -- not angry, not tough, not the con-man mask he always wears, but broken. Genuinely, completely broken. Stan reaches through the closing portal, his hand grasping at air.\n\n\"KIDS! KIDS, I'LL FIND YOU! I SWEAR I'LL FIND YOU!\"\n\nThe portal snaps shut. Bill winks -- his entire body tilting to one side in a jaunty, self-satisfied blink. \"WELL! THAT WAS FUN. NOW, WHERE WERE WE? OH RIGHT -- ETERNAL CHAOS! EIGHT-BALL, CRANK UP THE MUSIC!\"\n\nIn the grey dimension, Dipper and Mabel stand in nothing. There's a ground beneath them, but it's the same grey as everything else. There's air, but it tastes like nothing. There's light, but it comes from nowhere and casts no shadows. It's not painful. It's not cold. It's just... empty. A dimension of absolute nothing.\n\nMabel sits down on the grey ground. Dipper sits beside her.\n\n\"He'll find us,\" Mabel says. \"Grunkle Stan said he'd find us.\"\n\n\"Yeah,\" Dipper says. \"He will.\"\n\nThey wait. They wait for a very, very long time. Somewhere in another dimension, their family searches for them -- following leads, chasing rumors, never giving up. Stan spends every dollar he has. Ford builds device after device. Soos prays to every entity he can think of. Wendy punches things in frustration.\n\nBut the grey dimension has no doors. No exits. No seams. Bill chose it specifically because it's the one place in the multiverse that can't be reached from the outside. The twins are alive. They're together. But they're nowhere, and nowhere is forever.",
    ending: true
  },

  ending_fearamid_zodiac_fail: {
    text: "\"We have to try,\" Dipper says, clenching his fists. \"Six people is better than zero. Maybe the Zodiac will work at sixty percent power. Maybe it'll be enough to weaken Bill.\"\n\nFord hesitates, his scientific mind warring with his desperation. Then he nods, slowly, the way a man nods when he knows he's about to do something dangerous and possibly foolish but can't think of anything better.\n\n\"Everyone, form a circle,\" Ford commands. \"Stand on the symbols I indicate. Do NOT break the circle once we begin, no matter what happens.\"\n\nThey clear a space in the lower level of the Fearamid. Ford uses a piece of chalk to draw the Zodiac on the dark stone floor -- the ten-symbol circle, with Bill's image at the center. The symbols glow faintly as he draws them, as if the floor recognizes what they are and responds.\n\nDipper takes his position on the Pine Tree symbol. Mabel stands on the Shooting Star. Ford on the Six-Fingered Hand. Soos, nervously adjusting his hat, stands on the Question Mark. Wendy, jaw set and eyes hard, takes the Ice Bag. McGucket, still plucking his shoelace banjo, wanders onto the Fish symbol because Ford gently steers him there.\n\nFour symbols remain empty. The Fez. The Crescent. The Llama. The Pentagram. Four holes in the circle. Four missing pieces.\n\n\"Join hands,\" Ford says. \"And whatever you do -- DON'T LET GO.\"\n\nThey reach out. Hands clasp. The circle is formed -- incomplete, gap-toothed, but formed. Ford begins to chant. The words are in a language that predates human civilization, syllables that seem to reshape the air as they're spoken. The symbols on the floor glow brighter. The chalk lines pulse with white light.\n\nSomething happens.\n\nThe floor shakes. The walls of the Fearamid tremble. Above them, they can hear Bill's party stutter and stop. Then Bill's voice, sharp with alarm: \"WHAT IS THAT? WHAT ARE THEY DOING DOWN THERE?\"\n\nThe Zodiac is working. Even incomplete, the ritual is generating anti-Bill energy -- a frequency that disrupts his power, interferes with his control, threatens the very foundation of Weirdmageddon. The six occupied symbols blaze with light. The four empty ones flicker weakly, trying to activate without anyone standing on them.\n\nBill SCREAMS. Not from pain, but from rage. \"STOP THEM! STOP THEM NOW!\"\n\nBut the Zodiac energy creates a barrier that even the Henchmaniacs can't penetrate. Demons crash against it and bounce off. Bill himself slams into it from above, his massive form pressing against an invisible wall. The ritual is holding. It's working. It's WORKING.\n\nThen the empty symbols go dark.\n\nThe energy that was flowing through the circle hits the gaps and has nowhere to go. It's like water in a pipe hitting a dead end -- the pressure builds, and builds, and builds. Ford's face goes white. He realizes what's happening a second before it occurs.\n\n\"BREAK THE CIRCLE! EVERYONE BREAK THE--\"\n\nToo late.\n\nThe incomplete Zodiac releases its energy all at once, in every direction. A shockwave of raw mystical force explodes outward from the circle -- not the focused beam that a complete Zodiac would have produced, but a wild, uncontrolled detonation. The energy is both anti-Bill AND anti-everything-else. It doesn't discriminate.\n\nThe blast hits Bill first. He shrieks as the energy tears at his form, diminishing him, stripping away layers of power like paint being sandblasted off a wall. He shrinks from his massive size to something smaller, weaker, flickering. The Zodiac energy IS hurting him. The prophecy IS partially fulfilled.\n\nBut the blast doesn't stop with Bill. It tears through the Fearamid, shattering walls, cracking floors, ripping apart the impossible architecture. The lower levels collapse. The upper levels tilt. The entire fortress groans like a dying animal.\n\nAnd then the energy hits the circle's occupants.\n\nDipper feels it first -- a cold, heavy sensation starting at his feet, like the golden freezing from Bill's power but different. Not gold. Stone. The wild Zodiac energy, unable to find its proper channel, is converting everything nearby into its most stable form. And the most stable form, apparently, is rock.\n\nHe watches his hands turn grey. He watches Mabel, across the circle, her mouth open in a shout that petrifies before the sound can escape. He watches Ford, McGucket, Soos, Wendy -- all of them caught in the same wave, their bodies turning to stone in the positions they held when the blast hit. A circle of statues, hands still joined, faces frozen in expressions of shock and realization.\n\nDipper's last thought before the stone claims his mind is: We were so close. Four more people. Four more people and it would have worked.\n\nThe Fearamid collapses around them, but the stone circle holds. When the dust settles, six statues stand in the ruins of Bill's fortress, perfectly preserved, hands linked in an incomplete ring. Above them, Bill -- weakened, diminished, but alive -- surveys the wreckage of his home and the stone monuments to the people who almost stopped him.\n\n\"ALMOST,\" Bill mutters, his voice smaller than before, less confident, touched with something that might be the ghost of fear. \"ALMOST ISN'T GOOD ENOUGH.\"\n\nHe floats away to rebuild. The stone circle remains. And in Gravity Falls, people sometimes visit the strange statues in the ruins, and wonder what they were trying to do, and whether they came close. They came so, so close.",
    ending: true
  },

  ending_fearamid_chase: {
    text: "\"RUN!\" Dipper grabs Mabel's hand, and they bolt for the nearest exit -- a doorway on the far side of the throne room, an arched opening that leads to one of the Fearamid's many corridors. Bill is behind them, his riddle-solving distraction shattered, his patience gone.\n\n\"OH, ARE WE PLAYING TAG NOW?\" Bill's voice echoes through the corridors as they sprint. \"I LOVE TAG! I'M ALWAYS 'IT' THOUGH. BECAUSE, YOU KNOW, OMNIPOTENT BEING. HARD TO NOT BE 'IT' WHEN YOU CAN SEE THROUGH WALLS.\"\n\nThey run. Down corridors that twist and turn, through rooms that open onto other rooms, up staircases that lead sideways. The Fearamid's non-Euclidean architecture is disorienting under the best of circumstances, and these are spectacularly not the best of circumstances. Mabel takes a left. Dipper takes a right. They end up in the same hallway. They try going up. They end up going down. Every direction is wrong. Every turn leads deeper into the labyrinth.\n\n\"This way!\" Dipper shouts, spotting a corridor that looks different -- wider, with a faint light at the end. They sprint toward it. The light grows. For a beautiful, hopeful moment, Dipper thinks they've found an exit. A way out. A window, a gap, anything.\n\nThe corridor opens into... the throne room.\n\nThey're back where they started. Bill is sitting on his throne, exactly where they left him, watching them with an expression of infinite amusement. The Henchmaniacs line the walls, pointing and laughing.\n\n\"SURPRISE!\" Bill waves his little arms. \"DID I MENTION I REARRANGED THE FLOOR PLAN? EVERY EXIT LEADS BACK TO ME. BECAUSE THIS IS MY HOUSE AND I MAKE THE RULES AND THE RULES ARE THAT THERE ARE NO RULES EXCEPT THE ONE WHERE EVERYTHING LEADS BACK TO ME.\"\n\nThey turn and run again. A different corridor this time. They run harder, faster, their lungs burning, their legs aching. They pass landmarks they've never seen -- a room full of floating clocks, a hallway made entirely of teeth, a staircase that goes in a perfect circle. They run for what feels like minutes. Then hours. Then longer.\n\nThe corridor opens into the throne room.\n\nBill waves. \"WELCOME BACK! MISS ME?\"\n\nAgain. They run again. Through new corridors, old corridors, corridors that change as they're running through them. The walls shift. The floors tilt. Doors open and close on their own. Every path, every turn, every desperate sprint ends the same way.\n\nThe throne room. Bill. A wave. Laughter.\n\nThey run until their legs give out. They crawl until their arms give out. They lie on the cold stone floor of a corridor that they know, with absolute certainty, leads back to Bill's throne room, and they don't have the strength to move anymore.\n\n\"Dipper,\" Mabel pants, her cheek pressed against the floor. \"I think we're stuck.\"\n\n\"Yeah.\" Dipper stares at the ceiling, which is probably a floor or a wall depending on which direction reality feels like going today. \"I think we are.\"\n\nBill appears between them, small and casual, hovering at their eye level. His voice is almost gentle, the way a cat's purr is gentle when the cat is sitting on the mouse it just caught.\n\n\"HERE'S THE THING ABOUT MY HOUSE, KIDS. IT'S NOT A BUILDING. IT'S AN IDEA. AND YOU CAN'T RUN FROM AN IDEA. THE CORRIDORS DON'T HAVE A BEGINNING OR AN END -- THEY'RE A LOOP. A PERFECT, BEAUTIFUL LOOP. YOU'LL RUN THROUGH THEM FOREVER, AND EVERY TIME YOU THINK YOU'VE FOUND A WAY OUT, YOU'LL END UP RIGHT BACK HERE, LOOKING AT MY GORGEOUS TRIANGULAR FACE.\"\n\nHe tips his top hat.\n\n\"ENJOY YOUR EXERCISE, PINE TREE. SHOOTING STAR. I'LL CHECK IN ON YOU EVERY FEW DECADES.\"\n\nBill vanishes. The corridor stretches out before them, infinite and looping, lit by a faint golden glow that never changes. After a while, Dipper and Mabel get up. They walk, because what else is there to do? They hold hands, because that's the only thing that still makes sense.\n\nThey walk through corridors that loop. They find the throne room, empty now, and walk through it and out the other side and through more corridors that lead back to the throne room. Over and over. A loop without end.\n\nThey talk to pass the time. They tell each other stories. They play games -- I Spy is hard when everything looks the same, but they manage. They argue sometimes. They make up. They sleep in corridors and wake up in different corridors that look exactly the same.\n\nTime doesn't pass in the Fearamid. Or maybe it passes differently. Or maybe it loops, like everything else. They don't age. They don't get hungry (Bill, in a rare act of something that isn't quite mercy, has removed the need). They just walk, and talk, and loop, and walk.\n\nForever is a corridor. Forever is a turn that leads to the same room. Forever is your sister's hand in yours and the sound of your footsteps echoing back to you from all directions.",
    ending: true
  },

  ending_fearamid_collapse: {
    text: "\"We can bring this whole thing DOWN!\" Dipper shouts, grabbing the edge of the crack with both hands and pulling with all his strength. Mabel joins him, wedging her grappling hook deeper into the gap and using it as a lever. The crack widens -- six inches, a foot, two feet. The pink light blazes through, and with it comes a sound: a low, resonant hum that vibrates in their bones, in their teeth, in the spaces between their thoughts.\n\nThe Fearamid shudders.\n\nBill's reaction is instantaneous and volcanic. \"NO! YOU STUPID, STUPID CHILDREN! YOU DON'T UNDERSTAND WHAT YOU'RE DOING! THAT RIFT IS HOLDING THE DIMENSIONAL BARRIER TOGETHER! IF YOU TEAR IT OPEN--\"\n\n\"THAT'S THE IDEA!\" Mabel yells back, yanking the grappling hook sideways. The crack spreads like a fracture in ice, branching out in jagged lines across the wall, each new crack leaking that warm pink light.\n\nBut Bill is right. They don't understand what they're doing.\n\nThe crack was a controlled breach -- a thin, stable connection between dimensions, maintained in careful equilibrium. What Dipper and Mabel are doing is tearing that equilibrium apart. It's the difference between opening a window and knocking out a wall. One lets in a breeze. The other brings down the house.\n\nThe cracks spread across the floor, up the ceiling, through the walls. The Fearamid's dark stone splinters and fractures. The pink light fills every gap, every seam, every space between the bricks. The throne room begins to shake -- not the gentle tremor of before, but a violent, bone-rattling convulsion. Bill's playing-card throne collapses. The petrified figures along the walls topple and shatter. The Henchmaniacs scream and scatter.\n\nBill tries to seal the cracks. He presses his hands against the wall, pouring golden energy into the fractures, trying to weld reality back together. For a moment it works -- the cracks slow their spread, the shaking lessens. But there's too much damage. The rift has been destabilized beyond repair. Bill is trying to hold back an ocean with his hands.\n\nThe wall explodes.\n\nThe dimensional rift -- no longer a gentle pool of light but a HOWLING VORTEX of pink and gold energy -- tears open fully. It's not a doorway anymore. It's a wound. A gaping hole in the fabric of reality, and it's pulling everything toward it with the irresistible gravity of a black hole.\n\nFurniture flies across the room and vanishes into the vortex. Debris. Rubble. The remains of Bill's throne. Chunks of the Fearamid's walls tear free and spiral into the pink void. The Henchmaniacs grab onto whatever they can, but one by one, they lose their grip -- Kryptos goes first, spinning into the rift with a geometric scream. Then Keyhole. Then Teeth, gnashing futilely at the wind.\n\nBill fights it. Of course he fights it. He plants himself in the air and pushes back against the pull with every ounce of his immense power. His golden body blazes like a sun. \"I WILL NOT-- I AM THE MASTER OF-- THIS IS MY--\"\n\nThe Fearamid breaks.\n\nNot cracks. Not fractures. The entire fortress -- the massive inverted pyramid that has loomed over Gravity Falls since Weirdmageddon began -- breaks apart like a sandcastle hit by a wave. Huge chunks of obsidian stone rip free and tumble into the vortex. The chain that anchored it to the ground snaps with a sound like the world's largest guitar string. The Fearamid's peak crumbles. Its base shatters. The whole impossible structure, Bill Cipher's throne and fortress and monument to his own ego, comes apart in seconds.\n\nDipper and Mabel are caught in the pull. There's nothing to hold onto -- everything is moving, everything is falling, everything is being sucked into the screaming pink void between dimensions. Dipper reaches for Mabel. Their fingers touch. Brush against each other. Almost clasp.\n\nThe vortex pulls them apart.\n\nDipper goes one direction. Mabel goes another. Fragments of the Fearamid tumble between them. Bill's screaming voice echoes from somewhere, everywhere, nowhere. The pink light is blinding, overwhelming, all-consuming.\n\nDipper feels himself passing through the rift -- not into one dimension, but into ALL of them simultaneously. The vortex is uncontrolled, undialed, connected to everywhere and nowhere. He's scattered. Not destroyed -- scattered. Pieces of him flung across infinite dimensions like seeds thrown to the wind.\n\nIn one dimension, a boy in a pine tree hat wakes up in a forest he doesn't recognize, with no memory of how he got there and a sister's name on his lips that he can't quite remember.\n\nIn another dimension, a girl in a torn sweater opens her eyes to a sky full of unfamiliar stars, reaching for a hand that isn't there.\n\nIn a hundred other dimensions, fragments of the Fearamid rain down like meteorites, carrying pieces of Bill's power, pieces of Gravity Falls, pieces of a story that was supposed to end differently.\n\nThey're alive. Both of them. Somewhere in the infinite multiverse, Dipper Pines and Mabel Pines are alive. But they're separated by dimensions -- not miles, not oceans, but the fundamental walls between realities. Finding each other would require crossing the space between worlds, and neither of them knows where the other is, or even that the other survived.\n\nIn what's left of Gravity Falls, a crater sits where the town square used to be. The Fearamid is gone. Bill is gone -- diminished, scattered, reduced to fragments of chaotic energy dispersed across a thousand dimensions. Weirdmageddon is over. The sky is healing. But the cost is the two kids who tried to break a fortress and broke reality instead.\n\nStan stands at the edge of the crater. Ford stands beside him. Neither speaks. They stare at the empty sky where the Fearamid used to be, where the rift used to be, where their niece and nephew used to be.\n\n\"We'll find them,\" Stan says.\n\n\"They could be anywhere,\" Ford replies. \"Literally anywhere in the multiverse.\"\n\n\"Then we'll search the whole multiverse.\"\n\nFord looks at his brother. Stan looks back. And despite everything -- despite the impossible odds, despite the shattered sky, despite the vastness of infinity -- Ford nods.\n\n\"Then we'd better get started.\"",
    ending: true
  },

  axolotl_search: {
    text: "The world is ending, and Dipper Pines is reading a book.\n\nTo be fair, it's not just any book. It's Journal 3, and right now it might be the most important book in the universe. Dipper sits cross-legged on the floor of the Mystery Shack's attic, the journal open across his knees, while outside the windows the sky bleeds red and fractured and wrong. The Fearamid looms on the horizon like a mountain that shouldn't exist. Eye-bats shriek in the distance. Time itself hiccups - for a moment the clock on the wall runs backward, then sideways, then gives up entirely and starts spinning its hands in lazy circles.\n\nMabel sits beside him, Waddles pressed tight against her side. She's knitting furiously - a stress response, Dipper knows. The half-finished sweater in her lap already has a shooting star, a pine tree, and what appears to be an axolotl stitched into it, though Mabel claims that last one was supposed to be a dog.\n\n\"Here,\" Dipper says, jabbing his finger at a page near the back of the journal. The page is covered in Ford's cramped handwriting, but there's something else too - a watermark visible only under blacklight, and symbols that shift if you look at them from different angles. \"Great Uncle Ford wrote about something called the Axolotl. Capital A. He circled it like fifteen times and drew exclamation points around it.\"\n\n\"An axolotl is a type of salamander,\" Mabel says helpfully. \"They're pink and smiley and they look like they're always having a good time. I tried to get one at a pet store once but Mom said Waddles was enough responsibility.\"\n\n\"This isn't a regular axolotl, Mabel. Listen to this.\" Dipper reads aloud, his voice dropping low even though there's no one to overhear them. \"'A-X-O-L-O-T-L, my time has come to burn. I invoke the ancient power that I may return.' Ford found this phrase encoded in three different ancient texts. It's connected to Bill Cipher's origin story. The Axolotl is some kind of cosmic entity - something that exists outside normal reality, outside even the Nightmare Realm. And here's the important part -\" Dipper looks up, his eyes wide. \"Bill is afraid of it.\"\n\nMabel stops knitting. In the entire time they've known about Bill Cipher, through every terrifying encounter, every nightmare and manipulation and betrayal, they have never once seen Bill afraid of anything. Bill Cipher treats the entire multiverse like his personal comedy show. The idea that something out there makes him nervous is both thrilling and deeply unsettling.\n\n\"Ford writes that the Axolotl has power over time, reality, and existence itself,\" Dipper continues, flipping pages. \"It's the one entity that could theoretically undo what Bill has done. Undo Weirdmageddon entirely. But reaching it... Ford says it exists in a space 'between dimensions' - not in any one reality but in the gaps between all of them.\"\n\nOutside, something enormous crashes in the distance. The Shack trembles. Dust rains from the ceiling. Waddles squeals and burrows under Mabel's sweater pile.\n\n\"So we need to find a cosmic super-salamander that lives in the space between realities,\" Mabel says slowly. \"While the world is ending. While Bill is literally turning people into furniture.\"\n\n\"Yeah.\"\n\n\"Cool. Normal Tuesday stuff.\" Mabel sets down her knitting and cracks her knuckles. Her expression shifts from scared to determined - that fierce Mabel look that Dipper has learned to trust more than any journal entry. \"So how do we find it?\"\n\nDipper spreads the journal open wider. There are two threads to follow. Ford's journal contains more encoded information - hidden pages, invisible ink notes, coordinates that might lead somewhere. Ford was meticulous, paranoid even, and he hid information in layers precisely for situations like this. If Dipper can decode everything, the journal itself might contain the roadmap they need.\n\nBut there's another option. Old Man McGucket - Fiddleford McGucket - once worked alongside Ford on the portal project. He was there at the beginning, before he erased his own memories. After the memory gun's effects were reversed, McGucket got his mind back. He might remember things about the Axolotl that aren't in any journal. Things Ford told him. Things they discovered together.\n\n\"Two leads,\" Dipper says. \"The journal, or McGucket.\"\n\nMabel looks at the journal, then out the window at the burning sky. \"Whatever we do, bro-bro, we better do it fast. I don't think the world is going to wait for us to make a study guide.\"\n\nShe's right. The Fearamid pulses with sickly light. Somewhere in the distance, Bill's laughter echoes across the broken landscape like thunder from a storm that will never end. They have a lead - maybe the only lead that matters. The Axolotl. The one thing Bill fears.\n\nNow they just have to find it before Bill finds them.",
    choices: [
      { label: "Search Ford's journals for more clues", target: "axolotl_journal" },
      { label: "Find McGucket - he worked with Ford and might know something", target: "axolotl_mcgucket" }
    ]
  },
  axolotl_journal: {
    text: "Dipper dives into Journal 3 like his life depends on it. Because it does.\n\nHe starts with the blacklight - clicking on the portable UV flashlight he keeps in his vest pocket (he's been carrying it since, oh, approximately day three of this insane summer). Under the purple glow, Journal 3 transforms. Hidden text blooms across pages that looked blank moments ago. Diagrams appear. Warnings materialize in Ford's urgent handwriting, scrawled in the margins like the thoughts of a man who knew he was running out of time.\n\n\"Mabel, hand me the lemon juice,\" Dipper says.\n\n\"Why do you have lemon juice?\"\n\n\"Ford used at least three different types of invisible ink. Blacklight reveals one layer. Heat reveals another. And lemon juice activates the third.\" Dipper carefully dabs lemon juice onto a page near the back of the journal. Slowly, like a photograph developing, more text emerges.\n\nMabel leans over his shoulder. \"Bro, you are the nerdiest person alive and I mean that as the highest compliment.\"\n\nWhat the hidden pages reveal makes Dipper's heart race. Ford had done extensive research on the Axolotl - far more than the surface-level entries suggested. There are pages and pages of notes, cross-references, and diagrams. Ford describes the Axolotl as an entity of almost incomprehensible scope - a being that exists in the space between all dimensions, in what Ford calls 'the Interstitial Void' or, more poetically, 'the space where reality breathes.' Every dimension, every timeline, every possible universe - they all have tiny gaps between them, like the spaces between pages in a book. The Axolotl lives in those gaps.\n\n\"It's been there since before Bill,\" Dipper reads aloud. \"Since before the Nightmare Realm, since before most dimensions existed at all. Ford calls it 'the oldest patience' - it doesn't act, it waits. It doesn't command, it offers. It's the opposite of Bill in every way.\"\n\n\"So it's like a cosmic guidance counselor,\" Mabel offers.\n\n\"More like a cosmic... judge? No. A cosmic... option. It gives choices. Where Bill takes them away.\"\n\nThe journal contains coordinates - actual dimensional coordinates that Ford calculated for reaching the Axolotl. But they're encoded in a cipher that uses a combination of the Zodiac symbols and what appears to be an alien number system Ford encountered during his time in other dimensions. Dipper is pretty sure he can decode them given enough time. The coordinates seem to point to a specific location in Gravity Falls where the barrier between dimensions is thinnest.\n\nBut then Dipper finds something that makes his blood run cold. A warning, written in red ink so dark it's almost black, triple-underlined:\n\n\"BILL KNOWS ABOUT THE AXOLOTL. HE HAS SET TRAPS. FALSE CLUES. DECOY PORTALS. ANYONE SEARCHING FOR THE AXOLOTL WILL FIND BILL'S LURES FIRST. THE DREAM DEMON HAS HAD EONS TO PREPARE FOR THIS.\"\n\nDipper swallows hard. Of course Bill set traps. Bill is paranoid about the one thing that can actually threaten him. He'd have scattered false trails like breadcrumbs, leading anyone who searched for the Axolotl straight into his clutches.\n\nBut Ford left a countermeasure. At the very bottom of the page, in tiny handwriting that Dipper needs a magnifying glass to read, there's a note that changes everything:\n\n\"TRUST NOTHING THAT CLAIMS TO BE THE AXOLOTL UNLESS IT SPEAKS IN PALINDROMES. The true Axolotl's speech has a quality that Bill cannot replicate - a symmetry of language that reflects its nature as a being of perfect balance. Palindromes. Words and phrases that read the same forward and backward. This is the key. This is how you know.\"\n\nDipper copies this down carefully. Palindromes. A word that reads the same both ways. Like 'racecar' or 'level' or 'was it a car or a cat I saw.' The Axolotl speaks in palindromes. Bill can't fake that. It's their lifeline - the one way to tell truth from trap.\n\nMabel, who has been flipping through earlier pages of the journal, suddenly gasps. \"Dipper! Look at this!\"\n\nShe's found something wedged between two pages - a loose sheet of paper, folded small. Dipper unfolds it carefully. It's covered in a different kind of code - not Ford's usual ciphers but something more chaotic, more angular. The symbols look almost like Bill's own writing.\n\n\"This wasn't written by Ford,\" Dipper says slowly. \"I think... I think this is a message FROM Bill. Or at least, it uses his cipher system.\"\n\n\"Why would one of Bill's messages be in Ford's journal?\"\n\n\"Maybe Bill planted it. Maybe it's one of those traps Ford warned about.\" Dipper stares at the two paths before him. The encoded coordinates that Ford left - the real trail, careful and hidden. Or this mysterious message in Bill's own cipher, which could be a shortcut... or could be exactly the kind of trap Ford warned about.\n\nThe Shack groans around them. More eye-bats pass overhead. Time is not on their side. They need to make a decision - follow Ford's careful breadcrumbs, or risk the faster but potentially devastating shortcut.",
    choices: [
      { label: "Follow the encoded coordinates", target: "axolotl_clue" },
      { label: "There's a coded message - it seems to be from Bill himself", target: "axolotl_cipher_trap" }
    ]
  },
  axolotl_mcgucket: {
    text: "Finding McGucket means venturing outside, which means facing Weirdmageddon head-on.\n\nDipper and Mabel gear up as best they can. Dipper grabs his journal, his flashlight, and a grappling hook he borrowed (permanently) from the Shack's gift shop. Mabel dons her most battle-ready sweater - the one with the armored kitten on it - and fills her pockets with everything from silly string to glitter bombs. \"Glitter is basically tiny knives for your eyes,\" she explains when Dipper raises an eyebrow. \"Don't question my methods.\"\n\nThe trip to the junkyard is a nightmare. Literally. The streets of Gravity Falls have become a surrealist painting come to terrifying life. Buildings float upside down. The lake has turned to something that looks like water but moves like it's alive, reaching pseudopods toward anyone who gets too close. Gravity reverses without warning - they have to grab onto a fire hydrant at one point when the ground decides it's now a ceiling. Time stutters around them: a flower blooms, dies, and blooms again in the space of three seconds. A deer walks past them backward, un-eating a bush.\n\n\"This is the worst field trip ever,\" Mabel mutters, pulling Dipper into a ditch as a patrol of eye-bats soars overhead. The eye-bats are Bill's surveillance system - giant floating eyeballs with bat wings, scanning the landscape for anyone who hasn't been captured yet. Their pupils glow red when they spot something, and their screech is the kind of sound that makes your teeth hurt.\n\nThey make it to McGucket's junkyard by a combination of stealth, luck, and one very well-aimed glitter bomb that temporarily blinded an eye-bat (Mabel's methods: validated). The junkyard looks different now - someone has been busy.\n\nWhere there was once just a chaotic sprawl of rusted cars, broken appliances, and discarded furniture, there is now a fortress. McGucket has welded old cars into walls, stacked washing machines into watchtowers, and created a perimeter defense system out of what appears to be seventeen toasters wired to a car battery. A sign made from a bent license plate reads: \"TRESPASSERS WILL BE TOASTED (LITERALLY).\"\n\n\"HALT! WHO GOES THERE!\" A telescope made from tin cans swivels toward them from atop a tower of refrigerators. \"State yer name, yer business, and yer favorite type of banjo string!\"\n\n\"McGucket! It's us! Dipper and Mabel!\"\n\nA hatch opens in the side of a school bus and Fiddleford Hadron McGucket emerges, looking like a post-apocalyptic wizard. He's wearing goggles made from soda bottle bottoms, a cape fashioned from a shower curtain, and boots that appear to be constructed from duct tape and pure determination. His long white beard is braided with copper wire. He looks unhinged, brilliant, and absolutely in his element.\n\n\"Well butter my biscuits and call me a circuit board! The Pines twins!\" McGucket scurries toward them, surprisingly spry for a man his age. \"Get inside, get inside! Bill's got ears everywhere - well, eyes, technically, but you get my meanin'.\"\n\nInside the bunker, it's surprisingly cozy. McGucket has rigged up lighting from car headlights, heating from what Dipper is pretty sure is a repurposed nuclear fuel cell (he decides not to ask), and a surprisingly comfortable seating area made from old car seats. On every surface, there are inventions in various states of completion - devices and gadgets and gizmos that hum and whir and occasionally spark.\n\n\"McGucket, we need your help,\" Dipper says. \"We're looking for the Axolotl.\"\n\nMcGucket goes very, very still. His goggles-covered eyes stare at Dipper. For a long moment, the only sound is the hum of his inventions and the distant screams of Weirdmageddon outside.\n\n\"The Axolotl,\" he repeats quietly. All the manic energy drains from his voice. \"I remember now. After the memory gun's effects wore off, it all came back. Stanford and I... we made contact with it. Once. Briefly. Through the portal, before everything went wrong.\"\n\nHe sits down heavily on an old car seat. \"It was like touchin' the mind of the universe itself. So vast and calm and OLD. I saw things that made my brain feel like a thimble tryin' to hold the ocean. That's why I used the memory gun the first time - not because of Bill, but because of the Axolotl. My mind couldn't handle what I'd seen.\"\n\n\"But now you remember,\" Mabel says gently.\n\n\"I do. And I reckon my mind's tougher now than it was then.\" McGucket pushes his goggles up, revealing sharp, clear eyes. The old genius is in there, fully present. \"I can build a device to reach the Axolotl's dimension. I know the frequency, the resonance pattern. But I need parts I ain't got in this junkyard. Specific parts. Interdimensional-grade components.\"\n\n\"Where do we get those?\" Dipper asks, though something in McGucket's expression tells him he already knows the answer.\n\n\"The Fearamid,\" McGucket says. \"Bill's throne is built from pieces of other dimensions. Some of those pieces contain exactly the components I need. We'd have to sneak into the heart of Bill's domain and steal from him.\"\n\nThe Fearamid. Bill's floating pyramid of nightmares. Sneaking in there would be insanity.\n\nBut then again, searching for a cosmic salamander during the apocalypse is already insanity. What's a little more?",
    choices: [
      { label: "Help McGucket build the device", target: "axolotl_machine" },
      { label: "McGucket's plan sounds too dangerous - look for another way", target: "axolotl_journal" }
    ]
  },
  axolotl_clue: {
    text: "Dipper works the cipher like his life depends on it. Because it does.\n\nFord's encoded coordinates use a layered system - the Zodiac symbols map to a base-twelve number system that Ford adapted from a mathematical language he encountered in Dimension 52-B. Dipper has to cross-reference three different pages of the journal, use the blacklight twice, and at one point hold the page up to a mirror to read a reversed annotation. It takes him forty-five minutes of intense concentration while Mabel keeps watch at the window, calling out eye-bat positions like a spotter at a very weird tennis match.\n\n\"Two o'clock, high... clear. Nine o'clock, low... it's circling... okay, it moved on. You're good, keep going.\"\n\n\"Got it!\" Dipper nearly shouts, then catches himself and drops to a whisper. \"The coordinates point to a specific location in the forest. It's... Mabel, it's the cave. The cave where Bill was originally summoned.\"\n\nMabel turns from the window. \"The one Ford told us about? Where he made the deal?\"\n\n\"That's the one. Ford's notes say the cave is built on a natural weak point between dimensions - that's WHY Bill was able to be summoned there in the first place. The barrier between realities is thinnest at that spot. If there's anywhere in Gravity Falls where we could reach the Axolotl's dimension, it's there.\"\n\nThe trek through the forest is unlike anything they've experienced. Gravity Falls' woods were always strange - bottomless pits, magical creatures, the occasional gnome uprising - but Weirdmageddon has transformed them into something from a fever dream. Trees grow upside down, their roots clawing at the fractured sky. The ground shifts between solid earth and something that feels like walking on a sleeping creature's back. Colors are wrong: the bark is purple, the leaves are a shade of orange that hurts to look at, and shadows fall in directions that have nothing to do with where the light is coming from.\n\nThey pass a clearing where time has completely stopped - a bird frozen mid-flight, raindrops suspended in the air like glass beads. Dipper reaches out and touches one; it's warm. A few hundred yards later, they stumble through a pocket where time moves too fast - moss grows and dies on a rock in the space of a breath, and Mabel's hair grows two inches before they scramble out of the zone.\n\n\"I am NOT getting a Weirdmageddon haircut,\" Mabel declares, tucking her now-longer hair into her sweater.\n\nThe cave entrance is exactly where Ford's coordinates indicated - a narrow crack in a cliff face, almost invisible unless you know to look for it. Dipper recognizes subtle markings around the entrance: symbols that match the Zodiac wheel, carved into the rock so long ago that lichen has nearly swallowed them. Someone - or something - has been marking this place as special for a very, very long time.\n\nInside, the cave opens up into a chamber that takes their breath away.\n\nThe walls are covered in paintings. Not Ford's work - these are ancient. Thousands of years old, maybe tens of thousands. Done in pigments that have somehow retained their color across millennia. And the subject of the paintings is unmistakable: a massive salamander-like creature, pink and luminous, surrounded by stars and dimensional rifts. The Axolotl. Ancient humans knew about it. They painted it here, in this cave where the walls between worlds are thin, where you can almost hear the cosmic hum of other dimensions if you press your ear to the stone.\n\n\"Whoa,\" Mabel breathes, running her fingers just above the surface of the paintings without touching them. \"These are beautiful. Look - the people in the paintings are talking to it. They're reaching up and it's reaching down.\"\n\nShe's right. The paintings tell a story: humans approaching the Axolotl, communicating with it, receiving what looks like wisdom or healing or protection. There are also paintings of a triangular figure - Bill - and in these, the Axolotl stands between Bill and the humans, a cosmic guardian.\n\n\"This was a sacred place,\" Dipper says, his voice hushed with awe. \"People came here to contact the Axolotl. For thousands of years.\"\n\nAt the back of the cave, behind the paintings, there's something else. A section of the cave wall that doesn't look quite real. It shimmers faintly, like heat haze, and when Dipper holds his hand near it, he can feel a vibration - not physical, but something deeper, like reality itself humming at a frequency just beyond hearing.\n\n\"A natural portal,\" Dipper whispers. \"Dormant, but real. This is a doorway to the space between dimensions. To the Axolotl's domain.\"\n\nThe journal has instructions for activating natural portals - Ford documented the process in meticulous detail. But the cave paintings also seem to depict a ritual, older and more organic, that the ancient people used. Two paths to the same destination: Ford's scientific method, or the ancient ritual drawn on these walls. Both lead to the Axolotl. The question is which path to trust - the precision of science, or the wisdom of those who came before.",
    choices: [
      { label: "Activate the portal using the journal's instructions", target: "axolotl_portal" },
      { label: "Perform the ancient ritual depicted on the cave walls", target: "axolotl_ritual" }
    ]
  },
  axolotl_cipher_trap: {
    text: "Against every instinct screaming in the back of his mind, Dipper decides to decode Bill's message.\n\nHe tells himself it's strategic. Know your enemy, right? And maybe Bill accidentally revealed something useful. Villains do that sometimes - they're so confident in their own cleverness that they leave real information in their taunts. Dipper has seen it happen in, like, at least twelve mystery novels and nine detective shows.\n\nThe cipher is Bill's own system - a substitution code based on the reversed alphabet with additional symbol layers. Dipper's cracked it before. His fingers work quickly, translating symbol by symbol, letter by letter, while Mabel watches over his shoulder.\n\nThe decoded message reads: \"LOOKING FOR THE SALAMANDER? SMART KID. HERE'S A SHORTCUT - GO TO THE STATUE OF NATHANIEL NORTHWEST IN THE TOWN SQUARE. FACE NORTH. WALK EXACTLY 618 PACES. YOU'LL FIND A DOOR THAT WASN'T THERE BEFORE. KNOCK THREE TIMES AND SAY 'AXOLOTL.' YOU'RE WELCOME. - A FRIEND.\"\n\n\"A friend,\" Mabel reads, her voice dripping with skepticism. \"Since when does Bill sign things 'a friend?' He usually signs with a drawing of himself laughing.\"\n\n\"Maybe it's not from Bill,\" Dipper says, but even he doesn't believe it. The cipher is Bill's. The number 618 is suspicious - Bill loves hidden number games. And the whole thing feels too easy, too convenient, like a gift-wrapped present sitting in the middle of a minefield.\n\nBut the clock is ticking. The world is ending. And the message promises a shortcut.\n\n\"Ford's warning said Bill set traps for anyone looking for the Axolotl,\" Mabel reminds him, because Mabel has always been better at listening to warnings than Dipper has. \"This could be exactly one of those traps.\"\n\n\"Or it could be a real shortcut that Bill tried to disguise as a trap so no one would use it. A double-bluff.\"\n\n\"Dipper...\"\n\n\"I know, I know. But what if we're wrong? What if this saves time we don't have?\"\n\nThey go. Against Mabel's better judgment, against the warnings in the journal, against the screaming voice of reason in Dipper's own head that sounds suspiciously like Ford saying \"don't trust anything connected to Bill Cipher,\" they go.\n\nThe statue of Nathaniel Northwest still stands in what's left of the town square, though it's now wearing a party hat that materialized during Weirdmageddon. They face north. They walk 618 paces, counting carefully, stepping over debris and dodging pools of temporal distortion. At exactly 618 paces, they find it: a door. Standing upright in the middle of a field, frame and all, connected to nothing. It's golden, with an eye symbol on it. It is the most obviously suspicious door in the history of doors.\n\n\"This is a trap,\" Mabel says flatly.\n\n\"Let me just...\" Dipper knocks three times. \"Axolotl,\" he says.\n\nThe door swings open. Behind it is a swirling vortex of pink and gold light. It looks exactly like what you'd expect a portal to the Axolotl's dimension to look like. It's warm. It's inviting. It practically hums with cosmic energy.\n\n\"See?\" Dipper says. \"It's real. Let's go.\"\n\nThey step through. The moment they're both inside, the portal slams shut behind them. The pink light turns yellow. Then gold. Then a very specific, very familiar shade of glowing, malicious YELLOW. And a laugh begins - high-pitched, echoing, building in volume until it fills every corner of whatever space they've stumbled into.\n\nBill Cipher materializes in front of them, enormous, his single eye crinkled in what passes for a grin on a triangle. His top hat alone is the size of a house. His arms spread wide in a mockery of welcome.\n\n\"WELL, WELL, WELL, WELL, WELL, WELL, WELL!\" Bill's voice hits them like a physical force, each 'well' punctuated by a flash of blue fire. \"PINE TREE AND SHOOTING STAR, WALKING RIGHT INTO MY PARLOR! DID YOU REALLY THINK I'D LEAVE A ROAD MAP TO THE ONE THING THAT CAN STOP ME?\" He leans down until his eye is inches from their faces. \"I PLANTED THAT MESSAGE THIRTY YEARS AGO, KID. THIRTY YEARS, JUST WAITING FOR SOME SMART-BUT-NOT-SMART-ENOUGH HERO TO COME ALONG AND DECODE IT. AND WHADDYA KNOW - IT'S MY FAVORITE PAIR OF TWINS!\"\n\nDipper's stomach drops. The trap Ford warned about. The trap that was literally described in the journal. The trap he walked into anyway because he thought he was clever enough to outsmart a billion-year-old dream demon.\n\n\"I'M GONNA MAKE AN EXAMPLE OUT OF YOU TWO,\" Bill announces cheerfully, his fingers crackling with blue energy. \"SOMETHING FUN. SOMETHING CREATIVE. SOMETHING THAT REALLY CAPTURES YOUR ESSENCES AS PEOPLE.\"\n\nHe snaps his fingers. The world goes white. Then soft. Then... fuzzy. Dipper tries to scream but his mouth won't open right. His body feels wrong - flat, floppy, made of something that isn't skin or muscle or bone. He can see, but through plastic eyes. He can think, but his thoughts feel muffled, like they're wrapped in cotton. In fabric. In...\n\n\"OH, THIS IS MY BEST WORK YET!\" Bill howls with laughter, holding up his hands. On his left hand: a sock puppet with a blue pine tree hat, made from a white tube sock with googly eyes and yarn hair. On his right hand: a sock puppet with a pink sweater (hand-knitted, impossibly tiny), a glued-on smile made of felt, and yarn braids.\n\nDipper and Mabel. Sock puppets. Conscious, aware, and utterly helpless.\n\n\"LADIES AND GENTLEMEN AND BEINGS OF INCOMPREHENSIBLE GEOMETRY,\" Bill announces to his assembled demon friends - Teeth, Pyronica, Eight Ball, Kryptos, and the rest of his inner circle - \"TONIGHT'S ENTERTAINMENT: THE SOCK PUPPET SHOW!\"\n\nBill performs a two-hour puppet show retelling Dipper and Mabel's entire summer, but twisted and mocking. Puppet-Dipper has a squeaky voice and is afraid of everything. Puppet-Mabel is aggressively cheerful to the point of insanity. The demons love it. They howl with laughter at scenes that were, for the real twins, moments of genuine courage and growth, now reduced to comedy bits performed by socks.\n\nMabel's puppet has the tiniest knit sweater Dipper has ever seen. Even as a cosmic horror, Bill has an eye for craftsmanship. Inside their fabric prisons, the twins can only watch as their lives are turned into a joke, performed on the hands of the monster who ended their world.",
    ending: true
  },
  axolotl_machine: {
    text: "\"Alright then,\" McGucket says, his eyes lighting up with the particular gleam of a genius who's been given permission to build something impossible. \"Let's get to work.\"\n\nThe heist on the Fearamid is a story in itself - a terrifying, adrenaline-soaked dash through Bill's domain that involves Dipper's quick thinking, Mabel's grappling hook, and McGucket's frankly insane willingness to ride a homemade rocket-sled directly at a floating pyramid of nightmares. They get what they need: three crystals that hum with interdimensional energy, a chunk of Nightmare Realm material that looks like solidified bad dreams, and a small device that McGucket identifies as a \"dimensional resonance stabilizer\" which looks, to Dipper's untrained eye, like a fancy doorknob.\n\nBack at the junkyard bunker, McGucket builds.\n\nHe builds like a man possessed - which, given his history with Bill Cipher, is a comparison he probably wouldn't appreciate. His hands move with preternatural speed and precision, soldering, welding, wiring, and occasionally whacking things with a wrench while muttering equations. Dipper assists where he can, holding components in place and reading aloud from the journal's technical specifications. Mabel provides moral support, snacks (she found a vending machine that Weirdmageddon hadn't fully corrupted yet, though the candy bars taste slightly of existential dread), and an unwavering belief that this will work.\n\nThe finished device is... a lot.\n\nImagine a washing machine. Now imagine that washing machine had a baby with a 1972 Chevrolet engine, a church organ, and a satellite dish. The baby was then raised by a tuba and a Tesla coil. That baby is what sits in the middle of McGucket's bunker, humming with barely contained power, sparking occasionally, and making a sound like a cat purring inside a thunderstorm.\n\n\"I call her the Dimensional Washing Machine,\" McGucket announces proudly. \"Or the DWM. She'll punch through the barriers between dimensions and open a pathway to the Interstitial Void - the space between all realities where your cosmic salamander lives.\"\n\n\"It looks like it's going to explode,\" Dipper observes.\n\n\"Oh, she might! But she'll open the portal first. Probably.\"\n\n\"Probably?\"\n\n\"Science ain't about certainty, boy, it's about enthusiastic approximation!\"\n\nMcGucket throws the switch. The DWM roars to life. The tuba section produces a note so low it vibrates in their bones. The washing machine drum spins, the crystals from the Fearamid glow, and the dimensional resonance stabilizer (fancy doorknob) begins to turn on its own. The air in front of the device splits open like a zipper being pulled across the fabric of reality.\n\nThe tear is beautiful and terrifying. Through it, they can see... nothing. Not darkness - nothing. An absence so complete it makes their eyes water trying to process it. The space between dimensions isn't empty; it's the concept of space before space decided what it wanted to be. It's potential. It's the blank page before the story starts.\n\n\"There she is,\" McGucket whispers reverently. \"The gap between everythin'. The Axolotl's home.\"\n\nBut something is wrong. The tear isn't stable. It pulses and warps, its edges crackling with energy that shifts between colors that don't exist in normal reality. The DWM is shaking violently, bolts popping off, the tuba section producing increasingly distressed honking sounds. And through the tear, something is coming.\n\nNot the Axolotl. Something else entirely.\n\nFragments begin pouring through - pieces of broken realities, raw chaos energy, the debris of dimensions that collapsed or were destroyed (some, Dipper realizes with a chill, destroyed by Bill himself during his eons of interdimensional rampage). It's like opening a door and finding that the hallway behind it is flooding. Shards of other worlds tumble into the bunker: a piece of sky that's the wrong shade of blue, a fragment of ground from a world where the earth was made of something crystalline, a burst of music from a dimension where sound has physical form.\n\n\"The portal's too wide!\" McGucket shouts over the increasing roar. \"She's pullin' in debris from the Void! I need to calibrate the - the flangelator is - WATCH OUT!\"\n\nA wave of raw chaos energy surges through the tear - not solid, not liquid, not gas, but something more fundamental. Pure, unformed possibility, the stuff that realities are made from before they decide what they're going to be. It washes over the bunker floor like iridescent fog, and everywhere it touches, things change: a wrench becomes a flower becomes a small bird becomes a wrench again. The laws of physics are suggestions in this energy, not rules.\n\nThe tear is getting bigger. Through it, somewhere impossibly far away, they can feel the Axolotl - a vast, patient presence, aware of them, waiting. But between them and the Axolotl is a churning sea of interdimensional chaos.\n\n\"We can push through!\" Dipper shouts. \"The Axolotl is in there - I can feel it!\"\n\n\"Or we can try to stabilize the portal!\" McGucket yells back, already elbow-deep in the DWM's innards. \"If I can adjust the resonance, maybe I can filter out the debris and create a clean pathway! But it'll take time and I ain't sure the machine will hold!\"\n\nThe chaos energy swirls around their ankles. Time is running out. The machine screams. The portal pulses. Somewhere beyond the chaos, the Axolotl waits with infinite patience - but patience won't matter if the twins are consumed by the raw stuff of unformed reality before they can reach it.",
    choices: [
      { label: "Push through into the between-space despite the chaos", target: "axolotl_dimension" },
      { label: "Try to stabilize the portal with McGucket's help", target: "ending_axolotl_absorbed" }
    ]
  },
  axolotl_portal: {
    text: "Dipper opens Journal 3 to the page labeled 'NATURAL PORTAL ACTIVATION - EMERGENCY PROTOCOL' and starts reading.\n\nFord's instructions are precise, methodical, and written with the urgency of a man who knew that someday, someone might need to use them in exactly this kind of desperate situation. Step one: identify the portal surface. Done - the shimmering section of cave wall pulses faintly, like a sleeping heart. Step two: prepare a resonance field using quartz crystals arranged in the pattern of the Zodiac wheel. Dipper pulls out the bag of crystals he grabbed from the Shack (he packed them \"just in case,\" which Mabel mocked at the time and is now very grateful for). Step three: apply a controlled burst of interdimensional energy to wake the portal from dormancy.\n\n\"The journal says Ford used a modified electromagnet for step three,\" Dipper reads. \"We don't have a modified electromagnet.\"\n\n\"We have a Mabel,\" Mabel says, pulling out her grappling hook. \"I also have this.\" She produces a small device from her sweater pocket - one of McGucket's inventions she apparently pocketed during a previous visit. It sparks and hums. \"I have no idea what it does but it looks sciency.\"\n\nDipper examines it. It's a miniature electromagnetic pulse generator. Why Mabel had it, how she got it, and whether McGucket knows it's missing are all questions for a less apocalyptic day.\n\n\"Mabel, you beautiful, sweater-wearing genius. This is exactly what we need.\"\n\n\"I prefer the term 'accidentally prepared.'\"\n\nThey arrange the crystals on the cave floor in the Zodiac pattern. Dipper double-checks and triple-checks the alignment against Ford's diagrams. Each crystal sits at a point of the wheel: the Pine Tree, the Shooting Star, the Llama, the Hand, the Glasses, the Question Mark, the Ice Bag, the Star, the Crescent, the Stitched Heart. When the last crystal is placed, the cave hums. The ancient paintings on the walls seem to glow faintly, as if the long-dead artists are nodding in approval.\n\n\"Here goes everything,\" Dipper says, and activates the electromagnetic pulse.\n\nThe effect is not subtle.\n\nThe crystals ignite - not with fire but with light, each one blazing in a different color. The Zodiac pattern burns itself into the cave floor. The shimmering wall section ripples like water, then begins to peel open. Behind it is not another cave, not another place at all, but a FEELING - a sensation so profound it's almost physical. Like falling asleep and waking up at the exact same time. Like remembering something you never knew. Like homesickness for a home that doesn't exist yet.\n\nThe portal to the space between dimensions is open.\n\nThrough it, they can sense vast, quiet emptiness - not threatening, but patient. Waiting. Somewhere in that impossibility, the Axolotl exists. They can feel its awareness turning toward them like the gaze of something incomprehensibly gentle.\n\n\"It's real,\" Mabel whispers, tears in her eyes though she couldn't say why. \"Dipper, it's actually real. I can feel it.\"\n\n\"Me too,\" Dipper says, and his voice cracks.\n\nBut they aren't the only ones who can feel it.\n\nThe portal's activation sends a shockwave through the dimensional fabric of Gravity Falls - a ripple that Bill Cipher detects instantly. In the Fearamid, miles away, his eye snaps open wide. He feels it like a wound: the Axolotl's dimension, touching his domain. The one thing in all of existence that can actually undo his work.\n\nThe sky above the forest tears open. Bill's enormous eye appears, burning gold, filling the gap between the trees with terrible, focused light.\n\n\"PINE TREE! SHOOTING STAR!\" Bill's voice shakes the cave. Dust falls from the ceiling. The crystals rattle. \"GOING SOMEWHERE?\"\n\nEye-bats swarm from behind Bill's iris like tears from a furious eye - dozens of them, hundreds, screaming toward the cave entrance. Their screeches echo off the stone walls. The ground shakes with Bill's fury. He's not amused this time, not playing games - he's terrified, and Bill Cipher terrified is Bill Cipher at his most dangerous.\n\n\"THE COSMIC SALAMANDER CAN'T HELP YOU!\" Bill roars, but his voice has a quality Dipper has never heard before - a frantic edge, a crack in the supreme confidence. \"I WILL TEAR THIS CAVE APART ATOM BY ATOM BEFORE I LET YOU THROUGH THAT PORTAL!\"\n\nThe eye-bats are seconds away. Bill's energy is building - he's preparing to blast the entire hillside. The portal stands open, shimmering, offering passage to the only hope they have. But it won't stay open long, and if Bill destroys the crystals, it will close forever.\n\nDipper looks at Mabel. Mabel looks at Dipper. The eye-bats shriek. Bill's power crackles. The portal hums with cosmic patience.\n\nThey have seconds to decide: leap through the portal now, together, before Bill can stop them? Or stand and fight, keeping the portal open so that others - Ford, Stan, Soos, Wendy, everyone still free in Gravity Falls - might have a chance to follow?\n\nThe choice burns in their chests like twin stars deciding whether to shine or to shield.",
    choices: [
      { label: "Jump through the portal NOW before Bill can stop them", target: "axolotl_dimension" },
      { label: "Stay and fight to keep the portal open for others to follow", target: "ending_axolotl_sacrifice" }
    ]
  },
  axolotl_ritual: {
    text: "The ancient paintings tell a story, and within that story is a set of instructions.\n\nDipper studies the cave walls with the intensity of someone reading a treasure map, because that's exactly what this is. The paintings depict a ritual in sequential panels, like a prehistoric comic strip. Panel one: two figures approach the shimmering wall - the natural portal. Panel two: the figures draw symbols on the ground using pigment made from crushed minerals. Panel three: the figures stand on either side of the symbols and raise their hands. Panel four: the portal opens and the great pink salamander reaches through.\n\nBut there's a crucial detail that Dipper almost misses. In every panel, there are always TWO figures performing the ritual together. Never one. And the two figures have specific markings: one bears a tree symbol on their clothing, the other a star.\n\n\"Mabel,\" Dipper says quietly, his voice tight with awe. \"Look at the symbols on the figures.\"\n\nMabel looks. Her eyes go wide. \"That's... a pine tree. And a shooting star. Dipper, those are US. Those are our symbols from the Zodiac.\"\n\n\"This ritual was meant for us. Or for people like us. Two people, bonded, working together. The Pine Tree and the Shooting Star, performing the ritual as one.\" Dipper runs his hand through his hair, sending his hat slightly askew. \"The Zodiac symbols aren't random. They've been connected to this place, to this ritual, for thousands of years. Ford thought the Zodiac was about defeating Bill, but maybe it was always about reaching the Axolotl.\"\n\nThey get to work. Following the paintings, Mabel grinds minerals from the cave walls into colored pigments using stones - reds from iron oxide, yellows from sulfur deposits, whites from calcite. Dipper uses these to draw the symbols on the cave floor, copying them precisely from the paintings. A circle. Within the circle, the ten symbols of the Zodiac. Within the Zodiac, a spiral that represents the space between dimensions. It takes time. The symbols must be exact.\n\n\"This is like the world's most stressful art project,\" Mabel says, carefully adding details to the Shooting Star symbol with a steady hand that surprises even her. Under pressure, Mabel's artistic skills become laser-precise.\n\n\"Almost done,\" Dipper says, completing the final symbol. \"Now - the paintings show the two figures standing on opposite sides of the circle. I take the Pine Tree position, you take the Shooting Star. Then we raise our hands and speak the words.\"\n\n\"What words?\"\n\nDipper points to the paintings. Beneath the figures, there are symbols that aren't images but language - an ancient script that Ford actually documented in the journal. Dipper cross-references frantically, translating. \"It's the Axolotl invocation. 'A-X-O-L-O-T-L, my time has come to burn. I invoke the ancient power that I may return.' But in the original ancient language. Ford wrote the phonetic pronunciation here.\"\n\nThey take their positions. Dipper on the north side of the circle, standing on the Pine Tree symbol. Mabel on the south side, standing on the Shooting Star. The cave is silent except for the distant sounds of Weirdmageddon outside - explosions, laughter, the fabric of reality being used as a chew toy by a triangle-shaped monster.\n\nThey raise their hands. They begin to speak the ancient words together, their voices intertwining. The language is unlike anything they've ever spoken - it resonates in their chests, in their bones, in the spaces between their thoughts. The symbols on the floor begin to glow. First dimly, then brighter. The cave walls pulse. The ancient paintings seem to move, the figures in them nodding, encouraging, as if the long-dead artists left a piece of their hope embedded in the pigments.\n\nThe ritual is working.\n\nBut it's slow. The portal behind the wall brightens gradually, like dawn. It can't be rushed. And somewhere in the fractured sky above Gravity Falls, Bill Cipher feels it. Like a splinter in his mind. Like the one sound in the universe he never wants to hear. The Axolotl's frequency, humming through the dimensional barriers.\n\nBill's reaction is immediate and explosive.\n\nThe sky above the forest CRACKS - not the slow fracturing of Weirdmageddon's ongoing damage, but a sudden, violent split as Bill forces his attention directly onto this spot. His eye appears through the crack, burning with fury and something else: fear. Real, primal fear. The kind of fear that makes a being who has conquered dimensions act recklessly.\n\n\"WHAT ARE YOU DOING?!\" Bill's voice splits the air. The cave shakes. Stalactites crash down. The ritual circle flickers. \"STOP THAT! STOP THAT RIGHT NOW!\"\n\nEye-bats pour from the crack in the sky. Bill's weirdness waves cascade toward the forest, warping trees, inverting gravity in patches, turning the ground to liquid in places. His forces are converging on this cave from every direction. They can hear the crashing of things moving through the forest - not just eye-bats but Bill's more powerful minions, his Henchmaniacs, drawn by his panic like sharks to blood.\n\nThe ritual is halfway done. The portal is half-open. The Axolotl's presence is there, just barely, reaching toward them across the vast emptiness between dimensions. But the twins need to keep speaking, keep channeling, keep standing in their positions while Bill's forces close in like a tightening fist.\n\n\"DON'T STOP!\" Dipper shouts over the chaos. \"Whatever happens, don't stop the ritual!\"\n\nMabel nods, her face set with determination, and they keep chanting as the cave entrance darkens with the shadows of incoming eye-bats and the ground trembles with Bill's rage.",
    choices: [
      { label: "Complete the ritual despite Bill's forces closing in", target: "axolotl_summoning" },
      { label: "Abandon the ritual and flee", target: "ending_axolotl_petrified" }
    ]
  },
  axolotl_dimension: {
    text: "They step through, and reality ends.\n\nNot violently, not painfully - it simply stops being a relevant concept. One moment they're in a cave in Gravity Falls (or stumbling through McGucket's chaotic portal), and the next moment they're somewhere that isn't a 'where' at all. The space between dimensions has no ground, no sky, no walls, no air - and yet they can breathe, stand (on nothing), and see (nothing, but somehow clearly). It's like being inside a thought. Like standing in the moment between sleeping and waking, stretched out to infinity.\n\nDipper's first instinct is to panic. Every rational part of his brain screams that this is wrong - humans aren't supposed to be here, in the gaps between realities, in the margins of existence. His feet rest on nothing. His hands grasp nothing. When he looks down, there's no down. When he looks up, there's no up. Direction is a suggestion that the space between dimensions has politely declined.\n\n\"Mabel?\" His voice doesn't echo. It doesn't carry. It exists exactly where he speaks it and nowhere else.\n\n\"I'm here.\" Her hand finds his in the void. Warm. Real. The most solid thing in a place where solidity doesn't exist. \"I'm right here, Dipper.\"\n\nThey hold hands and look around at the impossible space.\n\nIt's not dark. It's not light. It's not any color that has a name, or even colors that don't have names. It's the visual equivalent of silence - not the absence of something but the presence of potential. This is what existence looks like before it decides to be a universe. The raw material of reality, unformed and infinite, stretching in every direction that doesn't exist.\n\nAnd it's not empty.\n\nThey can feel presences. Not close, not far (distance doesn't work here), but THERE. The first and most powerful is vast and warm - like standing near a gentle sun, like being wrapped in a blanket the size of a galaxy. It's patient. It's ancient. It's the Axolotl. They know it without knowing how they know it. The Axolotl is aware of them, has been aware of them since they started searching, maybe since before that. It waits.\n\nBut there are other presences too. Scattered through the void like debris in an ocean, they can sense fragments of... other things. Lost souls - beings from destroyed dimensions who wander the in-between space with nowhere to go and no reality to call home. Broken realities - pieces of worlds that collapsed or were destroyed, drifting like cosmic wreckage. And echoes. Echoes of terrible things. Of destruction and chaos and a very specific triangular laugh that has been cutting through dimensions for billions of years.\n\nBill's past. His history of destruction is embedded in this space like scars in skin. Every dimension he's invaded, every reality he's damaged, every world he's used as a toy and then discarded - they've all left marks in the between-space. And those marks whisper. They tell stories of what Bill has done across eons.\n\n\"Do you hear that?\" Mabel asks. Her voice is small but steady. \"It's like... memories. But not ours.\"\n\n\"The echoes of Bill's past,\" Dipper says. He can feel them pulling at his attention - images and sounds and emotions from Bill's long history of cosmic terror. A flat world burning. A pyramid rising from the ashes. A deal made in desperation. A second chance offered by something vast and kind. A second chance squandered, twisted, turned into a weapon.\n\nAnd beneath all of it, the Axolotl's call. Gentle. Patient. Come, it says, though not in words. Come, and tell me what you need.\n\nTwo paths present themselves - not physically, because paths don't exist here, but as feelings, as directions of intent. They can follow the Axolotl's warmth directly, going to meet the cosmic entity face to face (or face to... salamander). Or they can investigate the echoes first, learning the full truth of Bill's history before they face the one being that might be able to end this.\n\nDipper squeezes Mabel's hand. Mabel squeezes back.\n\n\"The Axolotl is waiting,\" Dipper says. \"It's been waiting for us. Maybe forever.\"\n\n\"But those echoes,\" Mabel says. \"They're showing us something about Bill. Something important. I can feel it - there's a story there that we're supposed to know.\"\n\nShe might be right. Knowledge is power, especially against a being like Bill. Understanding his past - truly understanding it - might be the key to everything. But the Axolotl's call is so warm, so inviting, and they've come so far.\n\nIn the space between dimensions, where time and distance are meaningless, Dipper and Mabel Pines face a choice that is entirely about direction of the heart.",
    choices: [
      { label: "Follow the Axolotl's call", target: "axolotl_meeting" },
      { label: "Investigate the echoes of Bill's past", target: "axolotl_bill_past" }
    ]
  },
  axolotl_meeting: {
    text: "They follow the warmth.\n\nIt's not walking. It's not flying. It's more like... agreeing. They agree to move toward the Axolotl, and the space between dimensions agrees to let them. The void reshapes itself around their intention, the formless potential parting like a curtain to reveal what's behind it.\n\nAnd what's behind it is the most beautiful thing either of them has ever seen.\n\nThe Axolotl fills the space between dimensions the way a sun fills a solar system - not just physically present but fundamentally central, a point around which everything else organizes itself. It's enormous. Not just big, but a kind of big that makes 'big' feel like an insult. Its body is made of starlight and something softer - a gentle pink radiance that pulses like a heartbeat, if a heartbeat could be the size of a universe. Its shape is unmistakably salamander-like: a broad, gentle head, external gills that wave like cosmic coral, tiny perfect legs, and a long, graceful tail that trails off into infinity.\n\nIts skin, if you can call it skin, is translucent and shimmering. Through it, they can see galaxies. Not reflections - actual galaxies, spinning within the Axolotl's body like fish in an aquarium. Dimensions orbit it like planets. Timelines flow through its gills like breath. It is, Dipper realizes with a shock that nearly buckles his knees, alive in a way that nothing else in the multiverse is alive. It IS the multiverse, or at least the connective tissue that holds it all together.\n\nAnd its eyes. Its eyes are ancient. Kind. Sad. Patient. They have seen everything - every dimension born and destroyed, every choice made and unmade, every soul that ever wandered the in-between space looking for something they'd lost. Those eyes look at Dipper and Mabel and see them completely: their fears, their hopes, their love for each other, their courage, their flaws, their entire beautiful, messy, complicated humanity.\n\nWhen it speaks, the voice comes from everywhere and nowhere. It's not heard with the ears but felt with the soul - a resonance that vibrates in the space between thoughts.\n\n\"I have waited long for those with hearts both brave and true. The question now, young Pines, is: what would you have me do?\"\n\nThe words are a palindrome - 'do' and 'od' might not be, but the structure, the feeling, the symmetry is there. This is the real Axolotl, not one of Bill's fakes. Ford's clue was right.\n\nMabel is crying. Not from fear or sadness but from the sheer overwhelming beauty of standing before something this vast and this gentle. Dipper's eyes are wet too, though he'll deny it later. The Axolotl radiates a calm so profound it's like sinking into warm water after being cold for years.\n\n\"You know about Weirdmageddon,\" Dipper says. His voice sounds tiny in this space, but the Axolotl hears every syllable.\n\n\"All that was, and is, and yet may be, unfolds in palindrome inside of me.\" The Axolotl's gills wave gently. \"I know of Bill. I know his pain. I know the world he broke in twain.\"\n\n\"Can you fix it?\" Mabel asks, her voice cracking. \"Can you undo what Bill did? Can you save Gravity Falls? Can you save everyone?\"\n\nThe Axolotl's great head lowers until one of its ancient, kind eyes is level with the twins. Up close, the eye is the size of a lake. Within it, they can see their own reflections - not physical reflections, but reflections of who they are: a boy who is brave and smart and sometimes too stubborn for his own good, and a girl who is fierce and creative and whose optimism is not naivety but a form of courage that most people never achieve.\n\n\"To mend what broke, to heal what bled - the path exists but must be led. The power rests within my form to calm the chaos, still the storm. But cosmic scales must balanced be - all magic carries weight, you see.\"\n\nA cost. There's always a cost. In Gravity Falls, in magic, in the multiverse - nothing is free. Every deal has a price. They learned that from Bill, the hard way.\n\n\"What's the cost?\" Dipper asks, because Dipper always needs to know the details.\n\nThe Axolotl's expression shifts - still kind, but deeper now, more complex. \"Two paths I offer, each one whole. Each mends the world but taxes soul.\"\n\nTwo options. They can plead with the Axolotl - appeal to its sense of compassion, tell it everything about why Gravity Falls matters, about why their world deserves to be saved. Or they can demand that it use its power to destroy Bill Cipher directly - cut the problem off at its source.\n\nThe Axolotl waits, patient as eternity, its pink glow warming the void.",
    choices: [
      { label: "Plead with the Axolotl to undo Weirdmageddon", target: "axolotl_plea" },
      { label: "Demand that the Axolotl destroy Bill Cipher", target: "axolotl_demand" }
    ]
  },
  axolotl_bill_past: {
    text: "They turn toward the echoes, and the void blooms with memories that aren't theirs.\n\nThe first thing they see is a world. But not a world like theirs - a flat world. Literally flat. Two-dimensional. A plane of existence where everything is lines and angles and shapes moving across an infinite surface. It's beautiful in its way - geometric patterns interlock like an endless puzzle, and the beings that live there are shapes: circles, squares, triangles, all interacting in a complex society built on mathematical precision.\n\nOne triangle stands out. He's small, yellow, and his single eye is wide with curiosity. He's different from the others - restless, questioning, always looking at the edges of his flat world and wondering what exists beyond the plane. The other shapes think he's strange. Dangerous, even. They're comfortable in their two dimensions. Why would anyone want more?\n\n\"That's Bill,\" Mabel whispers. \"Before he was... Bill.\"\n\nShe's right. This small, curious triangle is Bill Cipher, eons before the top hat, the bowtie, the cosmic power, and the madness. He's just a shape in a flat world who dreams of something bigger.\n\nThe echoes shift. They see Bill discovering the truth about his dimension - that higher dimensions exist, that his flat world is just one page in an infinite book. They see his excitement turn to frustration as no one believes him, as his own people call him insane, as the world he was born into becomes a prison he can't escape. They see his anger grow. His isolation. His desperation.\n\nAnd then they see the moment everything changed.\n\nBill Cipher burned his dimension. Not metaphorically - literally. He found a way to ignite the very fabric of his two-dimensional reality and watched it burn. Every being he'd ever known. Every shape that had ever existed in his world. Gone. Consumed by fire that Bill started because he couldn't stand being trapped anymore. He destroyed his own home to set himself free.\n\nMabel covers her mouth. Dipper feels sick. The echo shows them the aftermath: Bill, floating alone in the void where his dimension used to be, three-dimensional now, free at last - and utterly, completely alone. The silence of a destroyed world pressing in from every direction.\n\n\"He was sad,\" Mabel says, and her voice is complicated in a way that makes Dipper uncomfortable. \"Right after. He was actually sad about what he did.\"\n\n\"For about five seconds,\" Dipper mutters, but the echo confirms Mabel's observation. There was a moment - brief, barely a flicker - when the being who would become Bill Cipher felt the full weight of what he'd done. Regret. Horror. Grief. It lasted an instant before he crushed it down and chose madness instead.\n\nThe echoes shift again. Now they see the Axolotl. The cosmic salamander, appearing before the newly three-dimensional Bill in the void between dimensions. The Axolotl was already ancient then - old beyond reckoning, patient beyond comprehension. It looked at Bill and saw what he had done. It saw the destroyed dimension. It saw the madness creeping in. And it offered something extraordinary.\n\nA second chance.\n\n\"Saw what you did, know what you'll do. I offer now a path for you,\" the Axolotl's voice echoes through the memory. \"The choice is yours: create or rend. A second chance, not yet the end.\"\n\nThe Axolotl offered Bill redemption. A chance to use his newfound power to create rather than destroy. To build new dimensions instead of burning old ones. To transform his pain into something constructive.\n\nBill considered it. For one eternal moment, hanging in the void, he actually considered it. The fragment of him that still felt remorse reached toward the Axolotl's offer like a drowning man reaching for a rope.\n\nAnd then he laughed. That horrible, familiar laugh. He rejected the second chance. He chose chaos. He chose power. He chose to become the Bill Cipher they know - a trillion-year-old dream demon who treats the multiverse as his personal toy box.\n\nBut the fragment - that tiny piece of Bill that reached for redemption - didn't disappear. The Axolotl preserved it, held it in the space between dimensions like a seed saved from a burned forest. Somewhere in the vast chaos of Bill's existence, that fragment still exists. Small, quiet, buried under eons of madness and destruction, but alive.\n\n\"This changes things,\" Dipper says slowly. \"Bill can't truly be destroyed. He's too embedded in the dimensional structure - destroying him would leave a void in reality that might be worse than Bill himself. But that fragment... the piece of him the Axolotl saved...\"\n\n\"It means there's another way,\" Mabel finishes. Her eyes are bright. \"Not destroying Bill. Reaching the part of him that was once good.\"\n\nThe knowledge settles into them like a key finding its lock. They now understand something about Bill that Bill himself has forgotten - that he was once capable of choosing differently, and that choice still exists, preserved by the most patient being in existence.",
    choices: [
      { label: "Use this knowledge when meeting the Axolotl", target: "axolotl_meeting" },
      { label: "Try to reach out to the fragment of Bill that the Axolotl once saved", target: "axolotl_bill_fragment" }
    ]
  },
  axolotl_plea: {
    text: "Dipper and Mabel don't strategize. They don't plan what to say. They just... talk.\n\nMabel goes first, because Mabel has always been the one who leads with her heart. She tells the Axolotl about Waddles - her pig, her best non-human friend, who she won at the fair and who sleeps at the foot of her bed and who trusts her completely. She talks about Candy and Grenda, her friends who accepted her exactly as she is, glitter addiction and all. She talks about making sweaters - how each one is a piece of her soul, knitted into wool and joy, and how giving someone a sweater is her way of saying 'I love you and I made this love into something you can wear.'\n\nShe talks about Soos, who is the kindest person she's ever met, who would walk through fire for the Pines family and has, basically. About Wendy, who is everything cool and brave and real, who showed Mabel that being a teenager doesn't have to mean losing yourself. About Grunkle Stan, who is grumpy and scheming and dishonest and also the most loving person in the world, who built a portal to save his brother and kept a family together with duct tape and determination.\n\nDipper talks next. He tells the Axolotl about the mysteries - how Gravity Falls was the first place where his curiosity was rewarded instead of mocked. How finding Journal 3 wasn't just finding a book but finding himself. He talks about the creatures in the woods, the secrets underground, the way every answered question revealed ten new ones and how that was wonderful, not frustrating, because it meant the world was bigger and stranger and more magical than anyone imagined.\n\nHe talks about Ford, who showed him what a life of discovery could look like. About how Ford's mistakes - the portal, Bill, the isolation - taught Dipper that being smart isn't enough, that you also need people. He talks about his own growth: how he came to Gravity Falls as a scared kid who hid behind his journal and is leaving (if they survive this) as someone who faces things directly, who trusts others, who knows that bravery isn't the absence of fear but the decision that something else is more important.\n\nHe talks about Mabel. About how she drives him crazy and saves him constantly. About how her optimism isn't naive but radical - a choice to see the best in things when it would be so much easier to see the worst. About how being her twin is the single greatest fact of his existence.\n\nMabel talks about Dipper. About how his brain works like a supercomputer and his heart works like a compass, always pointing toward what's right. About how he pretends to be the logical one but is actually the most emotionally brave person she knows, because he feels everything deeply and keeps going anyway.\n\nThey talk about their parents, waiting back in Piedmont, who have no idea their children are in the space between dimensions negotiating with a cosmic salamander for the fate of reality. About their bedroom at home, divided down the middle with tape that neither of them respects. About the car ride to Gravity Falls at the beginning of summer, how Mabel was excited and Dipper was anxious and neither of them had any idea what was coming.\n\nThe Axolotl listens to every word. Its great eyes are soft. Its gills wave gently. It says nothing, but its silence is not emptiness - it's the silence of something that considers every syllable sacred.\n\nWhen they finish, there are tears on both their faces. The void around them feels warmer, charged with the truth of their words.\n\nThe Axolotl speaks.\n\n\"No tale I've heard in eon's span has moved me so since time began. Your love is real, your hearts are true, and so I'll share what I can do.\" It pauses, and its expression becomes grave. \"To mend your world, to heal the scar, to put each broken piece ajar - this power lives within my reach. But balance has a price to teach.\"\n\nThe pink light dims slightly. The galaxies inside the Axolotl slow their spinning.\n\n\"Would you give up your memories of this summer to undo what Bill has done?\"\n\nThe question hits them like a physical blow. Their memories. The entire summer. Every adventure, every discovery, every friendship forged and lesson learned. The journals. The monsters. The bonds. The growth. Everything that made them who they are now - gone, erased, as if the summer never happened.\n\nThe cost of saving the world is forgetting why it was worth saving.\n\nDipper and Mabel look at each other. The Axolotl waits with the patience of eternity.",
    choices: [
      { label: "Yes - give up the memories to save everyone", target: "ending_axolotl_reset_bittersweet" },
      { label: "No - there has to be another way", target: "axolotl_test" }
    ]
  },
  axolotl_demand: {
    text: "\"Destroy him,\" Dipper says.\n\nHis voice is hard, sharp-edged with anger that has been building all summer. Every nightmare Bill inflicted. Every manipulation. Every person turned to stone or gold or worse. Ford trapped. Stan endangered. Mabel imprisoned in a bubble of false happiness. The sky torn open. Reality shattered. Gravity Falls, their magical, wonderful, weird little town, turned into a playground for a trillion-year-old sadist.\n\n\"Destroy Bill Cipher,\" Dipper repeats. \"You have the power. He's the problem. Remove the problem and everything goes back to normal.\"\n\nMabel puts a hand on his arm. \"Dipper...\"\n\n\"No, Mabel. I'm tired of being scared of him. I'm tired of every plan being about surviving him or outsmarting him or running from him. He's a bully. The biggest bully in the universe. And when you have the power to stop a bully permanently, you STOP THEM.\"\n\nThe Axolotl's expression changes. The vast, gentle face shifts - still kind, always kind, but now carrying a sadness so deep it makes the void around them feel heavier. Its pink glow dims to something more muted, more contemplative. The galaxies inside its body slow their spinning, as if the universe itself is holding its breath.\n\n\"To destroy by force is Bill Cipher's way,\" the Axolotl says, its voice resonating through the space between dimensions like a bell made of starlight. \"Would you become what you seek to end today?\"\n\nThe words hit Dipper like cold water. He opens his mouth to argue, but the Axolotl continues.\n\n\"I understand your anger, child of pine. Your rage is just - this I do not decline. But hear me now, and hear me well: the tale of Bill is not mine to quell. Through force of will and cosmic might, yes, I could end him. Snuff his light. But what you ask holds consequence dire - to douse the demon, you'd spread the fire.\"\n\nThe Axolotl shows them. In the void, images form: what would happen if Bill were destroyed by force. It's not pretty. Bill Cipher, for all his chaos and evil, is woven into the fabric of reality. He's been part of the dimensional structure for trillions of years. Ripping him out would be like pulling a load-bearing wall from a building - the whole thing would come down.\n\n\"Bill is a cancer,\" the Axolotl explains, \"but he is a cancer that has grown into the bones. To cut it out entirely would kill the patient. Dimensions would collapse. Realities would fold. The void between worlds - my home - would fill with the debris of a thousand shattered universes. The cure would be worse than the disease.\"\n\n\"Then what's the point?\" Dipper shouts, his voice cracking. \"What's the point of being the most powerful being in existence if you won't DO anything?\"\n\n\"Dipper,\" Mabel says quietly, but he's past listening.\n\n\"You could stop this! You could fix everything! But you're just sitting here in the void, speaking in riddles, while our world BURNS!\"\n\nThe Axolotl absorbs his anger like an ocean absorbing a stone. No reaction, no flinch - just patient, infinite understanding.\n\n\"The angry heart sees only walls where doors exist,\" it says gently. \"To twist the cosmic frame by force leaves holes that will persist. I offered Bill a second chance - he chose the path of pain. To match his violence with my own would make his choice in vain. There IS a way, young Pine Tree - paths of patience, paths of grace. But not the path of destruction. Not in this time or place.\"\n\nDipper wants to keep arguing. He wants to rage at the cosmic injustice of it - that the one being powerful enough to simply END Bill Cipher won't do it. But somewhere deep inside, past the anger and the fear and the exhaustion, he knows the Axolotl is right. Fighting fire with fire just makes more fire. Hasn't that been the lesson of the entire summer? Ford tried to fight Bill with science and ended up trapped. Stan tried to fight Bill with trickery and ended up in danger. Violence and force are Bill's game, and you don't beat someone by playing their game better - you beat them by changing the game entirely.\n\nBut the realization comes too late.\n\n\"I am sorry, children,\" the Axolotl says, and its sorrow is the saddest thing either of them has ever felt. \"You came seeking destruction, and that I cannot provide. I must return you now to your world, to the path you've chosen.\"\n\nThe void brightens. The Axolotl's form recedes. The space between dimensions folds around them like gentle hands, and they're falling, falling, falling -\n\nThey land in the cave. The portal is closed. The crystals are dark. And standing at the cave entrance, blocking the only exit, his single eye burning with malicious glee, is Bill Cipher.\n\n\"BACK SO SOON?\" Bill's voice drips with mockery. He's smaller here than in the Fearamid - human-sized, which somehow makes him MORE unsettling. \"THE COSMIC SALAMANDER TURNED YOU DOWN, HUH? CAN'T SAY I'M SURPRISED. OLD PINKY NEVER DID HAVE THE GUTS TO DO WHAT NEEDS TO BE DONE.\"\n\nBill floats closer, his eye narrowing to a slit. \"DON'T WORRY, KIDS. I'LL MAKE SURE YOU NEVER BOTHER HIM AGAIN.\"\n\nHe snaps his fingers. Blue fire. Blinding light. A sensation of falling that doesn't end - falling through dimensions, through layers of reality, through the fabric of existence itself, tumbling into a place that is dark and cold and infinitely vast.\n\nThe Nightmare Realm. Not the Nightmare Realm Bill came from - that's been folded into Gravity Falls. This is somewhere deeper, darker. A place where fear takes physical form. Where your worst nightmares congeal into shapes that chase you through an endless darkness that has no walls, no ceiling, no floor, no horizon. Just darkness, and fear, and the sound of your own heartbeat reminding you that you're alive, that you can still feel, that the terror will never stop.\n\nDipper reaches for Mabel's hand in the dark. He finds it. They hold on.\n\nThey demanded destruction. They got destruction. Just not the kind they wanted.\n\nIn the endless dark, their fears take shape and begin to close in, and the twins run - together, always together - through a nightmare that has no end and no exit, because they chose anger when the universe was offering something gentler, and the universe, as it always does, gave them exactly what they asked for.",
    ending: true
  },
  axolotl_test: {
    text: "\"No.\"\n\nDipper says it first, but Mabel is right behind him.\n\n\"No way. Absolutely not. Hard pass. Negative. Nope-ity nope nope,\" Mabel adds, because Mabel has never said anything just once when she could say it five times with enthusiasm.\n\nThe Axolotl's great eyes blink slowly. Its expression doesn't change, but something in the quality of its pink glow shifts - warmer, brighter, as if their refusal has pleased it rather than disappointed it.\n\n\"These memories are everything,\" Dipper says, and his voice shakes with the intensity of what he's feeling. \"This summer changed us. It made us who we are. If you take those memories, you save the world but you destroy the people who fought to save it. Mabel and I won't be us anymore. We'll be the versions of us from BEFORE - before we learned to be brave, before we learned to trust each other completely, before we understood that the world is bigger and stranger and more wonderful than we ever imagined. You'd be saving our lives but erasing our selves.\"\n\n\"What he said,\" Mabel nods firmly. \"Also, I refuse to forget Waddles. I refuse to forget the day I won him. I refuse to forget the look on Pacifica's face when she realized she could be a good person. I refuse to forget Grunkle Stan's face when Ford came through the portal. Those memories aren't just OURS - they belong to everyone who was part of them.\"\n\nThe Axolotl is quiet for a long moment. Then, incredibly, it smiles. The cosmic salamander's face breaks into an expression of pure, radiant approval that lights up the void between dimensions like a sunrise in a place that has never known sun.\n\n\"You've passed the first test without knowing it was one,\" the Axolotl says, and its voice hums with something that might be pride. \"The sacrifice of memory - I offered it to see if you would take the easy path. The path that costs you everything that matters while pretending to cost nothing at all. You refused. This is wisdom.\"\n\nMabel's jaw drops. \"That was a TEST? The whole 'give up your memories' thing was a fake-out?\"\n\n\"Not fake. Real. Had you accepted, I would have honored the bargain. But the cost would have been greater than you knew - for memories are the mortar of the soul. Without them, the wall still stands but crumbles at the first wind.\" The Axolotl's gills wave contemplatively. \"You chose the harder path. The braver path. And so I offer you the true test.\"\n\nThe void around them shifts. The formless potential takes on shape, structure, dimension. The space between realities reorganizes itself into something that looks almost like a room - a vast, circular chamber made of starlight and memory, with the Axolotl coiled around its edges like a cosmic frame.\n\n\"The Test of the Twin Stars,\" the Axolotl announces. \"Both must face what they fear most - not monsters, not demons, not the dark. The fears that live inside, that leave their mark.\"\n\nThe chamber splits. Not physically - it's more like the space develops two focal points, two centers of gravity. In one, Dipper sees his deepest fears taking shape: the terror that he's not smart enough, that his intelligence is all he has and it's not enough. The fear that he'll always be second - second to Mabel's social ease, second to Ford's genius, second to everyone. The crushing anxiety that without the mysteries, without the journals, without the role of 'the smart one,' he is nothing. Just a scared kid with a birthmark and a hat.\n\nIn the other focal point, Mabel sees hers: the fear that growing up means becoming serious, means losing the color and joy and silliness that define her. The fear that her optimism is a burden to others - that people tolerate her enthusiasm rather than appreciate it. The dread that she's not taken seriously, that everyone sees her as the funny one, the silly one, the one who makes sweaters while Dipper does the real work.\n\nThese fears aren't abstract. They take form. They become scenes, memories twisted into nightmares, voices that whisper the worst things they've ever thought about themselves.\n\n\"Face these fears,\" the Axolotl says gently. \"This is my test. Not of strength or cleverness, but of heart. Face what you fear about yourselves, and in the facing, find the truth.\"\n\nDipper looks at Mabel across the chamber. Mabel looks at Dipper. Their fears swirl around them like storms, intensely personal, deeply painful.\n\n\"There are two ways to face a storm,\" the Axolotl adds, its voice soft as starlight. \"Together, holding fast. Or alone, proving your own strength. Both are valid. Both have consequences. Choose.\"\n\nThe Test of the Twin Stars. Their deepest fears, made manifest. Together or alone - the choice will determine everything.",
    choices: [
      { label: "Face the fears together, supporting each other", target: "ending_axolotl_reset_good" },
      { label: "Try to face the fears alone to prove individual strength", target: "ending_axolotl_timeloop" }
    ]
  },
  axolotl_bill_fragment: {
    text: "Mabel makes the decision before Dipper can finish weighing the pros and cons.\n\n\"We're going to talk to him,\" she says, already moving toward the echo of Bill's past. \"The little triangle. The one the Axolotl saved. If there's a piece of Bill that's still good, we have to try.\"\n\n\"Mabel, this is BILL CIPHER we're talking about. Even a fragment of him could be dangerous.\"\n\n\"Or it could be the answer. Dipper, we just saw his whole history. He wasn't always like this. He was curious and lonely and scared, and he made terrible choices because no one helped him make better ones. What if someone had?\" Her eyes are fierce. \"What if someone does?\"\n\nDipper wants to argue. Every logical part of his brain is screaming that this is insane, that no part of Bill Cipher should be trusted, that reaching out to a fragment of the most dangerous being in the multiverse is the definition of a bad idea. But he also remembers what the echoes showed them: a small triangle reaching for the Axolotl's offer of redemption before fear and anger pulled him back. And he remembers something Ford once said: 'The most dangerous thing in any dimension isn't power. It's loneliness.'\n\n\"Fine,\" Dipper says. \"But if the fragment tries anything - ANYTHING - we bail immediately.\"\n\n\"Deal,\" Mabel agrees, and they dive deeper into the echoes.\n\nFinding the fragment is like searching for a whisper in a hurricane. Bill's presence in the between-space is enormous and chaotic - eons of destruction and madness have left marks everywhere, screaming echoes of maniacal laughter and cosmic vandalism. The fragment, by contrast, is tiny. A splinter of who Bill was before he chose to become what he is now.\n\nThey find it in the quietest corner of the void - or rather, it finds them. Drawn by their intention, attracted by the fact that someone is actually looking for it, the fragment materializes slowly, hesitantly, like a scared animal emerging from hiding.\n\nIt's a triangle. Small - maybe a foot tall, barely larger than a slice of pizza. Yellow, but a muted yellow, not the blazing gold of Bill Cipher. No top hat. No bowtie. No cane. No black limbs or glowing bricks. Just a simple, flat triangle with a single eye.\n\nAnd that eye looks tired. Unspeakably, incomprehensibly tired. This is a fragment of consciousness that has been buried under trillions of years of madness, hidden away in the space between dimensions by an Axolotl who refused to give up on it, waiting in silence and darkness for someone - anyone - to come looking.\n\n\"Hi,\" Mabel says softly. She crouches down to the fragment's level, the way you'd approach a lost kitten. \"I'm Mabel. This is my brother Dipper. We're... well, we're kind of trying to save the world from you. From the other you. The big scary you.\"\n\nThe fragment blinks. Its eye is not menacing - it's confused, like someone waking from a very long sleep.\n\n\"You can hear us, right?\" Dipper asks cautiously. \"Do you... do you know who you are?\"\n\nThe fragment doesn't speak. Not like Bill speaks, with the all-caps shouting and the theatrical flair. Instead, it projects a feeling: confusion, loneliness, a dim awareness that it was once part of something larger, something that chose to go in a direction this fragment couldn't follow. It's like talking to an echo of a person - not the person themselves, but the memory of who they were before everything went wrong.\n\n\"You're the part that wanted to say yes,\" Mabel says, understanding flooding her voice. \"When the Axolotl offered a second chance, you wanted to take it. But the rest of Bill said no. And you've been here ever since.\"\n\nThe fragment pulses faintly. Affirmation.\n\nMabel sits down cross-legged in the void, facing the fragment. She reaches into her sweater pocket and pulls out a ball of yarn she's been carrying since the beginning of their journey. Absent-mindedly, she starts knitting. It's what she does when she's thinking, when she's processing, when she's trying to make something good out of raw materials.\n\n\"You know what's funny?\" she says conversationally, as if she's not sitting in the void between dimensions talking to a fragment of the most dangerous being in existence. \"Bill - the big one - he's always going on about how feelings are weakness. How caring about people makes you vulnerable. How being alone and powerful is better than being connected and limited. But look at him now. He's got all the power in the universe and he's still not happy. He's still performing. Still needing an audience. Still desperate for attention. He destroyed his whole dimension because he was lonely, and becoming the most powerful being in existence didn't fix the loneliness - it just made it infinite.\"\n\nThe fragment's eye widens slightly. It's listening.\n\n\"You felt that,\" Mabel continues. \"You knew it wasn't working. That's why the Axolotl saved you - because you're the part of Bill that understood. The part that knew power wasn't the answer.\"\n\nDipper watches his sister work. She's doing what she does best - reaching out, connecting, finding the humanity (or in this case, the fundamental decency) in someone everyone else has written off. It's her superpower. It always has been.",
    choices: [
      { label: "Try to help the Bill fragment remember who it was", target: "ending_axolotl_reformed" },
      { label: "The Bill fragment is too dangerous to trust", target: "axolotl_meeting" }
    ]
  },
  axolotl_summoning: {
    text: "\"DON'T STOP!\" Dipper screams, and they don't stop.\n\nThe ancient words pour from their mouths in unison, twin voices rising above the chaos. The ritual circle blazes beneath their feet, the Zodiac symbols burning with colors that have no names, pulsing in rhythm with their chanting. The cave walls shake. Stalactites crash down, shattering on the cave floor inches from where they stand, but the ritual circle is a haven - inside it, the ground is steady, the air is still, the ancient magic holding firm against Bill's assault.\n\nOutside the circle, the world is ending even harder.\n\nEye-bats swarm through the cave entrance, their shrieks bouncing off the stone walls in a deafening cacophony. They dive at the twins but can't penetrate the ritual circle - they bounce off an invisible barrier and crash into walls, dazed and furious. Bill's weirdness waves wash over the cave, transforming everything they touch: stalactites become tentacles, the cave floor outside the circle turns to liquid, bats flying overhead become inside-out versions of themselves. But inside the circle, inside the ancient magic, the twins are protected.\n\nBill himself arrives.\n\nNot a projection, not an echo - Bill Cipher in his full, terrifying glory, squeezing his triangular form through the cave entrance, which distorts and stretches to accommodate him. His single eye is BLAZING, not with amusement or theatrical menace but with raw, primal FEAR. The Axolotl is responding to the ritual. Bill can feel it. The one thing in all of existence that makes him afraid is reaching into his domain, and two twelve-year-olds are the ones who invited it.\n\n\"STOP THIS RIGHT NOW!\" Bill roars. The cave shakes so hard that rocks fall from the ceiling. \"DO YOU HAVE ANY IDEA WHAT YOU'RE DOING?! THAT THING ISN'T YOUR FRIEND! IT'S NOT ANYONE'S FRIEND! IT JUST SITS THERE IN THE VOID, JUDGING EVERYONE, THINKING IT'S BETTER THAN -\"\n\n\"Keep chanting!\" Dipper shouts to Mabel, and she nods, her voice steady and clear.\n\nBill's flames turn blue, then white, then a searing ultraviolet. He throws everything he has at the ritual circle. Blue fire crashes against the invisible barrier. Reality-warping energy splashes off it like water off glass. He tries to grab them with his hands, his limbs extending and stretching, but the barrier holds. The ancient magic is older than Bill. It was here before him. It was designed specifically to resist him.\n\n\"PINE TREE! SHOOTING STAR! I'M WARNING YOU!\" Bill's voice reaches a pitch that's almost a scream. \"IF YOU BRING THAT THING HERE, THERE'S NO GOING BACK! FOR ANY OF US!\"\n\nThe ritual reaches its crescendo. The twins speak the final words together - 'A-X-O-L-O-T-L' - and each letter rings through the cave like a bell struck by the hand of the universe. A beam of pink light erupts from the center of the ritual circle, shooting straight up through the cave ceiling as if the rock isn't there, piercing through the fractured sky of Weirdmageddon, reaching up and up and up until it touches something beyond the sky entirely.\n\nThe Axolotl responds.\n\nIts presence fills the cave - not its body, which is too vast to fit in any single dimension, but its AWARENESS. Its consciousness. Its attention. The air in the cave suddenly feels thicker, richer, charged with a calm so profound it makes every nerve in their bodies relax. The pink light settles into a warm glow that permeates everything - the walls, the floor, the air itself. The ancient paintings on the walls seem to come alive, the prehistoric figures dancing and celebrating, the painted Axolotl moving, breathing, its painted eyes opening.\n\nBill Cipher recoils. Actually physically recoils, stumbling backward, his single eye wide with something neither twin has ever seen on his face: genuine terror.\n\n\"No,\" Bill says, and for the first time his voice is small. Not loud, not theatrical, not ALL CAPS. Just... small. \"No, no, no. Not you. Not again.\"\n\nThe eye-bats flee. They pour out of the cave entrance like water from a broken glass, screeching in panic, unable to tolerate the Axolotl's presence. Bill's weirdness effects start to reverse - the transformed stalactites turn back to stone, the liquid floor solidifies, the reality-warps smooth out like wrinkles being ironed from fabric.\n\n\"YOU,\" Bill hisses at the pink light, at the presence that fills the cave. \"YOU ALWAYS RUIN EVERYTHING. YOU AND YOUR PATIENCE AND YOUR STUPID SECOND CHANCES AND YOUR -\"\n\nThe Axolotl's presence pulses once, gently, and Bill goes silent. Not forced silent, not magically muzzled - just... hushed. Like a child who's been throwing a tantrum and suddenly realized an adult is watching.\n\nThe cave is quiet. The pink light glows. Bill floats in the entrance, suddenly very small, very alone, and very, very afraid.\n\nThe Axolotl's awareness turns to the twins, warm and vast and infinitely patient. It speaks, not in sound but in feeling: Come. I am here. Tell me what you need.\n\nBut Bill is here too. Right here, in the cave, weakened by the Axolotl's presence but not gone. And the Axolotl's power is flowing through the twins - they can feel it, cosmic energy humming in their veins like music.",
    choices: [
      { label: "Hold the ritual circle while the Axolotl manifests", target: "axolotl_meeting" },
      { label: "Bill is here - confront him while the Axolotl's power flows", target: "ending_axolotl_confrontation" }
    ]
  },
  ending_axolotl_reset_good: {
    text: "They reach for each other at the same moment.\n\nIt's instinct - twelve years of being twins, twelve years of shared birthday cakes and bunk beds and arguments about who gets the window seat and silent understandings that don't need words. When the test begins and their fears rise like tsunamis on either side, Dipper and Mabel don't hesitate. They grab each other's hands and hold on.\n\nThe fears crash against them. Together.\n\nDipper's fear comes first: a version of himself stripped of everything that makes him feel valuable. No journal. No mysteries. No hat. Just Mason 'Dipper' Pines, standing in a classroom back in Piedmont where no one thinks he's special, where his hand-raising and his overthinking and his desperate need to be the smartest person in the room aren't admirable but annoying. A teacher sighs when he asks another question. Classmates roll their eyes. A test comes back and someone else scored higher, and without being the smart one, who is he? Nothing. Nobody. A scared kid with a weird birthmark hiding under a hat.\n\nThe fear wraps around him like ice water. But Mabel is there, holding his hand, and she speaks directly into the nightmare.\n\n\"You're not nothing,\" she says, and her voice cuts through the fear like sunlight through fog. \"You're my brother. You're the bravest person I know - not because you're not scared, but because you're ALWAYS scared and you do stuff anyway. You don't need to be the smartest person in the room, Dipper. You just need to be you. The kid who finds journals in the woods and believes in impossible things and never, ever gives up. THAT'S who you are. With or without mysteries.\"\n\nThe classroom nightmare wavers. Cracks appear in it. Through the cracks, light shines.\n\nMabel's fear comes next: a version of herself that the world has sanded down. Older, serious, wearing gray clothes, no sweaters, no glitter, no color. An adult Mabel who was told so many times that her enthusiasm was 'too much' that she finally believed it. A Mabel who stopped knitting, stopped creating, stopped seeing the best in people because the world convinced her that optimism was childish. She sits alone in a gray office at a gray desk, and nothing sparkles, and nothing is fun, and the girl who once bedazzled everything she touched has become invisible.\n\nThe fear wraps around her like a leaden blanket. But Dipper is there, holding her hand, and he speaks into her nightmare with fierce conviction.\n\n\"Growing up doesn't mean becoming boring,\" he says. \"It doesn't mean losing yourself. Mabel, your optimism isn't childish - it's the strongest thing about you. You looked at Bill Cipher, an all-powerful dream demon, and your first instinct was to hit him with a kitten fist. You befriended a multi-bear. You punched a unicorn. Your joy isn't weakness - it's a WEAPON. And anyone who tells you to be less yourself is wrong. They're just wrong.\"\n\nThe gray office nightmare shatters. Color floods in. Sweaters and glitter and shooting stars and the irrepressible Mabel-ness that no amount of growing up can ever truly extinguish.\n\nThey stand together in the wreckage of their fears, hand in hand, and something happens that neither of them expects. Their joined hands begin to glow. Not with the Axolotl's pink light, but with their own light - warm and golden and twin-bright, the combined luminance of two people who know each other completely, who see each other's worst fears and love each other anyway, who are stronger together than they could ever be apart.\n\nThe light expands. It fills the test chamber. It fills the space between dimensions. It fills the void with warmth and connection and the simple, radical power of two kids who love their family and their friends and each other, and who refused to let fear define them.\n\nThe Axolotl smiles.\n\nNot a human smile - something bigger, something cosmic, something that rearranges the very fabric of the between-space into an expression of pure, profound joy. Its great eyes shine with tears made of starlight. Its pink glow brightens until the entire void is suffused with gentle, healing radiance.\n\n\"You have shown what Bill never could,\" the Axolotl says, its voice resonating with the weight of a truth that echoes across every dimension in existence. \"The strength of connection. The power of love without condition. Not love that bargains. Not love that demands. Love that holds on. Love that sees. Love that stays.\"\n\nThe Axolotl uncoils. Its vast body moves through the between-space with the grace of a river flowing uphill - impossible and beautiful. It reaches toward the twins with awareness and intent and a power so immense that the void itself trembles.\n\n\"I undo what was done. I heal what was torn. The world reborn, as on the morn.\"\n\nReality shifts.\n\nNot violently, not painfully, but like waking from a dream - gently, gradually, then all at once. The fractured sky of Gravity Falls heals, cracks sealing themselves like wounds closing. The Fearamid dissolves - not destroyed but unmade, its substance returning to wherever it came from, Bill's throne of chaos disassembling itself piece by piece. Time unknots. Gravity remembers which direction it's supposed to go. The sun rises - the real sun, not the bleeding red orb of Weirdmageddon, but a warm, golden, beautiful Pacific Northwest summer sunrise.\n\nThe eye-bats vanish. The weirdness waves recede. The lake un-turns from whatever horrifying substance it had become and goes back to being water. Trees right themselves. Buildings settle onto their foundations. The people who were turned to stone, to gold, to furniture, to decorations for Bill's throne - they change back. Flesh and blood and beating hearts and confused expressions as they look around at a world that's suddenly, impossibly, normal again.\n\nBill Cipher screams. Not in anger - in dissolution. The Axolotl doesn't destroy him; it CONTAINS him. Wraps him in layers of cosmic patience, folding him into the space between dimensions where he can rage and scheme and laugh all he wants without hurting anyone. It's not a permanent solution - the Axolotl is clear about that. Bill will always exist. But he'll exist somewhere where his chaos can't reach anyone, bound by the same cosmic balance that keeps the dimensions spinning.\n\nAnd the twins - the twins wake up.\n\nThey're lying in the attic of the Mystery Shack. Morning light streams through the windows - real, warm, honest sunlight. Waddles is asleep at the foot of Mabel's bed, snoring softly. Dipper's hat is on the bedpost. Mabel's sweater collection is draped over every available surface. The room smells like pine and old wood and home.\n\nDipper sits up. Mabel sits up. They look at each other.\n\nThey remember EVERYTHING.\n\nThe Axolotl's final gift. Not just the restoration of their world but the preservation of their memories - every adventure, every friendship, every lesson, every scare, every laugh, every moment of the summer that changed them forever. They remember the journals and the monsters and the Axolotl's vast, kind eyes. They remember holding hands in the void. They remember facing their fears and choosing each other.\n\n\"Did that actually happen?\" Mabel whispers.\n\n\"Yeah,\" Dipper says, and then he's hugging her, and she's hugging him, and Waddles wakes up and oinks in confusion but joins the hug anyway because he's a good pig.\n\nDownstairs, Grunkle Stan is making pancakes. They can hear him arguing with the stove (\"Work, you worthless piece of - there we go\") and humming off-key. Ford is at the kitchen table, already writing in a new journal, documenting everything he remembers about Weirdmageddon with the urgency of a scientist who knows how important the record is.\n\nSoos arrives at nine sharp, wearing his question mark t-shirt, whistling the same tune he whistles every morning. \"Hey dudes! Beautiful day, right? I feel like something really good happened but I can't figure out what. It's like the world took a really good nap.\"\n\nWendy shows up on her bike, flannel shirt open to the summer breeze, and she takes one look at Dipper and Mabel and KNOWS. She can see it in their faces. She doesn't say anything - she just nods, a slow, respectful nod, and that's enough.\n\nThe town of Gravity Falls goes about its day. Lazy Susan serves coffee at the diner. Tyler Cutebiker rides past on his bicycle singing loudly. Old Man McGucket tinkers in his junkyard, building something wonderful and probably dangerous. The gnomes cause minor trouble in the forest. The Manotaurs are arm-wrestling somewhere on a mountain. The Multi-Bear is listening to his favorite pop songs in his cave.\n\nEveryone remembers Weirdmageddon as a bad dream - vivid and terrifying but fading at the edges, the way dreams do. Only the twins remember it fully. Only they know what really happened: that two twelve-year-olds traveled to the space between dimensions, met a cosmic salamander made of starlight and patience, faced their deepest fears, and saved the world with nothing but honesty and the unbreakable bond between a brother and a sister.\n\nThat evening, as the sun sets over Gravity Falls and paints the sky in oranges and pinks and golds, Dipper and Mabel sit on the roof of the Mystery Shack. Waddles is between them. The journal is in Dipper's lap. Mabel is knitting a new sweater - pink, with an axolotl on it. A real axolotl this time, not a dog.\n\n\"Best summer ever?\" Mabel asks.\n\n\"Best summer ever,\" Dipper confirms.\n\nSomewhere, in the space between dimensions, the Axolotl smiles. The world turns. The stars come out. And in a little town in Oregon where nothing is normal and everything is wonderful, two kids watch the sky and know, with absolute certainty, that whatever comes next, they'll face it together.\n\nThe summer isn't over yet. There are still mysteries to solve, friends to hang out with, and sweaters to knit. But for now, this moment is perfect. And they'll remember it forever.",
    ending: true
  },
  ending_axolotl_reformed: {
    text: "Mabel begins doing what Mabel does best: she talks to the fragment like it's a person. Because it is. Or it was. Or it could be again.\n\n\"Tell me about your world,\" she says to the tiny, tired triangle. \"The flat one. Before everything went wrong. What was it like?\"\n\nThe fragment doesn't speak in words. It projects feelings, images, impressions - fragmented and faded like a photograph left in the sun for a billion years. But Mabel is patient (when she wants to be), and Dipper is attentive (always), and slowly, piece by piece, the fragment shares.\n\nIt shares what it was like to be a shape in a flat world. The geometric beauty of it - patterns interlocking perfectly, angles meeting with mathematical precision, a world where everything fit together like a puzzle. It was limited, yes, but it was home. There were other shapes the fragment cared about - friends, if triangles can have friends. Shapes that were kind to it when others weren't. Shapes that listened when it talked about its dreams of higher dimensions.\n\nThose shapes burned with everything else.\n\nThe fragment projects grief so old and so deep that Mabel's eyes fill with tears. This is the part of Bill that mourned. The part that looked at the ashes of its home and felt horror instead of liberation. The part that wanted to say 'I'm sorry' but was drowned out by the part that screamed 'I'm FREE.'\n\n\"I'm sorry that happened to you,\" Mabel says, and she means it completely, without reservation, without irony. Mabel Pines is sorry that a fragment of the most evil being in the multiverse once had to watch its home burn. Because Mabel's compassion doesn't have conditions. That's what makes it powerful.\n\nThe fragment pulses. Brighter. Warmer. Mabel's kindness is feeding it, nourishing a part of Bill that has been starving in the dark for trillions of years.\n\nDipper steps in, approaching carefully. \"You wanted to take the Axolotl's deal,\" he says. \"You wanted the second chance. To create instead of destroy. What would you have created?\"\n\nAnother projection, brighter this time: a dimension. Not the flat world restored, but something new - a world of three dimensions, full of color and life and variety. It's beautiful. It's the world Bill COULD have made if he'd chosen differently.\n\n\"You can still make that choice,\" Mabel says. \"It's not too late.\"\n\nMabel kneels down and hugs the fragment. She wraps her arms around the tiny tired triangle and hugs it the way she hugs Waddles, the way she hugs Grunkle Stan - completely, without reservation, as if her arms could shield it from the entire hostile universe.\n\nThe fragment glows. Not Bill's menacing gold, but something warmer, softer. A gentle yellow, like morning light. Mabel's compassion and Dipper's understanding are doing what cosmic power couldn't: they're making the fragment strong enough to choose.\n\nThe fragment chooses.\n\nIt reaches out toward the vast, chaotic presence of Bill Cipher that permeates the dimensional structure. It touches the madness. And it speaks, for the first time in trillions of years, in a voice that is small and tired and absolutely certain:\n\n\"I want to go home. And this isn't home. Chaos isn't home.\"\n\nBill feels it. Back in Gravity Falls, in the Fearamid, the master of Weirdmageddon staggers. Something is happening inside him - something he can't control, can't manipulate. A part of himself he forgot existed is waking up and it's DISAGREEING with him.\n\nFor one breathless moment, the twins think it's working. The Fearamid cracks. The sky flickers. Bill's power wavers.\n\nThen Bill laughs.\n\nNot the panicked laugh of something losing control. The deep, delighted laugh of something that has just been handed exactly what it wanted.\n\n\"OH, KID,\" Bill says, and his voice fills the between-space like flood water. \"OH, THAT'S ADORABLE. YOU REALLY THOUGHT A HUG WOULD FIX A TRILLION-YEAR-OLD CHAOS ENTITY? SHOOTING STAR, I LOVE YOUR OPTIMISM. I REALLY DO. IT'S LIKE WATCHING A GOLDFISH TRY TO FIGHT A HURRICANE.\"\n\nThe fragment screams. Not in rebellion - in pain. Bill's consciousness crashes down on it like a tidal wave, drowning the tiny voice of conscience in an ocean of madness. The gentle yellow light flickers, sputters, and goes out. The fragment doesn't die - it's absorbed. Pulled back into the whole, the brief moment of individuation crushed flat by the sheer weight of everything Bill is.\n\n\"THANKS FOR WAKING THAT UP, BY THE WAY,\" Bill continues, his voice dripping with amusement. \"IT'S BEEN SLEEPING FOR EONS AND I'D HONESTLY FORGOTTEN IT WAS THERE. NOW THAT I KNOW, I CAN MAKE SURE IT NEVER WAKES UP AGAIN. SO REALLY, YOU DID ME A FAVOR.\"\n\nBut that's not the worst part. The worst part is what happens next.\n\nWhen the fragment reached out to Bill, it created a connection. A bridge between the between-space and Bill's domain. A bridge that Mabel's compassion built and Dipper's understanding reinforced. A bridge that Bill can now walk across.\n\n\"AND LOOK AT THAT,\" Bill says, his eye blazing with golden fire. \"YOU LEFT THE DOOR OPEN.\"\n\nBill's power surges through the connection, flooding the between-space. The Axolotl recoils - not in fear, but in sorrow. It knows what's happening. The children, in their kindness, in their beautiful, foolish, heartbreaking kindness, have given Bill exactly what he needed: a pathway into the one dimension he couldn't reach on his own.\n\n\"HELLO, AXOLOTL,\" Bill says, and for the first time in the cosmic salamander's eternal existence, something hostile is standing in its home. \"LONG TIME NO SEE. LOVE WHAT YOU'VE DONE WITH THE PLACE. VERY... PINK.\"\n\nThe Axolotl fights. Its power is vast, ancient, deeper than Bill's chaos. But Bill has something the Axolotl doesn't: he's willing to destroy. The Axolotl creates; Bill unmakes. And in the between-space, where reality is soft and malleable, unmake is stronger than creation.\n\nThe twins are caught in the middle. The between-space warps around them, Bill's chaos and the Axolotl's order tearing at each other like two storms colliding. Dipper grabs Mabel's hand. Mabel grabs his. They hold on.\n\n\"This is my fault,\" Mabel whispers. \"I thought I could save him. I thought compassion would be enough.\"\n\n\"It SHOULD have been enough,\" Dipper says fiercely. \"In any fair universe, it would have been enough.\"\n\n\"NEWSFLASH, PINE TREE,\" Bill roars over the cosmic din. \"THE UNIVERSE ISN'T FAIR. IT NEVER WAS. THAT'S WHAT MAKES IT FUN.\"\n\nThe Axolotl speaks one last time, its voice fading as Bill's power pushes it back, further and further into the recesses of the between-space. \"The kindness was real. The choice was real. The fragment wanted to be saved. But wanting is not enough against a trillion years of madness. Some fires are too large for even the purest water to extinguish.\"\n\nPink light dims. Gold light blazes. The between-space transforms around the twins, shifting from the Axolotl's gentle warmth to Bill's chaotic carnival. The last thing they see is the Axolotl's great eyes, filled with sorrow and apology, closing as it retreats into dimensions Bill cannot follow.\n\nThey're alone with Bill.\n\nHe shrinks down to his normal size, floating in front of them with his hands behind his back, his eye half-lidded with satisfaction. The between-space around them has become a golden cage, a pocket dimension decorated in Bill's aesthetic: floating geometric shapes, colors that shouldn't exist, and a floor made of something that might be solidified nightmares.\n\n\"SO,\" Bill says conversationally, spinning his cane. \"HERE'S WHAT'S GOING TO HAPPEN. YOU TWO ARE GOING TO STAY RIGHT HERE, IN MY BRAND-NEW POCKET DIMENSION, AND YOU'RE GOING TO KEEP ME COMPANY. FOREVER. BECAUSE HERE'S THE THING ABOUT COMPASSION, SHOOTING STAR - IT MADE YOU PREDICTABLE. I KNEW EXACTLY WHAT YOU'D DO THE MOMENT YOU SAW THAT LITTLE PIECE OF ME CRYING IN THE CORNER. YOU'D TRY TO HELP. YOU'D OPEN YOUR HEART. AND WHEN YOU OPENED YOUR HEART, YOU OPENED THE DOOR.\"\n\nMabel doesn't cry. She stares at Bill with an expression that holds no hatred, no anger - just a deep, aching sadness. Not for herself. For the fragment. For the tiny part of Bill that really did want to be saved, that really did want to go home, that really did choose differently - and was swallowed whole by the monster it was trapped inside.\n\n\"He was real,\" she says quietly. \"The part of you that wanted to change. He was REAL.\"\n\n\"SURE HE WAS,\" Bill agrees. \"AND NOW HE'S GONE. THAT'S WHAT HAPPENS TO REAL THINGS IN MY WORLD, SHOOTING STAR. THEY GET USED UP.\"\n\nThe golden cage closes. The twins sit together in Bill's pocket dimension, holding hands, surrounded by chaos and geometry and the distant sound of Bill's laughter. Somewhere, impossibly far away, the Axolotl mourns. And in the space where the fragment briefly lived - the tiny, tired triangle that wanted to go home - there is nothing. Just gold. Just Bill. Just the echo of a choice that was brave and kind and completely, devastatingly insufficient.\n\nMabel's compassion was real. The fragment's desire for redemption was real. But Bill Cipher is a trillion-year-old nightmare, and some nightmares are too old and too deep and too fundamentally broken for even the purest heart to heal. The universe doesn't reward kindness. It just watches.",
    ending: true
  },
  ending_axolotl_reset_bittersweet: {
    text: "\"Yes,\" Dipper says, and the word costs him more than anything he's ever said.\n\nMabel nods beside him, tears streaming freely down her face. She can't speak. She just nods.\n\nThe Axolotl's great eyes close for a moment, and when they open, they're filled with something that looks like sorrow and respect intertwined. \"The bravest choice is often the one that breaks the heart. To give what's precious for another's sake - this is where courage starts.\"\n\nDipper squeezes Mabel's hand. \"We have to,\" he says, as much to himself as to her. \"Everyone back home - Stan, Ford, Soos, Wendy, Mom, Dad, the whole town - they're counting on us. Even if they don't know it. Even if they'll never know it.\"\n\n\"I know,\" Mabel whispers. \"I just... I want to remember it all one more time. Before it goes.\"\n\nSo they remember. Together, floating in the void before the cosmic salamander, they run through the summer in their minds. The bus to Gravity Falls. The Mystery Shack. Finding Journal 3 in the woods. The gnomes. The lake monster. Every weird, wonderful, terrifying discovery. Pacifica's redemption at the mansion. Stan's secret. The portal. Ford stepping through. Bill's manipulations. The rift. Weirdmageddon.\n\nEvery moment. Every laugh. Every scare. Every lesson learned and bond forged.\n\nThey hold the memories like cupped water, knowing they're about to open their hands.\n\n\"We're ready,\" Dipper says.\n\n\"Brave hearts, brave stars,\" the Axolotl murmurs. \"In letting go, you'll heal the scars.\"\n\nThe pink light intensifies. The Axolotl's body unfurls, extending through the between-space, reaching into every dimension, every timeline, touching the fracture points where Bill's Weirdmageddon tore reality apart. Its power flows like water filling cracks in dry earth. The damage begins to reverse.\n\nAnd the memories begin to go.\n\nIt's not painful. That's almost the worst part. It's more like fog rolling in. The edges soften first. Dipper feels the details of their adventures blurring, the specific words of conversations fading. Mabel clings to the memories harder. She recites them like a list: \"Waddles. I won Waddles at the fair. Grappling hook. Sweater Town is where I go when I'm sad. Dipper's real name is -\" She stops. She can't remember Dipper's real name. It's gone, dissolved into the pink light like sugar in water.\n\nThe memories cascade away. The journal - what journal? There was a town. A summer. A feeling of adventure. There was a pig? There were friends. The best friends.\n\nThere was each other. Always each other.\n\nThen it's gone. All of it.\n\nBut something else is gone too. Something the Axolotl didn't mention. Something it maybe didn't know.\n\nThe bond. The twin bond. The invisible thread that connected Dipper and Mabel Pines since before they were born, the thing that made them finish each other's sentences and know when the other was scared and reach for each other's hands in the dark without looking. The summer in Gravity Falls didn't create that bond - but it strengthened it, reinforced it, turned it from a childhood connection into something unbreakable. And now the summer is gone, and the bond...\n\nThe bond is thin. So thin.\n\nMabel Pines wakes up in her bed in Piedmont, California, on a warm June morning. Her mother is calling from downstairs.\n\n\"Kids! You need to start packing! The bus to your great uncle's place leaves tomorrow!\"\n\nMabel looks across the room at her brother. He's reading a book. She feels like she should say something to him, but she doesn't know what. He feels like a stranger wearing a familiar face. She used to know him better than anyone in the world. She's sure of that. But the specifics are gone, and without specifics, \"knowing someone\" is just a vague feeling that could mean anything.\n\n\"Where are we going again?\" Dipper asks, not looking up from his book.\n\n\"Some town in Oregon. Gravity Falls.\"\n\n\"Sounds boring.\"\n\n\"Yeah,\" Mabel says. She doesn't add anything. She used to be the kind of person who would find something exciting about any situation, who would spin boring into adventure with sheer force of optimism. But that Mabel was built by a summer she doesn't remember, and this Mabel is just... a twelve-year-old girl who doesn't want to spend three months in the middle of nowhere.\n\n\"I don't want to go,\" she says.\n\n\"Me neither,\" Dipper says.\n\nTheir mother insists. The bus leaves the next morning. They sit on opposite sides, not together. Mabel stares out the window. Dipper reads. They don't talk. There's nothing to talk about. They're siblings, sure, but siblings who've drifted into the quiet distance that sometimes happens when kids hit twelve - when being a twin stops feeling like a superpower and starts feeling like a label.\n\nThe bus pulls into Gravity Falls. A sign reads: WELCOME TO GRAVITY FALLS. Below it, someone has graffitied a small triangle with one eye. Neither twin notices.\n\nStan is waiting on the porch. He looks at them and something happens in his face - a cascade of emotions that starts with hope and ends with something that looks like his heart breaking in slow motion. Because Stan REMEMBERS. The Axolotl erased the twins' memories, but it didn't erase everyone's. Stan remembers the summer. He remembers the kids who saved the world. And the kids standing in front of him right now are not those kids.\n\n\"Welcome to the Mystery Shack,\" Stan says, and his voice cracks on \"shack.\" He reaches out to ruffle Dipper's hat - but Dipper isn't wearing a hat. He doesn't wear hats anymore. That was a different Dipper.\n\nMabel doesn't run up and hug him. She gives a small, polite wave. \"Hi, Great Uncle Stan.\"\n\n\"It's Grunkle Stan,\" he says automatically, and watches as the words mean nothing to her.\n\nFord finds Stan that night on the porch, staring at nothing. \"They don't remember,\" Stan says. \"Not any of it. Not the journal, not Bill, not... any of us. Ford, they don't even remember each other. Not really. They sat on opposite sides of the bus.\"\n\nFord is quiet for a long time. \"The Axolotl took too much,\" he says finally. \"Or the price was bigger than we understood. The memories of the summer were tangled up with everything else - their bond, their growth, who they became. You can't remove the roots without killing the tree.\"\n\n\"Can we tell them? Show them the journals, jog their memories?\"\n\n\"I don't know. We can try.\"\n\nThey try. Over the course of the summer, Ford shows Dipper Journal 3. Dipper finds it interesting the way he finds any book interesting - with intellectual curiosity but no emotional connection. He doesn't feel the electric thrill of discovery. He doesn't clutch it to his chest like it's the most important thing in the world. He reads it like homework.\n\nMabel doesn't make friends with Soos. She's polite to him, but she doesn't call him her handyman-bestie. She doesn't teach him ridiculous dance moves. She knits, but her sweaters are plain - solid colors, simple patterns. No shooting stars. No cat faces. No sparkle.\n\nThe twins don't explore the forest. They don't fight gnomes. They don't discover the bunker or the lake monster or any of the hundred magical things that made the first summer extraordinary. They stay in the Shack, play on their phones when there's signal, and count the days until they can go home.\n\nStan watches them drift through the summer like ghosts of the children they used to be, and it's the worst thing he's ever experienced - worse than thirty years of hiding Ford's portal, worse than the years of loneliness and con artistry and sleeping in his car. Because those were his own problems, his own pain. This is watching two kids who saved the world become two kids who don't care about anything, and knowing that the world they saved is the reason they're broken.\n\nOn the last day of summer, Mabel finds a photo stuck behind a drawer in the attic. It's a picture of her and Dipper, arms around each other, wearing matching sweaters, grinning like idiots in front of the Mystery Shack. They look deliriously happy. They look like best friends. They look like the most important person in each other's world.\n\n\"When was this taken?\" she asks Stan.\n\nStan looks at the photo. He looks at Mabel. And he lies, because the truth would be too complicated and too cruel. \"Must be from your parents' visit a few years back. You were little.\"\n\nMabel puts the photo back. She doesn't take it with her.\n\nThe bus home is quiet. The twins sit apart. Gravity Falls shrinks in the rear window, and neither of them looks back.\n\nThe Axolotl kept its promise. Weirdmageddon is undone. Bill is contained. Reality is healed. The world is saved. But the twins who saved it are gone - not dead, not trapped, just... diminished. Reduced to ordinary children who had an extraordinary summer stolen from their hearts, and who will never know what they lost or why they feel, sometimes, on quiet nights, like there's a hole in their chest shaped like something they can't name.\n\nStan keeps Journal 3 on a shelf in the gift shop. Sometimes tourists pick it up and flip through it. \"Cool prop,\" they say. \"Very realistic.\" Stan charges them five dollars to hold it and doesn't correct them.\n\nAnd in the space between dimensions, the Axolotl holds two small lights - the memories of a brave summer - and wonders if the price was too high. It saved the world. It broke the heroes. The math works out, cosmically speaking. The numbers balance.\n\nBut the Axolotl, for all its ancient wisdom, has never been good with the kind of math that measures what a family is worth.",
    ending: true
  },
  ending_axolotl_timeloop: {
    text: "\"I can do this on my own,\" Dipper says, pulling his hand from Mabel's. \"We both can. We're strong enough individually - we don't need to be a team for everything.\"\n\nMabel hesitates, then nods. \"Yeah. Yeah, you're right. I can handle my fears. You can handle yours. We'll both pass the test and then we'll have proven we're strong all by ourselves.\"\n\nThe Axolotl's expression shifts. The cosmic warmth in the test chamber dims, just slightly, and its voice carries a note of gentle warning: \"Together you are strong. Apart, you are what Bill was - alone and afraid.\"\n\nBut the twins have already separated, moving to their respective sides of the chamber, each facing their fears alone.\n\nDipper enters his nightmare solo. The classroom. The test scores. The feeling of being ordinary. Without Mabel's hand in his, without her voice cutting through the fog, the fears are louder. More convincing. The voice that says 'you're nothing without your journal' sounds reasonable. The crushing weight of mediocrity presses down on him and there's no one to tell him it's a lie, because the one person who could is on the other side of the chamber, fighting her own battle.\n\nHe fights. He fights hard. He tells himself everything Mabel would have said - that he's brave, that he's more than his intelligence, that fear doesn't define him. But the words ring hollow in his own voice. It's like telling yourself a joke - even if it's funny, the delivery is wrong. Some truths only sound true when someone else says them. When someone who loves you says them.\n\nMabel enters her nightmare solo. The gray office. The serious clothes. The world that ground down her sparkle. Without Dipper's steady presence, without his fierce insistence that her joy is a weapon, the fears dig deeper. The voice that says 'grow up, stop being silly, the world doesn't want your sunshine' gets louder and louder. She tries to knit, but her hands won't make the patterns right. She tries to smile, but the gray Mabel in the mirror doesn't smile back.\n\nShe fights too. She tells herself what Dipper would have said - that growing up doesn't mean becoming boring, that her optimism is courage, that she matters exactly as she is. But the words feel like quotes rather than truths. Borrowed armor rather than her own skin.\n\nAlone, they are each brilliant and brave and strong. But alone, they are incomplete. Dipper's logic without Mabel's heart becomes cold. Mabel's heart without Dipper's logic becomes untethered. They are twin stars trying to shine without each other's gravity, and slowly, inexorably, they drift into darkness.\n\nDipper falls. Not physically - he just... stops fighting. The fear wins. The classroom closes in. The mediocrity becomes real. He sits at a desk in an endless school and stops raising his hand and stops asking questions and stops being Dipper.\n\nMabel falls. The gray takes over. The sweaters disappear. The color drains. She sits in the office and stops knitting and stops smiling and stops being Mabel.\n\nThe Axolotl watches with eyes that overflow with cosmic sorrow. It tried to warn them. It always tries. But the test is the test, and the choice was theirs.\n\n\"The test is failed,\" it says, and each word carries the weight of a dying star. \"But mercy lives where hope has passed. I give you time to try once more - a loop, a chance, as I've done before.\"\n\nThe Axolotl doesn't punish. It has never punished. Instead, it does what it always does: it offers another chance. But this chance is imperfect, because the failure has introduced an error into the cosmic calculation. Time loops. Reality folds. The twins are sent back - not to the beginning of their search, but to the beginning of Weirdmageddon itself.\n\nThey wake in the Mystery Shack. The sky is tearing open. Bill is ascending. Weirdmageddon is starting. Again. From the top.\n\nThere's a feeling of dread in Dipper's stomach that he can't explain. A sense that he's been here before. He looks at Mabel and sees the same confusion in her eyes - a half-remembered feeling, like a dream fading at the edges.\n\n\"Mabel... have we done this before?\"\n\n\"I don't know. I feel like... I feel like I should know something. Something important. About a salamander?\"\n\nThey don't remember the details. The loop erases most of the specifics. But the FEELING persists - a deep, aching sense that they're missing something crucial, that there's a lesson they almost learned and then lost. Like having a word on the tip of your tongue forever.\n\nThey go through it all again. The search. The clues. The Axolotl. The test. And each time, at the critical moment, some version of the same choice presents itself: together or apart? And each time, some ghost of pride or independence or the desire to prove themselves pulls them to the same answer: apart.\n\nLoop after loop after loop. The details change - different paths to the Axolotl, different conversations, different adventures along the way. But the ending is always the same: the test, the separation, the failure, the loop. They're Sisyphus with a choose-your-own-adventure book, reliving the same story with different page turns but the same conclusion.\n\nSomewhere in the iterations, Dipper starts keeping a journal of his deja vu. He fills pages with half-remembered feelings and almost-grasped truths. 'We failed a test,' he writes. 'We need to stay together. WHY CAN'T I REMEMBER?'\n\nSomewhere in the iterations, Mabel knits a sweater with an axolotl on it and doesn't know why. She just knows it's important. She just knows that something vast and kind is watching her and waiting for her to figure it out.\n\nThe Axolotl waits. It will always wait. It has the patience of eternity and the hope of a gardener watching a seed that hasn't sprouted yet. Someday - maybe in the next loop, maybe in the hundredth, maybe in the thousandth - the twins will choose differently. They'll reach for each other instead of standing alone. They'll remember, in their bones if not their minds, that together they are everything and apart they are just echoes.\n\nBut until that day, the loop continues. And Dipper and Mabel Pines wake up in the Mystery Shack with the sky tearing open and a feeling in their hearts like a word they can't quite remember, a lesson they can't quite learn, a choice they keep making wrong in a story they can't quite escape.",
    ending: true
  },
  ending_axolotl_absorbed: {
    text: "\"I can fix it!\" McGucket shouts, diving back into the guts of the Dimensional Washing Machine. \"Just give me thirty seconds! Maybe forty! A minute tops!\"\n\nDipper and Mabel help however they can - Mabel holds a flashlight while Dipper reads aloud from the journal's technical specifications. McGucket works with manic speed, rewiring circuits, adjusting crystals, tightening bolts that rattle loose as fast as he can tighten them. The tuba section honks in protest. The washing machine drum spins erratically. The portal to the between-space writhes and pulses, its edges growing more unstable by the second.\n\n\"Almost... almost...\" McGucket's hands blur over the controls. \"If I can just reverse the polarity of the flangelator and recalibrate the dimensional resonance to - oh no.\"\n\n'Oh no' is never what you want to hear from a genius working on an interdimensional portal.\n\nThe portal tears wide open. Not a controlled opening - a rupture, like a seam splitting on a bag that's been overstuffed. The tear in reality expands from the size of a doorway to the size of the entire bunker in a heartbeat. Through it pours raw chaos energy - the fundamental, unformed substance of the between-space, the stuff that realities are made from before they decide what they want to be.\n\nIt's beautiful and terrifying. Iridescent, shifting, alive with potential. Where it touches things, they transform uncontrollably - a wrench becomes a bird becomes a mathematical equation becomes a smell becomes a memory becomes a color that doesn't exist. The laws of physics aren't just being broken; they're being dissolved. The chaos energy doesn't care about rules because it predates rules. It's the raw material from which rules are made.\n\n\"SHUT IT DOWN!\" Dipper screams.\n\n\"I CAN'T!\" McGucket screams back. \"THE MACHINE IS GONE!\"\n\nHe's right. The Dimensional Washing Machine has been consumed by the chaos energy, transformed into something that is no longer a machine and no longer not-a-machine but something in between - a quantum superposition of all possible devices, flickering between states too fast for the human eye to track. Their tool for reaching the Axolotl has become part of the very chaos they were trying to navigate.\n\nThe chaos energy floods outward from the bunker. It pours through the junkyard like a slow-motion tsunami, transforming everything it touches. Cars become trees become houses become abstract concepts become cars again. McGucket's carefully built fortifications dissolve into pure possibility. The junkyard, the surrounding forest, the ground beneath their feet - all of it becoming unstable, unfixed, uncertain.\n\nAnd far away, in the Fearamid, Bill Cipher feels it. His eye widens - not with fear this time, but with DELIGHT. The chaos energy is the same fundamental stuff that powers his reality-warping abilities. It's dimensional fuel, and the twins have just opened a fire hydrant of it pointed directly at him.\n\n\"Oh, KIDS!\" Bill's voice echoes across the transformed landscape, gleeful and terrible. \"YOU SHOULDN'T HAVE! A PORTAL TO THE RAW CHAOS OF THE BETWEEN-SPACE? FOR ME? IT'S NOT EVEN MY BIRTHDAY!\"\n\nThe Fearamid pulses. It begins absorbing the chaos energy like a sponge absorbing water, growing larger, more powerful, more REAL. Bill was already the master of Weirdmageddon, but he was working with limited power, burning through what he brought from the Nightmare Realm. Now he has an unlimited supply. The chaos energy flows into his domain and Bill Cipher becomes something worse than he was before - not just a dream demon but a chaos god, fueled by the raw stuff of un-reality itself.\n\nThe Fearamid expands. Its walls of nightmare brick grow outward, absorbing the landscape, absorbing the town, absorbing everything. Buildings fold into its structure. Trees become decorative columns. The lake becomes a reflecting pool on its surface. Gravity Falls is being consumed, piece by piece, absorbed into the Fearamid and transformed into more of Bill's playground.\n\n\"We have to run!\" Dipper grabs Mabel's hand. They run. McGucket runs beside them, his shower-curtain cape flapping behind him. But there's nowhere to run TO. The Fearamid is growing in every direction. The chaos energy is everywhere. The ground beneath their feet shifts and transforms as they run, and running becomes harder, then impossible, as the very concept of 'ground' becomes optional.\n\n\"I'm sorry,\" McGucket says, and for the first time since they've known him, the genius inventor looks truly defeated. \"I thought I could control it. I thought the machine would hold. I was wrong. I was so wrong.\"\n\nThe Fearamid's wall reaches them. It doesn't hit them - it absorbs them. The nightmare brick flows around them like honey, warm and terrible, and they feel themselves becoming part of its structure. Not turned to stone, not transformed into objects - woven into the Fearamid itself, their consciousness spread across the bricks like mortar, alive but dispersed, aware but unable to act.\n\nDipper can feel Mabel somewhere in the structure. He can feel McGucket. He can feel every person who's been absorbed, every mind woven into Bill's throne, conscious and trapped. The Fearamid hums with stolen thoughts and borrowed dreams. Its walls are made of people.\n\nBill laughs, and the laugh echoes through every brick, through every trapped consciousness, through every stolen soul. \"THANKS FOR THE UPGRADE, PINE TREE! THE NEW FEARAMID RUNS ON PURE CHAOS AND HUMAN DREAMS! IT'S LIKE A SMOOTHIE MADE OF SUFFERING AND UNLIMITED COSMIC POWER!\"\n\nIn the space between dimensions, the Axolotl feels the portal close. It feels the chaos energy redirected. It feels the twins - those brave, doomed children who came so close to reaching it - absorbed into the very thing they were trying to defeat. Its ancient eyes close in sorrow. Another chance lost. Another timeline gone wrong. The Axolotl is patient, but even infinite patience carries grief.\n\nThe twins exist within the Fearamid, conscious but formless, together but apart, part of the structure of their enemy's throne forever. They can hear Bill's parties. They can feel his triumphs. They are the walls of his castle, and in the castle, the party never ends.",
    ending: true
  },
  ending_axolotl_sacrifice: {
    text: "\"We can't just save ourselves,\" Mabel says, and Dipper knows she's right even though every survival instinct he has is screaming otherwise.\n\nThe portal is open. The Axolotl waits beyond it. But if they both jump through now, the portal will close behind them. The crystals will go dark. The Zodiac pattern will fade. No one else - not Ford, not Stan, not Soos, not Wendy, not anyone still free in Gravity Falls - will ever be able to follow. The twins will reach the Axolotl, maybe even save themselves, but everyone else will be left behind in Bill's nightmare.\n\n\"We hold the portal open,\" Dipper says, his jaw set. \"We fight Bill's forces and keep the portal active until someone else can get here. Ford, Stan, anyone - if they can reach this cave, they can go through.\"\n\nThe eye-bats hit like a screaming tidal wave. Dozens of them, pouring through the cave entrance, their shrieks bouncing off the stone walls in a deafening cacophony. Bill's power crackles around them, warping the cave's edges, trying to collapse the ceiling, trying to destroy the crystal formation that keeps the portal alive.\n\nMabel fights with everything she has. Grappling hook, glitter bombs, a slingshot loaded with ball bearings, and sheer Mabel fury. She takes down eye-bats left and right, her aim surprisingly deadly, her war cry a mixture of \"FOR GRAVITY FALLS!\" and \"EAT GLITTER, EYEBALLS!\" Dipper fights alongside her, using the journal as both shield and reference guide, shouting weaknesses and strategies as he spots them. Together, they hold the cave entrance against wave after wave of Bill's minions.\n\nBut Bill isn't sending just eye-bats anymore. Larger things come through the forest - warped creatures, demons from the Fearamid's inner circle, reality-bending nightmares that don't obey physics or common sense. The twins are pushed back, step by step, toward the portal. The crystal formation takes hits. Two crystals crack. The portal flickers.\n\n\"It's not going to hold!\" Dipper shouts. \"We need help and no one's coming!\"\n\nThe portal sputters. Three more crystals shatter. The gateway to the Axolotl's dimension is closing, collapsing, the shimmering doorway shrinking from the size of a wall to the size of a door to the size of a window. In moments, it'll be gone.\n\nMabel makes her decision in a heartbeat. In a single, fluid motion, she grabs Dipper by the shoulders and SHOVES him toward the portal. He stumbles backward, off-balance, arms pinwheeling.\n\n\"MABEL, WHAT ARE YOU -\"\n\n\"Find the Axolotl!\" she shouts, already turning back to face the incoming wave. \"Save everyone! I'll hold them off!\"\n\n\"NO! MABEL, I WON'T LEAVE YOU!\"\n\n\"You're not leaving me! You're saving me! You're saving EVERYONE!\" She looks over her shoulder, and her face is fierce and beautiful and absolutely certain. Mabel Pines has never been more sure of anything in her life. \"I believe in you, Dipper. Now GO!\"\n\nThe portal closes around him. The last thing he sees is Mabel turning to face Bill's army alone, grappling hook in hand, sweater blazing with a shooting star, small and brave and impossible and perfect.\n\nDipper falls into the between-space, screaming his sister's name.\n\nHe finds the Axolotl alone. The cosmic salamander sees him - sees the terror in his eyes, the tears on his face, the desperate, broken determination of a boy who just lost his other half. It sees Mabel too, in the cave, fighting an unwinnable battle with everything she has while the portal closes forever behind her.\n\n\"Please,\" Dipper says. He doesn't plead eloquently. He doesn't make a speech. He just says \"please\" over and over, his voice cracking, his whole body shaking. \"Please help. Please save her. Please save them all. Please. Please.\"\n\nThe Axolotl acts. Its power unfurls across dimensions, reaching into Gravity Falls, undoing Weirdmageddon with the careful precision of a surgeon removing stitches. The sky heals. The Fearamid dissolves. Bill is contained, wrapped in cosmic patience and sealed away. The town is restored. The people are freed. Gravity Falls is saved.\n\nBut Mabel. Mabel was standing at the threshold when the portal closed. She was half in the cave and half in the between-space, straddling two realities, and when the Axolotl's power washed through, it caught her in the transition. She didn't go back to Gravity Falls with everyone else. She went... somewhere else. Into the between-space. Into the Axolotl's domain.\n\nDipper returns to a saved world. The sky is blue. The sun is warm. The Mystery Shack stands intact. Stan and Ford are on the porch, confused but alive. Soos is there. Wendy is there. Everyone is there except the one person who matters most.\n\n\"Where's Mabel?\" Stan asks, and Dipper can't answer. He just holds up the grappling hook - the only thing that came back through the portal with him. Mabel's grappling hook. He found it in his hand when he woke up, warm from her grip.\n\nThe Axolotl speaks to him that night, in a dream. Its voice is gentle, sorrowful, and absolutely honest.\n\n\"Your sister lives. She is not lost. She dwells within the in-between, where I shall be her host. She is safe. She is whole. She is loved. But the bridge between your world and mine is not a bridge that mortal feet can walk more than once. She cannot come home, and you cannot come here. Not in the way you wish.\"\n\n\"Then how?\" Dipper demands, even in the dream.\n\n\"In dreams,\" the Axolotl says. \"She will visit you in dreams. And you will visit her. Every night, when sleep takes hold, the space between your minds will bridge the space between your worlds.\"\n\nIt's not enough. It will never be enough. But it's what they have.\n\nEvery night, Dipper dreams of the between-space. Mabel is there - not a memory, not an echo, but HER, real and alive and wearing a sweater that the Axolotl somehow helped her knit from the fabric of unrealized dimensions. She tells him about the between-space - how it's strange and beautiful and how the Axolotl teaches her things about the universe that make Ford's journals look like picture books. She says she's okay. She says she misses him. She says the Axolotl lets her watch Gravity Falls sometimes, through the gaps between realities, and she can see everyone living their lives.\n\nDipper tells her about the waking world. About Stan's new tourist attraction: 'I Survived Weirdmageddon' t-shirts selling like hotcakes. About Ford's research into getting her back (ongoing, obsessive, hopeful). About Soos becoming the Shack's manager. About Wendy saving him a seat on the bus every morning.\n\nThey talk. They laugh. They cry. And when Dipper wakes up, he can still feel the warmth of his sister's hug, fading like mist in sunlight but never quite disappearing.\n\nGravity Falls is saved. The hero lived. But the cost was everything - not a life, but a presence. A sister who exists in the space between the stars, watching over her brother through the thin walls of sleep.\n\nOn Dipper's bedside table, Mabel's grappling hook sits next to a journal where he records every dream. And every night, without fail, the twins find each other in the space between waking and sleeping, between dimensions and dreams, between the world that was saved and the one who saved it.\n\nTogether. Always together. Just not in the way they planned.",
    ending: true
  },
  ending_axolotl_petrified: {
    text: "The ritual is failing. That much is clear.\n\nBill's forces are too many and too close. The invisible barrier around the ritual circle is weakening - eye-bats are pressing against it, their combined weight creating visible dents in the magical shield. Bill himself is pouring energy into the cave, his terror of the Axolotl making him more powerful and more reckless than the twins have ever seen. The ground shakes. Cracks spiderweb across the cave floor, threatening to destroy the carefully drawn symbols. The chanting falters as dust and debris rain from the ceiling.\n\n\"We have to go!\" Dipper shouts over the cacophony. \"The circle won't hold! If we stay here, we're -\"\n\n\"But we're so CLOSE!\" Mabel shouts back. The portal behind the wall is half-open, pink light spilling through the cracks, the Axolotl's presence tantalizingly near.\n\nA stalactite the size of a car crashes down three feet from the ritual circle. The impact shatters two of the Zodiac crystals. The barrier flickers and dies. Eye-bats pour in.\n\n\"RUN!\" Dipper grabs Mabel's hand and they run.\n\nBut they've stopped the ritual too late. The Axolotl's power, half-summoned and incomplete, dissipates like smoke. The portal dims. The pink light fades. And Bill Cipher, free of the Axolotl's approaching presence, turns his full, undivided, FURIOUS attention on the two children who dared to summon his oldest enemy.\n\nThey make it fifty feet from the ritual circle before Bill's power catches them.\n\nIt doesn't hit them like a blast. It seeps up from the ground, cold and ancient and vengeful. Their feet stick first. Dipper looks down and sees stone creeping up his shoes, his ankles, his shins - gray, cold, hard, turning living flesh to solid rock with the inexorable patience of geological time sped up to seconds.\n\n\"MABEL!\" He reaches for her. She reaches for him. Their fingers touch, then stiffen, then freeze. The stone climbs their bodies like ivy growing in fast-forward - legs, waist, chest, arms. It's not painful but it's COLD, a coldness that goes deeper than skin, deeper than bone, all the way to the soul.\n\nBill appears in the cave entrance, and his fury is biblical. His single eye blazes white-hot. His form crackles with energy that warps the air around him. He's not performing now, not putting on a show for his demons. This is Bill Cipher genuinely enraged, genuinely frightened, and channeling every ounce of that fear into punishment.\n\n\"YOU ALMOST SUMMONED IT,\" Bill hisses, his voice low and dangerous and nothing like his usual theatrical bluster. \"YOU ACTUALLY ALMOST BROUGHT THE AXOLOTL INTO MY DOMAIN. DO YOU KNOW WHAT THAT WOULD HAVE DONE? DO YOU KNOW HOW CLOSE YOU CAME?\"\n\nThe stone reaches their necks. Dipper can still see, still hear, still think. Beside him, Mabel's face is frozen mid-shout, her expression one of fierce defiance even as the stone claims it. Her hand is reaching for his. His hand is reaching for hers. They're six inches apart. They will always be six inches apart.\n\n\"I SHOULD UNMAKE YOU,\" Bill continues, floating closer. \"I SHOULD ERASE YOU FROM EVERY TIMELINE, EVERY DIMENSION, EVERY POSSIBILITY. I SHOULD MAKE IT SO THAT DIPPER AND MABEL PINES NEVER EXISTED IN ANY REALITY.\"\n\nHe stops. His eye narrows.\n\n\"BUT THAT WOULD BE TOO KIND.\"\n\nThe stone finishes its work. Two statues stand in the cave - a boy reaching for his sister, a girl reaching for her brother, frozen in eternal almost-contact. The stone is gray and cold and detailed, every thread of Mabel's sweater preserved, every wrinkle in Dipper's vest captured, their expressions locked in a moment of courage and love and desperate hope.\n\nBill surveys his work. Then, with a gesture, he rearranges the cave. The ancient paintings shift, making room. The new statues are positioned among the prehistoric art, integrated into the cave's millennia-old gallery. Two children, reaching for each other, becoming part of the same wall of art that depicts the Axolotl and the history of human contact with the cosmic.\n\n\"THERE,\" Bill says, and now his voice is back to its theatrical lilt, the fear buried under layers of showmanship. \"A NEW ADDITION TO THE COLLECTION. THE PINE TREE AND THE SHOOTING STAR, PRESERVED FOR POSTERITY. LET THEM SERVE AS A WARNING TO ANYONE ELSE WHO GETS CLEVER IDEAS ABOUT COSMIC SALAMANDERS.\"\n\nHe leaves. The cave goes quiet. The portal is dead, the crystals are shattered, and two stone children stand among paintings that are thousands of years old, becoming just another part of the cave's long memory.\n\nThey're conscious. That's the cruelest part. Bill left them aware - able to think, to feel, to know exactly what happened and exactly how close they came. They can feel each other's presence, six inches and an eternity apart. They can feel the Axolotl's faded warmth, a ghost of the cosmic presence that almost saved them. They can hear the drip of water in the cave and the distant sounds of Weirdmageddon continuing outside, the world they failed to save going on without them.\n\nYears pass. Decades. The cave remains. Weirdmageddon eventually ends - not through heroics but through Bill's own boredom, as the dream demon moves on to torment other dimensions and Gravity Falls slowly, painfully heals itself. The cave becomes a curiosity, then a legend, then a secret known only to a few.\n\nHikers sometimes find it. They marvel at the ancient paintings. They pause at the two stone figures - \"So lifelike,\" they say. \"Look, they're reaching for each other. It's beautiful. Must be thousands of years old.\"\n\nIt's not thousands of years old. It's two kids who tried to save the world and almost succeeded. Almost.\n\nIn the stone, Dipper and Mabel dream. Not true dreams - stone doesn't dream - but something like dreams, echoes of consciousness trapped in mineral permanence. They dream of summer. Of sweaters and journals and pig sidekicks. Of a cosmic salamander made of starlight and patience. Of how close they came, and how six inches can be the longest distance in the universe.\n\nThe cave keeps their secret. The paintings watch over them. And in the space between dimensions, the Axolotl mourns two brave children who reached for the light and were turned to stone for the reaching.",
    ending: true
  },
  ending_axolotl_confrontation: {
    text: "The Axolotl's power flows through them like liquid starlight, and Dipper makes a choice that surprises even himself.\n\n\"Bill is RIGHT HERE,\" he says to Mabel. \"The Axolotl's energy is IN us. We might never get this chance again. We can fight him NOW.\"\n\nMabel looks at the small, terrified triangle floating at the cave entrance. Bill Cipher, dream demon, master of Weirdmageddon, conqueror of realities - and right now, in the presence of the Axolotl's power, he looks almost fragile. His single eye is wide. His usual confidence has evaporated. For the first time in trillions of years, Bill is facing something he can't mock, can't manipulate, and can't destroy.\n\n\"Let's do this,\" Mabel says, and the twins step forward together.\n\nThe battle is unlike anything Gravity Falls has ever seen, and given that this is a town where the regularly scheduled weirdness includes gnome uprisings and lake monster sightings, that's saying something.\n\nDipper and Mabel don't fight with fists or weapons. They fight with the Axolotl's energy - cosmic power channeled through human hearts, guided by human courage, shaped by the bond between two twelve-year-olds who have spent an entire summer learning to face impossible things. The energy flows through their hands in streams of pink light, meeting Bill's blue fire in cascading explosions that shake the cave to its foundations.\n\nBill fights back with everything he has, which is considerable. Blue fire. Reality warps. Temporal distortions. He throws nightmares at them - literal nightmares pulled from their subconscious, weaponized and given form. A giant version of Dipper's test anxiety. A monstrous embodiment of Mabel's fear of growing up. Twisted versions of their friends, warped into puppet-like horrors.\n\nBut the Axolotl's power burns through the nightmares like sunlight through cobwebs. Each one dissolves in pink fire, and the twins push forward, step by step, driving Bill back through the cave, through the forest, out into the open air of Weirdmageddon.\n\nThe battle draws attention. Bill's forces converge - eye-bats, henchmaniacs, warped creatures of every description. But the twins are channeling something beyond mortal power, and Bill's forces break against them like waves against a lighthouse. Eye-bats evaporate. Henchmaniacs flee. The weirdness waves that have plagued Gravity Falls since Weirdmageddon began reverse themselves wherever the twins' power touches.\n\nBill is losing. For the first time since he left the Nightmare Realm, Bill Cipher is being pushed back, diminished, his power shrinking in the face of something older and deeper and fundamentally opposed to everything he represents. Chaos meeting order. Destruction meeting creation. Fear meeting courage.\n\n\"YOU CAN'T BEAT ME!\" Bill screams, but his voice is smaller now, thinner, stripped of its cosmic reverb. \"I'M BILL CIPHER! I'VE EXISTED FOR TRILLIONS OF YEARS! I'VE DESTROYED DIMENSIONS! I'VE -\"\n\n\"You've been alone for trillions of years,\" Mabel says, and the simple truth of it hits Bill harder than any cosmic blast. \"And we're not alone. That's why we're winning.\"\n\nThe final push. Dipper and Mabel, hand in hand, channel everything they have - every ounce of the Axolotl's power, every bit of their own courage, every drop of love they carry for their family and friends and their weird, wonderful town. The energy builds between their clasped hands and erupts outward in a wave of pink-gold light that washes over Bill like a sunrise drowning a shadow.\n\nBill screams. He shrinks. He diminishes. From the size of a building to the size of a house to the size of a person to the size of a basketball. His power drains, siphoned away by the Axolotl's energy and dispersed harmlessly into the dimensional structure. He's still Bill Cipher, but he's Bill Cipher at one percent power - a weakened, diminished version of the dream demon, all his grand plans and cosmic ambitions reduced to impotent fury.\n\nDipper and Mabel drive him back into the cave. The ritual circle still glows faintly on the floor. Using the last of the Axolotl's energy, they seal Bill inside the cave - not just physically but dimensionally, wrapping him in layers of the same ancient magic that the ritual invoked. The seal closes over the cave entrance like a door slamming shut, and Bill's furious screams fade to muffled, distant echoes.\n\nWeirdmageddon collapses without its architect. The Fearamid crumbles. The sky heals - slowly, imperfectly, like a wound closing. The eye-bats disintegrate. Time resumes its normal flow. Gravity remembers its direction. The lake goes back to being water. Buildings settle back onto their foundations with groans and creaks.\n\nGravity Falls is saved.\n\nBut the twins know, even as they stumble out of the cave exhausted and drained and triumphant, that it's not permanent. The Axolotl's energy has left them. Their bodies feel like they've run a marathon, climbed a mountain, and then been asked to do it again. And the seal on the cave, while powerful, is not eternal. Ford examines it later and estimates it will hold for decades - maybe centuries, if they're lucky. But not forever.\n\n\"Bill is contained,\" Ford tells them, his voice carefully neutral. \"Not destroyed. Not erased. Contained. The seal is strong - stronger than anything I could have built with technology. But it will weaken over time. Eventually, inevitably, it will fail. And when it does...\"\n\n\"We'll be ready,\" Dipper says.\n\nThe town rebuilds. It takes weeks. The physical damage of Weirdmageddon is extensive - buildings warped, streets cracked, the forest in a state of botanical confusion as trees try to remember which direction they're supposed to grow. But Gravity Falls has always been resilient, and its people are tougher than any dream demon gives them credit for.\n\nThe Mystery Shack reopens. Stan adds a new attraction: 'The Cave of the Sealed Demon - Real Supernatural Imprisonment! Only $15 admission! Children half price!' Ford objects. Stan charges $20.\n\nBut beneath the humor and the rebuilding and the return to normalcy, there's a weight that the twins carry. They saved the world, but they didn't finish the job. Bill is locked in a cave in the forest, weakened but alive, plotting and scheming and waiting. The threat isn't gone - it's postponed. They've bought time, not victory.\n\nSome nights, Dipper walks to the edge of the forest and listens. If he's very quiet, if the wind is just right, he can hear something from deep underground - faint, muffled, barely there. Laughter. Bill's laughter. Patient now, where it used to be manic. Waiting, where it used to be impatient. Bill has all the time in the world, and he knows, as the twins know, as the Axolotl knows, that 'sealed' is not the same as 'stopped.'\n\nBut that's a problem for another day. Maybe another generation. For now, the sun is shining, the Mystery Shack is open for business, and Gravity Falls is as weird and wonderful and safe as it's ever been.\n\nFor now.",
    ending: true
  },

};
